1 OBJE @O1117@
1 NOTE Tribute &amp; Message From The Family
2 CONT 
2 CONT Ella Louise Seal Toups passed away peacefully on November 10, 2015 in The Woodlands, Texas after a brief illness. Her two sons Joe and Mike were at her side as the Lord welcomed her home. Lou or Ms. Lou as her friends would often call her was 85 ye
2 CONC ars old. Lou was preceded in death by her mother, Hazel Vernetta Johnson Seal, her father, George Albert Seal and her beloved husband Neil in 1986.
2 CONT Lou was born in Natchitoches, Louisiana on December 2, 1929 and spent much of her childhood growing up in Louisiana. After moving to Texas briefly, she later graduated from Texarkana High School but again returned to Louisiana to attend college a
2 CONC t the University of Southwestern Louisiana (USL) in Lafayette. Lou had a lifelong passion for art and devoted herself to studying commercial art while at USL. After college Lou applied her art background by working as a draftsman for the Sohio Oi
2 CONC l Company in Lafayette in the late 1940's. She was often proud to claim that she was the first female draftsman (draftswoman) in the oil business at that time. It was at Sohio after World War II that she met a handsome Air Force veteran named Nei
2 CONC l J. Toups and the two were married on January 5, 1952. After two sons were born in 1958 and 1959 the family rooted itself in South Louisiana for the next fifteen years and Lou quit work to devote herself full time to raising her family and helpin
2 CONC g others in Lafayette.
2 CONT Lou was a tireless, enthusiastic volunteer and thoroughly enjoyed bringing her love of creating with her hands to any volunteer endeavor. In fact if it involved any sort of mothering and/or art she would throw all her passion at the task. Be it Sun
2 CONC day school teacher, room mother, den mother or neighborhood mom, many beyond her own family came to benefit from her generosity and talents. After helping her husband publish a genealogical history book entitled "The Toups Clan and How It All Began
2 CONC " in 1969 and equally proud of her heritage, she began in earnest a research project of her own, eventually tracing her family's history back to Europe. Lou and her family moved to Spring, Texas in 1974 and she once again helped support her local c
2 CONC hurch, community college and a number of other projects in the Spring/Woodlands area.
2 CONT Lou is survived by her two sons, O'Neil J. Toups III and Michael G. Toups, both of the Woodlands, TX, daughter-in-law Katherine M. Toups, four grandchildren, Faith Renee Pitman, Michael J. Toups, Collin C. Toups and Corey J. Toups as well as thre
2 CONC e great grandchildren, Colton Bailes, Brooke Bailes and Caleb N. Toups.
2 CONT Services will be at 10:00 AM on Saturday November 14th, 2015 at Earthman Resthaven Funeral Home located at 13102 North Freeway in Houston, Texas. Interment will immediately follow at Resthaven Cemetary in Houston.
