1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;span style="color: #000000; font-family: arial,helvetica,sans-serif; font-size: small;"&gt;MELANSON, Pierre, came from England and married at Port-Royal Marguerite Mius, according to six depositions: one from the wid
2 CONC ow of his grandson Pierre Melanson (&lt;em&gt;Doc. in&amp;eacute;d.&lt;/em&gt;, Vol. III, p. 24), one from his great-grandson Louis-Athanase Trahan (&lt;em&gt;ibid.&lt;/em&gt;, p. 38), two from the widower of one and the husband of another of his g
2 CONC reat-granddaughters (&lt;em&gt;ibid.&lt;/em&gt;, Vol. II, p. 181; Vol. III, p. 118), one from the stepfather of the husband of another great-granddaughter (&lt;em&gt;ibid.&lt;/em&gt;, Vol. III, p. 113), and one from the husband of one of his great-
2 CONC great-granddaughters (&lt;em&gt;ibid.&lt;/em&gt;, p. 125). All of these depositions mistakenly call Pierre Melanson&amp;rsquo;s wife Anne-Marie, rather than Marguerite, but various records in the registers of Port-Royal, Grand-Pr&amp;eacute;, and B
2 CONC eaubassin, as well as several censuses, all provide the latter given name (see &lt;em&gt;DGFA-1&lt;/em&gt;, pp. 1148-1150). A seventh deposition, that of Pierre Melanson&amp;rsquo;s great-great-grandson Jean-Baptiste LeBlanc (&lt;em&gt;Doc. in&amp;
2 CONC eacute;d.&lt;/em&gt;, Vol. III, p. 55), gives Anne, rather than Anne-Marie, as her first name, and states that Pierre came from Scotland, instead of England. This Scottish origin is seconded by Joseph LeBlanc dit Le Maigre, widower of Pierre&amp;rs
2 CONC quo;s granddaughter Anne Bourg, who also changes Pierre&amp;rsquo;s wife&amp;rsquo;s name to Fran&amp;ccedil;oise de La Tour, adding that she was of noble extraction (&lt;em&gt;ibid.&lt;/em&gt;, Vol. II, p. 175). It might be thought that this mean
2 CONC s that Pierre Melanson had been married twice, but Joseph LeBlanc&amp;rsquo;s wife&amp;rsquo;s mother was some ten years younger than her brother Philippe, the ancestor of all the other Melanson descendants on Belle-&amp;Icirc;le, who was obviousl
2 CONC y named after their maternal grandfather Philippe Mius d&amp;rsquo;Entremont, and it can likewise be shown that Marguerite Mius was the mother of at least four of their younger siblings, so she must have been the mother of all of Pierre Melanson&am
2 CONC p;rsquo;s known children (see &lt;em&gt;DGFA-1, loc. cit.&lt;/em&gt;). As for the noble extraction of Pierre Melanson&amp;rsquo;s wife, that is attested by the fact that her father was a baron, and Joseph LeBlanc may have attributed to her the nam
2 CONC e de La Tour because her father had been closely associated with Charles de Saint-&amp;Eacute;tienne de La Tour (see &lt;em&gt;ibid.&lt;/em&gt;, pp. 1201-1202). &lt;/span&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
