2 CONC ;&lt;span style="font-family: 'arial','sans-serif'"&gt;Louis Carroll Walker Crosby Obituary&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="margin: 5pt 0.5in"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt; font-family: 'arial black','sans-serif'"&gt;Louis Carroll Walke
2 CONC r Crosby, son of Alen Crosby and Miss Walker, born in the state of Missouri, the city of St. Louis, in 1817 in October, died in 1887 on the 9 of August in Bayou Lafourche of heart trouble; half brother of John Crosby and brother of Alexis Crosby.&l
2 CONC t;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="margin: 5pt 0.5in"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt; font-family: 'arial black','sans-serif'"&gt;He [Louis Carroll Walker Crosby] went to St. Louis Prep and completed his necessary papers with General Jackson t
2 CONC o attend West Point. He deserted and went to Marseille, France. He stayed 2 years and returned to Florida and to New Orleans. He finished his last days in Bayou Lafourche; all his parents/relatives/family died of yellow fever in 1819 in Natchez, Mi
2 CONC ssissippi, except John and Alexis.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 11pt; font-family: 'arial','sans-serif'"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp
2 CONC ;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;font face="arial,helvetica,sans-serif" size="3"&gt;This obituary comes from a hand-written account by Louis Carroll Crosby, Jr., now in the possession of Rose-Marie Eltzroth Williams
2 CONC , given her by her grandmother Angele Crosby Eltzroth Pinckney who found it in her mother&amp;#39;s trunk. (Angele&amp;#39;s mother was Emelie Baudoin Crosby. The writer of the obituary, Louis Carroll Crosby, Jr., was Angele&amp;#39;s father.) Th
2 CONC e obituary was translated by Richard Crosby, grandson of Angele&amp;#39;s brother Louis Richard &amp;quot;Bill&amp;quot; Crosby, 29 March 1987, on the occasion of Clyde Alfred and Sara Platts Eltzroth&amp;#39;s 50th wedding anniversary.&lt;/font&gt
2 CONC ;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt"&gt;&lt;font face="arial,helvetica,sans-serif" size="3"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 11pt; font-family: 'arial','sans-serif'"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&a
2 CONC mp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;The obituary is at odds with Angele&amp;#39;s account of her grandfather before she found the document. Previously, she had said he was born in England and raised b
2 CONC y his godmother who wanted him to be a priest. Angele said he ran away to America to escape the church.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;          &lt;p style="margin-bottom: 0.0001pt; line-height: normal"&gt;&lt;font face="arial,helvetica,sans-s
2 CONC erif" size="3"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'arial','sans-serif'"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;One theory for the difference   betwee
2 CONC n the obituary and Angele&amp;#39;s account is that Angele may have been   confusing her grandfather Louis Carroll Walker Crosby with her   great-grandfather Alen/Alan/Allen Crosby. She was born in 1889, two years   after her grandfather&amp;#39;
2 CONC s death; her own father died when she was a toddler.   (She always said he died when she was &lt;em&gt;eighteen&lt;/em&gt;   months old, but it has recently been determined that he died 6 June 1891   which means that she was &lt;em&gt;twenty-six&lt
2 CONC ;/em&gt;   months old at the time.)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p style="margin-bottom: 0.0001pt; line-height: normal"&gt;&lt;font face="arial,helvetica,sans-serif" size="3"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'arial','sans-serif'"&gt;Ang
2 CONC ele was not the   only one confused about where Louis Carroll Walker Crosby was born: in the   1860 federal census, &amp;quot;Sant Louis&amp;quot; is given as his birthplace. But the   1850, 1870, and 1880 censuses show it as Louisiana. However, i
2 CONC n the 1880   census, which is the first time people were asked where their parents were   born, Armand Andr&amp;eacute; Crosby and Louisa Crosby Polkey, two of the children of   Louis Carroll Walker Crosby and his first wife Clarisse Theriot, sai
2 CONC d their   father was born in England. Their sibling Aurelien Crosby said in &amp;quot;Mis.&amp;quot;   (Perhaps the census taker could spell neither Missouri nor Mississippi and   didn&amp;#39;t think it mattered.) In 1880, Louis Carroll Walker Cro
2 CONC sby said both his   parents (Alen/Alan/Allen Crosby and Miss Walker) were born in Virginia. No   England anywhere.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt"&gt;&lt;font face="arial,helvetica,sans-serif" size="3"&g
2 CONC t;&lt;span style="font-size: 11pt; font-family: 'arial','sans-serif'"&gt;Interestingly, a Mrs. Crosby, pauper, died   in Natchez of bilious fever in 1819 according to the Mississippi State   Gazette, 24 July 1819. There was a yellow fever epidemi
2 CONC c in Natchez in 1817,   beginning in September and lasting through November. No record of deaths of   anyone with the surname Crosby has been found for those months, nor is there   any record of their burials in the Natchez cemetery records. Howeve
2 CONC r, further   research in Missouri, Mississippi, and Virginia may provide more information.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt"&gt;&lt;font face="arial,helvetica,sans-serif" size="3"&gt;&lt;span style="font-s
2 CONC ize: 11pt; font-family: 'arial','sans-serif'"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;             &lt;p style="margin-bottom: 0.0001pt; line-height: normal"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: 'arial','sans-serif'"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&
2 CONC lt;/p&gt;        &lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;       &amp;nbsp;          &amp;nbsp;  </line></content></metadataxml>
