1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>ierre was born in England after his parents, who were Hugenots, left La&lt;br&gt;Rochelle, France in 1628, and went to Yorkshire, England.&amp;nbsp; In the spring&lt;br&gt;of 1657, the family embarked from England on boa
2 CONC rd the vessel&lt;br&gt;Satisfaction, under the command of Capt. Peter Butler. They first stopped&lt;br&gt;at Boston, then it appears they stopped at Fort St. John.&amp;nbsp; After the&lt;br&gt;Treaty of Breda, under which in 1667 England ceded Acad
2 CONC ia to France, his&lt;br&gt;parents and siblings sought refuge under the Protstant government at&lt;br&gt;Boston. Pierre and his brother Charles left in Acadia with their families&lt;br&gt;used their mother&amp;#39;s maiden name, perhaps to avoid an
2 CONC y problems with the&lt;br&gt;Franch Catholics. Pierre married in about 1664 to Marie Marguerite Mius&lt;br&gt;d&amp;#39;Entremont, after having abjured his Protestant faith.&amp;nbsp; His wife was from&lt;br&gt;a Hugenot background also.&amp;nbsp
2 CONC ; The couple had ten known children:&lt;br&gt;1. Phillipe&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; b. 1666&amp;nbsp; m. @@ 1695 Marie Dugas&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; d. July 1744&lt;br&gt;2. Cecile,&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; b. 1668&amp;nbsp; m.&amp;nbsp;&amp;n
2 CONC bsp; 1687 Jean Landry&lt;br&gt;3. Pierre&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; b. 1670&amp;nbsp; m. @@ 1693 Marie Blanchard&lt;br&gt;4. Marie&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; b. 1673&amp;nbsp; m.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; 1694 Germai
2 CONC n Landry&lt;br&gt;5. Marguerite b. 1676&amp;nbsp; m.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; 1696 Alexandre Bourg d. 1745&lt;br&gt;6. Isabelle&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; b. 1679&amp;nbsp; m.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; 1709 1st Pierre-Allain Bugeauld, 2nd Rene&lt;br&gt;LeBlanc&lt;br
2 CONC &gt;7. Jean&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; b. 1681&amp;nbsp; m. Jan 11, 1701 Marguerite Dugas&lt;br&gt;8. Madeleine&amp;nbsp; b. 1684&amp;nbsp; m. Nov 29, 1702 Louis-Simon de St. Aubin&lt;br&gt;9. Anne&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbs
2 CONC p;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; b. 1686&amp;nbsp; m. Oct 25, 1705 Thomas Jacob&lt;br&gt;10. Paul&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; b. 1691&amp;nbsp; m. Nov&amp;nbsp; 8, 1712 Marie Terriot&lt;br&gt;All of the above childre
2 CONC n were born at Grand Pre, the Acadian settlement&lt;br&gt;founded by their father. Pierre Melancon&amp;#39;s wife was Marie Marguerite Mius&lt;br&gt;d&amp;#39;Entremnot, daughter of Philippe Mius d&amp;#39;Entremont and of Madeleine Helie.&lt;br&gt
2 CONC ;&amp;nbsp;She was born in 1649 at Cherbourg, France.&amp;nbsp; Pierre and his brother&lt;br&gt;Charles arrived in Acadia with the British. The Grand Pre (known as La&lt;br&gt;Grand Prairie) region in the Bassin des Mines was developed originally b
2 CONC y&lt;br&gt;Pierre Melancon and Pierre Theriot in the late 1680 and spread as far as&lt;br&gt;Pisiquid (Windsor).&amp;nbsp; In June of 1755 he was arrested by the English for&lt;br&gt;transporting goods in his boat, it was a new law he was not awar
2 CONC e of.</line></content></metadataxml>
