1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;By: Leo G. Cyr; Page: 85&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Pierre Leverdure married Priscilla about 1630 in England. Pierre was a French Protestant who fled to England about 1628 from the persecution of Calvinism in La Rochelle
2 CONC .&amp;nbsp; Reverend Clarence d&amp;#39;Entremont surmises that Priscilla was from the north eastern part of England between Lincolnshire and Durham, probably from Bradford. Her name was probably Melanson, which is still found in Yorkshire under va
2 CONC rious spellings.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In 1657 Pierre and Priscilla embarked on the Satisfaction with their sons Pierre, Charles and Jean for Fort St. Jean in Acadia.&amp;nbsp; Then years later, the Treaty of Breda restored Acadia to France. Pierre an
2 CONC d Priscilla found it expedient to move to Boston out of range of Acadian unfriendliness, leaving behind their sons Pierre and Charles, who had married Acadian girls and&amp;nbsp; becam Catholics.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Probably after his arrival in Bost
2 CONC on Pierre LeVerdure lost all his worldly possessions, so that he and his wife became completely dependent on their son John (Jean) who had accompanied them to Boston. A series of misfortunes, including the disappearance of John (Jean) affected Pier
2 CONC re&amp;#39;s heart and he died sometime between August 1676 and May 1677. Priscilla married a Captain William Wright Senior a Dorchester innkeeper, on April 8, 1680. She died in 1691 or early 1692.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;During the English occupation o
2 CONC f Acadia, the family had become known as often by the name Melanson as by LeVerdure. Were they being as English as possible during the occupationh by stressing Priscillas patronymic? Does this help to explain Acadian animosity toward them? Be tha
2 CONC t as it may Pierre and charles signed themselves Melanson, and eventually the name Laverdure was superseded in Acadia, although there is evidence that brother John (Jean) used Laverdure in Boston. the descendants of Pierre and Charles became know
2 CONC n by the Acadian form of their name Melanson or Melancon.&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
