2 CONT http://www.geni.com/people/Pierre-Laverdure/6000000002803304167 
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;div style="margin: 10px; padding: 0px; color: #333333; font-family: arial, 'helvetica neue', helvetica, sans-serif; font-size: 12px; line-height: 16.799999237060547px;"&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="margin-: 0px; ma
2 CONC rgin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 20px !important; padding: 0px; border---radius: 2px; border--right-radius: 2px; border-bottom-right-radius: 2px; border-bottom--radius: 2px;"&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="margin: 0px; padding: 0px; border-bottom-st
2 CONC yle: dashed; border-bottom-width: 1px; border-bottom-color: #eeeeee;"&gt;About Pierre Laverdure dit Melan&amp;ccedil;on / Melanson, I&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="margin: 0px; padding: 5px 0px 0px; word-wrap: break-word; word-break: break
2 CONC -word;"&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;The following is from&amp;nbsp;http://gregors-gathering.ca/Acadia/Melanson/melansons-intro-gen1.htm#Pierre1&amp;nbsp;which is a great website by Greg M
2 CONC elanson. Click the link to read much more.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;The Melanson family surname is quite unique in the fact that it is one of the only surnames that's use can b
2 CONC e traced to originating with one particular generation.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;Melanson family crest: designed by Margaret C. Melanson&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin
2 CONC : 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;The progenitors of all Melansons, Pierre Laverdure and his wife Priscilla, were not known by the Melanson surname but rather by the name, or title, of Laverdure. However, two of their sons, Pier
2 CONC re and Charles, appear to be the first to have begun using "Mellanson" - in well documented, written form.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;Pierre Laverdure&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p s
2 CONC tyle="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;The reason behind the use of Laverdure as an apparent surname for Pierre is not known for certain. It is likely that the name was not actually his surname but rather a title or nickn
2 CONC ame that may have referred to an area of France that he came from, families ties in France or property that he may have owned in France. Since two of his sons went by the Mellanson surname, societal logic would dictate that this was their father'
2 CONC s surname. However, no record has been found to put the Mellanson surname with Pierre senior.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;----------------------------&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p st
2 CONC yle="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;The family of Pierre LAVERDURE and Priscilla MELAN&amp;Ccedil;ON&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;[85578] LAVERDURE, Pier
2 CONC re (..), died au cours de l'hiver 1676-1677&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;ul style="margin: 0px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; list-style: none;"&gt;</line><line>&lt;li style="margin: 0px 0px 0px 30px; padding: 2px 0px; list-style-type: disc; color: #444444;"
2 CONC &gt;married before 1632, from .. (England)&lt;/li&gt;</line><line>&lt;/ul&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;MELAN&amp;Ccedil;ON, Priscilla (.. &amp;amp; .. [109600]), born about 1602&lt;/p&gt;<
2 CONC /line><line>&lt;pre style="font-family: monospace, serif; font-size: 1em; white-space: normal !important; margin: 0px; padding: 0px;"&gt;1) Pierre, stone mason (tailleur de pierres), born about 1632 (rec. 1686) or 1633 (rec. 1693), married about 16
2 CONC 65 Marguerite MIUS d'ENTREMONT&lt;/pre&gt;</line><line>&lt;pre style="font-family: monospace, serif; font-size: 1em; white-space: normal !important; margin: 0px; padding: 0px;"&gt;2) Charles, ploughman (laboureur), born about 1643 (rec. 1671), 164
2 CONC 2 (rec. 1686) or 1646 (rec. 1699), died between 1699 and 1701, married about 1663 Marie DUGAS&lt;/pre&gt;</line><line>&lt;pre style="font-family: monospace, serif; font-size: 1em; white-space: normal !important; margin: 0px; padding: 0px;"&gt;3) Jo
2 CONC hn, married about 1680 Sarah ..&lt;/pre&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;Bibliographie : Dictionnaire g&amp;eacute;n&amp;eacute;alogique des familles acadiennes (White);http://www.umoncton.ca/
2 CONC etudeacadiennes/centre/cea.html; Dictionnaire des Acadiens d'Archange Godbout; New England Marriages Prior to 1700&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;http://www.francogene.com/quebec--gene
2 CONC alogy/085/085578.php&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;--------------------&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;French Huguenot
2 CONC . Came to Acadia with Sir Thomas Temple onboard Satisfaction. -------------------- dit LAVERDURE. He was a French Protestant who exiled himself to England. -------------------- I was born in France (date unknown) and died in Boston, New England i
2 CONC n either 1676 or 1677. I married Priscilla (maiden name unknown) in England in 1631 and we had 3 children; Pierre Melanson (dit Laverdure born - around 1632, Charles Melanson (dit La Ramee) born around 1643, and Jean Melanson (dit Laverdure) date o
2 CONC f birth unknown. --------------------&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;pre style="font-family: monospace, serif; font-size: 1em; white-space: normal !important; margin: 0px; padding: 0px;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/pre&gt;</line><line>&lt;pre style="font-family
2 CONC : monospace, serif; font-size: 1em; white-space: normal !important; margin: 0px; padding: 0px;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/pre&gt;</line><line>&lt;pre style="font-family: monospace, serif; font-size: 1em; white-space: normal !important; margin: 0px; padding
2 CONC : 0px;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/pre&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;The Melanson Family&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;--------------
2 CONC ------------------------------------------------------------------&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;Introduction to the Melansons&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px
2 CONC ; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;The Melanson family surname is quite unique in the fact that it is one of the only surnames that's use can be traced to originating with one particular generation.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;pre style="font-fam
2 CONC ily: monospace, serif; font-size: 1em; white-space: normal !important; margin: 0px; padding: 0px;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/pre&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;The progenitors of all Melansons, Pier
2 CONC re Laverdure and his wife Priscilla, were not known by the Melanson surname but rather by the name, or title, of Laverdure. However, two of their sons, Pierre and Charles, appear to be the first to have begun using "Mellanson" - in well documented
2 CONC , written form.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;Pierre Laverdure&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;The reason behind the us
2 CONC e of Laverdure as an apparent surname for Pierre is not known for certain. It is likely that the name was not actually his surname but rather a title or nickname that may have referred to an area of France that he came from, families ties in Franc
2 CONC e or property that he may have owned in France. Since two of his sons went by the Mellanson surname, societal logic would dictate that this was their father's surname. However, no record has been found to put the Mellanson surname with Pierre senio
2 CONC r.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;Of the Laverdure name, Pierre was not alone in using it in Acadia. There were several men of French origin who also used the Laverdure/La Verdure name
2 CONC , although in these cases the name was clearly a title. A good example is that of Germain Doucet, once the Commander-in-Chief at Port Royal, who was known as "Germain Doucet dit La Verdure". Coincidentally, Germain was the father of Pierre Mellanso
2 CONC n's wife's mother, Marguerite Doucet.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;In the case of the Mellanson brothers, Pierre Mellanson would become known as "La Verdure" while Charles Mellanso
2 CONC n would become known as "La Ram&amp;eacute;e".&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;The French recorded such titles and nicknames by using the French word "dit" before the title, i.e.: "di
2 CONC t La Verdure", or in English, "called La Verdure". When referring to a female, the French would use the word "dite". Both "dit" and "dite" are pronounced "dee".&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17
2 CONC px;"&gt;During their time in Boston, Pierre Laverdure, his wife Priscilla and their son John were known by the name "Laverdure". This name was also to be consistently used as a surname for Pierre and Priscilla's granddaughter, Marie Melanson (firs
2 CONC t child and eldest daughter of Charles dit La Ram&amp;eacute;e Mellanson and Marie Dugas) who went to Boston to live with her grandmother at a young age. Marie became known as Mary Laverdure until later marrying David Basset.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>
2 CONC &lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;http://www.gregors-gathering.ca/Acadia/Melanson/melansons-intro-gen1.htm#Pierre1&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt
2 CONC ;Family Arrival in Acadia&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;Of the family's arrival in Acadia, it is widely accepted that Pierre and Priscilla landed in 1657 after sailing from England wi
2 CONC th their sons onboard the ship Satisfaction. It is also generally accepted that the family disembarked at St. John's fort at the mouth of the St. John River. The family had sailed to Acadia with the newly appointed English Governor of Acadia, Sir T
2 CONC homas Temple and a group of other settlers. Pierre and Priscilla, however, were to reside in Acadia for only 10 years.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;A Boston court document from 167
2 CONC 7 (Priscilla's petition of May 3, 1677) recorded Priscilla's late husband, "Peter Leverdure", as being a Frenchman and a Protestant and "Priscilla Leverdure" as being an Englishwoman. This document helped early researchers, such as Father Clarenc
2 CONC e d'&amp;Eacute;ntremont, establish a connection between Pierre Laverdure, his wife Priscilla and sons Pierre and Charles. The document also served to clear up the persistent claim that the origins of the Melanson family were Scottish. On the sam
2 CONC e note, about refuting the claims of Scottish birth (this time for Pierre and Charles Mellanson), a second document, written by John Adams in 1720, decisively describes Pierre Laverdure's son Pierre as being "an aged English Gentleman...".&lt;/p&gt
2 CONC ;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;The petition goes on to state that Priscilla's husband had left "[St.] John's fort to escape the wrath of his countrymen Papists". This latter statement clearl
2 CONC y suggests that Pierre was a French Huguenot who might have left France as the Catholic government's tolerance for the Protestant Huguenots began to rapidly deteriorate during the 1620's. Either due to the problems unwinding in France or for some o
2 CONC ther reason, Pierre ended up in England were he and his Priscilla were married about 1630 (SW).&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;Ten years before Priscilla's petition the 1667 Treaty o
2 CONC f Breda between the English and the French had ceded Acadia back to France. Pierre and Priscilla, both Protestants, were probably unable to fathom the idea of living under a French Catholic government and thus departed for Protestant ruled Boston
2 CONC , Massachusetts, sometime between 1667 and 1770 (Sir Thomas Temple had managed to delay the actual handing over of Acadia to French until 1670).&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;http://w
2 CONC ww.gregors-gathering.ca/Acadia/Melanson/melansons-intro-gen1.htm#Pierre1&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;Pierre and Charles Mellanson&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0p
2 CONC x 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;Two of Pierre and Priscilla's sons, Pierre dit Laverdure Mellanson and Charles dit La Ram&amp;eacute;e Mellanson, having already converted to Catholicism and married Acadian girls, remained in Acadia t
2 CONC o raise their families. Pierre and Priscilla's third known son, John Laverdure, either accompanied his parents when they departed for Boston or joined them there at a later date.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0p
2 CONC x; line-height: 17px;"&gt;Eldest son Pierre dit Laverdure married Marie-Marguerite Muis d'&amp;Eacute;ntremont, daughter of Philippe Muis d'&amp;Eacute;ntremont &amp;amp; Madeleine H&amp;eacute;lie while his younger brother Charles dit La Ram&amp;e
2 CONC acute;e married Marie Dugas, daughter of Abraham Dugas &amp;amp; Marguerite-Louise Doucet.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;Pierre and his wife Marie-Marguerite would eventually go on t
2 CONC o found Grand Pr&amp;eacute; at Les Mines (Minas), along with Pierre Terriot, where they would raise their family. Grand Pr&amp;eacute; became a favorite site for many young Acadians to relocate to as it was abundant in prime marshland and it wa
2 CONC s a good distance away from the central English authorities at Annapolis Royal. Very soon, the population would be thrice that as the population at Port Royal/Annapolis Royal.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px
2 CONC ; line-height: 17px;"&gt;Pierre would later be designated the Captain of the Militia, while Acadia was under French control, and was also to be named a seigneurial agent (collecting rents) which placed him in a position of authority in the Minas Ba
2 CONC sin region. Pierre it seems, was also to become a spy for the French.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;Charles dit La Ram&amp;eacute;e and his wife Marie would establish their family nea
2 CONC r the old Port Royal habitation in the Port Royal basin at what is today known as the Melanson Settlement (sometimes referred to as the "Melanson Village" in old records and maps). The settlement grew quite large over the years with a total of nin
2 CONC e households being located on the land during its peak times.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;Charles and his wife Marie seem to have done reasonably well as the census' show their clea
2 CONC red land expanding and their livestock increasing. They also had a large section of dyked marshland along the Rivi&amp;egrave;re Daupin (the Annapolis River) adjacent to their property. It was from this dyke that archaeologists recently retrieved t
2 CONC wo intact aboiteax, one of which is the largest and oldest aboiteau found to date.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;Other archaeological digs at the Melanson Settlement discovered the fo
2 CONC undations of many of the homes and buildings that once stood on the site, including the structure that housed Charles Melanson (son of Charles dit La Ram&amp;eacute;e) and his wife Anne Bourg. The digs within these stone foundations have produced n
2 CONC umerous artifacts that include an earthen cooking pot, a sword hand guard, beads, musket shot, and a variety of utensils, all of which are on display at the Memorial Chapel at Grand Pr&amp;eacute;.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0p
2 CONC x 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;Besides farming, in his elder years on the opposing side of his brother Pierre, Charles became a spy for the English.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height
2 CONC : 17px;"&gt;Pierre and Charles' descendants would remain on the lands of the Melansons Settlement and Grand Pr&amp;eacute; until the fateful year of 1755 that saw the Melansons and their families dispersed, along with an estimated 9,000 of their fe
2 CONC llow Acadians, to various parts of England, France and the British colonies of New England during the Expulsions.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;Of the Melanson Acadians that were depo
2 CONC rted to or remained in English territories after the Expulsions, the original spelling of the "Melanson" surname was, for the most part, retained. For those that were deported to or otherwise ended up in French territories, the name was widely fren
2 CONC chinized with the "s" in "son" being replaced with a "c" or cedilla according to the french pronunciation of the soft "s" on the word "son".&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;------------
2 CONC -------- Arrive en Acadie en 1657 avec Thomas Temple sur le bateau "Satisfaction". Stephen White, p. 1146, donne un peu plus d'historique sur l'arriv&amp;eacute;e des Melanson en Acadie. Margaret Melanson, dans son livre, mentionne que Pierre et so
2 CONC n fr&amp;egrave;re Charles sont les deux premiers Melanson en Acadie. Elle mentionne que leur m&amp;egrave;re est d'origine de l'Angleterre mais que leur p&amp;egrave;re est d'origine fran&amp;ccedil;aise et qu'il s'appelait Pierre Laverdure. &amp;
2 CONC Eacute;galement, elle avance l'hypoth&amp;egrave;se que le p&amp;egrave;re de ce Pierre Laverdure pourrait &amp;ecirc;tre un &amp;eacute;cossais du nom de McMellan. Mc signifie "fils" en &amp;eacute;cosse et avec l'influence anglaise, il aurait lai
2 CONC ss&amp;eacute; tomb&amp;eacute; le Mc pour le "son" &amp;agrave; la fin (format anglais).&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;Added by Roxanne Landry: Found this on the net:&lt;/p&gt;</line
2 CONC ><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;Notes for Pierre Melanson: - The ancestor in Acadia, Pierre Laverdure, was a French Huguenot who went to England where he married an Englishwoman named Prescila Melanso
2 CONC n. Pierre Laverdure came to Acadia with Sir Thomas Temple during the English occupation. Two of his sons adopted their mother's surname, Melanson, and stayed in Acadia while a third, John, kept the surname Laverdure and moved to Boston. After Acadi
2 CONC a was returned to France following the treaty of Br&amp;eacute;da, in 1667, the family settled in Boston with the exception of his two sons, Pierre and Charles Melanson who had married Acadians and converted to Roman Catholicism. Pierre Melanson ma
2 CONC rried Marie Mius d'Entremont and, along with Pierre Th&amp;eacute;riault, helped to found the settlement of Grand- Pr&amp;eacute;. The 1671 census describes him as a tailor but he refused to give his age and the number of his cattle. Charles Melans
2 CONC on married Marie Dugas and settled at Port-Royal. The site of his establishment is well known and was studied by archeologists, during the 1980s. Charles Melanson was fairly well educated. In 1695, he wrote to the governor of Massachussets giving h
2 CONC im information on French activities in Acadia. He also went regularly to Boston, possibly to visit his daughter, Marie, who lived there where she had married a French Huguenot, David Basset. Three of Charles' letters to governor Stoughton are prese
2 CONC rved in the Massachusetts archives. Despite the family connections and collaboration with the Boston colonists, the British authorities deported the families of his grandsons and their descendants. They were shipped to several New England colonie
2 CONC s as well as to prisons in the south of England. After the 1764 peace treaty, several of the family members found a home in France (Belle-&amp;Icirc;le-en-mer) and in Louisiana. A certain number of Melanson families managed to escape their persecut
2 CONC ors and found refuge in Quebec, particularly in the Trois-Rivi&amp;egrave;res region. Several returned to Acadia and settled in northern New Brunswick and in the Baie-Sainte-Marie area of Nova Scotia. Several descendants of the family left their ma
2 CONC rk in Acadian history, notably the Most Reverend Arthur Melanson who became the first archbishop of Moncton and who founded the teaching order of the Filles de Marie-de-l'Assomption. [Fidele Theriault of Fredericton, New Brunswick] ----------------
2 CONC ---- Notes for Pierre Melanson (found this on the net):&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;- The ancestor in Acadia, Pierre Laverdure, was a French Huguenot who went to England where he ma
2 CONC rried an Englishwoman named Priscilla Melanson. Pierre Laverdure came to Acadia with Sir Thomas Temple during the English occupation. Two of his sons adopted their mother's surname, Melanson, and stayed in Acadia while a third, John, kept the surna
2 CONC me Laverdure and moved to Boston. After Acadia was returned to France following the treaty of Br&amp;eacute;da, in 1667, the family settled in Boston with the exception of his two sons, Pierre and Charles Melanson who had married Acadians and conve
2 CONC rted to Roman Catholicism. Pierre Melanson married Marie Mius d'Entremont and, along with Pierre Th&amp;eacute;riault, helped to found the settlement of Grand-Pr&amp;eacute;. The 1671 census describes him as a tailor but he refused to give his ag
2 CONC e and the number of his cattle. Charles Melanson married Marie Dugas and settled at Port-Royal. The site of his establishment is well known and was studied by archeologists, during the 1980s. Charles Melanson was fairly well educated. In 1695, he w
2 CONC rote to the governor of Massachussets giving him information on French activities in Acadia. He also went regularly to Boston, possibly to visit his daughter, Marie, who lived there where she had married a French Huguenot, David Basset. Three of Ch
2 CONC arles' letters to governor Stoughton are preserved in the Massachusetts archives. Despite the family connections and collaboration with the Boston colonists, the British authorities deported the families of his grandsons and their descendants. The
2 CONC y were shipped to several New England colonies as well as to prisons in the south of England. After the 1764 peace treaty, several of the family members found a home in France (Belle-&amp;Icirc;le-en-mer) and in Louisiana. A certain number of Melan
2 CONC son families managed to escape their persecutors and found refuge in Quebec, particularly in the Trois-Rivi&amp;egrave;res region. Several returned to Acadia and settled in northern New Brunswick and in the Baie-Sainte-Marie area of Nova Scotia. Se
2 CONC veral descendants of the family left their mark in Acadian history, notably the Most Reverend Arthur Melanson who became the first archbishop of Moncton and who founded the teaching order of the Filles de Marie-de-l'Assomption. [Fidele Theriault o
2 CONC f Fredericton, New Brunswick]&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="margin: 10px; padding: 0px; color: #333333; font-family: arial, 'helvetica neue', helvetica, sans-serif; f
2 CONC ont-size: 12px; line-height: 16.799999237060547px;"&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="margin-: 0px; margin-right: 0px; margin-bottom: 20px !important; padding: 0px; border---radius: 2px; border--right-radius: 2px; border-bottom-right-radius: 2px; bord
2 CONC er-bottom--radius: 2px;"&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="margin: 0px; padding: 0px; border-bottom-style: dashed; border-bottom-width: 1px; border-bottom-color: #eeeeee;"&gt;view all Pierre Laverdure's Timeline&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="
2 CONC margin: 0px; padding: 5px 0px 0px;"&gt;1608</line><line>&lt;div style="margin: 0px; padding: 0px; font-weight: bold;"&gt;March 1608&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="margin: 0px; padding: 0px; font-weight: bold;"&gt;Birth of Pierre&lt;/div&gt;
2 CONC </line><line>&lt;div style="margin: 0px; padding: 0px; font-weight: bold; font-size: 11px;"&gt;La Rochelle, Clarent-Maritim, France&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&amp;nbsp;1630</line><line>&lt;div style="margin: 0px; padding: 0px; font-weight: bold;"&gt;
2 CONC 1630&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="margin: 0px; padding: 0px; color: #707070; font-size: 11px;"&gt;Age 21&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="margin: 0px; padding: 0px; font-weight: bold;"&gt;Marriage of Pierre Laverdure dit Melan&amp;c
