1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14pt"&gt;The &lt;span&gt;Dubbs&lt;/span&gt; - &lt;span&gt;Toops&lt;/span&gt; Families Descendents of Hans Jacob &lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;
2 CONC /em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14pt"&gt;, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;p&gt;Compiled by Roger Bruce &lt;span&gt;Dubbs&lt;/span&gt; of Universal City, Texas dated May 1995&lt;/p&gt;&lt;div style="border-bottom: windowtext 1.5pt double; border-
2 CONC : medium none; padding-bottom: 1pt; padding-: 0in; padding-right: 0in; border-: medium none; border-right: medium none; padding-: 0in"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 8pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;John Dups 22 Sept 1746 (East Ha
2 CONC nover Township, PA) - 1 Apr 1818&amp;nbsp; (Ross Cty, OH) Married (1773) Christiana ? 1746 - 27 Jul 1819&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Children - Daniel 1774, Henry 1777, John ?, Elizabeth ?, Catherine ?, Jacob ?, Barbara ?, Samuel 1786, Leonard 1790, Adam 179
2 CONC 3 &amp;amp; Christiana 1795&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Since John &lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;, on the death of his father, chose as guardian, Henry Waggoner, a blacksmith, it is believed he became a blacksmith as well a
2 CONC s a farmer. The only tradition has come down about his generation is that the sons were bound out to learn a trade. This was the custom of the time and not confined to orphans.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 1
2 CONC 0pt"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; 1776 John &lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt; was a drummer in the Company of Captain Thomas &lt;span&gt;Koppenheffer&lt;/span&gt; of the Lancaster County Militia and his name appea
2 CONC rs on various muster rolls of the Revolutionary period. A note is appended to one stating that John &lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt; and several others had deserted - the whole group of them &amp;quot;Living in &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size
2 CONC : 10pt"&gt;Hanover&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Township&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; near Adam Harper&amp;#39;s.&amp;quot; This must have a temporary AWOL fo
2 CONC r he is listed again in 1778 and 1781. &lt;span&gt;(Pa. archives, 5th Series, Vol. 7, pp 153, 545, 1054 and 1055.)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Besides his birth record and his militia service, th
2 CONC e only other record of John &lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt; in &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Pennsylvania&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; is that of a son, Henry, born to John and Christine &lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/spa
2 CONC n&gt; in 1777 and recorded in the &lt;span&gt;Swatara&lt;/span&gt; Church Book. John &lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt; is believed to have left &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Lancaster&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt
2 CONC ; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;County&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; in 1781 since his son John, born 1871, was born in &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Virginia&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font
2 CONC -size: 10pt"&gt;. A &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Virginia&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; census of 1784 shows him with a family of five living in &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Hampshire Count
2 CONC y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Virginia&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; (now &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;West Virginia&lt;/span&gt;&lt;spa
2 CONC n style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;). By 1800 the family was in Ross County, Ohio where the eldest son married in 1799.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Ross County, Ohio was a raw frontier in 1800 and the records kept w
2 CONC ere few and scanty. The first tax record found lists John &lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt; paying tax on 100 acres of land in &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Twin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;spa
2 CONC n style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Township&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; on Waugh&amp;#39;s Run in 1808. He paid his tax (something like 75 cents per year) up until 1818 the year he died. The probate record is very brief and appears u
2 CONC nder the name of John &lt;span&gt;Toops&lt;/span&gt;, although the tombstone (which still exists and is legible in the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Moore&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span sty
2 CONC le="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Cemetery&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; on the Cincinnati Pike, Twin &lt;span&gt;township&lt;/span&gt;, Ross County, Ohio) lists it correctly as Dups. Samuel &lt;span&gt;Toops&lt;/span&gt; was listed as adm
2 CONC inistrator and Daniel &lt;span&gt;Toops&lt;/span&gt; as his bondsman. But there is no record of the disposal of the estate or any deed transferring land.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Hans Jacob &lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp
2 CONC ; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;3&lt;/span&gt; Mar 1715 (&lt;span&gt;Affoltern&lt;/span&gt;, Switzerland) - 1761 (Lebanon, PA) Married (1744) Barbara &lt;span&gt;Wierick&lt;/span&gt; 1717 (Lebanon, PA) 1769&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 1
2 CONC 0pt"&gt;Hans Jacob &lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt; was born &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;3 March 1715&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; in &lt;span&gt;Affoltern&lt;/span&gt; at &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size
2 CONC : 10pt"&gt;Mt.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Albis&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; near &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Z
2 CONC urich&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Switzerland&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;. He was the sixth child with three older sisters and two older and one younger bro
2 CONC ther. His older brother, Heinrich, had gone to &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Pennsylvania&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; in 1729 and, as soon as he was 21, Jacob followed. &lt;span&gt;Leaving his home on &lt;/sp
2 CONC an&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;4 April 1736&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; together with Rudolph &lt;span&gt;Haug&lt;/span&gt;, a young man of 25 years.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;
2 CONC /span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; They arrived in &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Philadelphia&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; on the ship &amp;quot;Princess Augusta&amp;quot;, on &lt;/span&gt;&lt;spa
2 CONC n style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;16 September 1736&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;. The route by which they traveled was up the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Rhine&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; &lt
2 CONC ;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;River&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; in small boats to the mouth of the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Rhine&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; at &lt;/
2 CONC span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Rotterdam&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; where they waited for an English ship bound for &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;America&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 1
2 CONC 0pt"&gt;. Another &lt;span&gt;passenger&lt;/span&gt; on this same ship was &lt;span&gt;Jost&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;, who had no direct relationship to Jacob as he was later proved to have come from &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="fo
2 CONC nt-size: 10pt"&gt;Germany&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;At the end of the journey the passengers were taken to the courthouse in Philadelphia where they took th
2 CONC e oath of allegiance to the King of England and at that time Hans Jacob &lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt; signed his name in legible German script, using the spelling &lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;, though in the Swiss records the name of his family ha
2 CONC d been as often recorded as Dubs as Dups. In &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;America&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;, however, it would be spelled much more often &lt;span&gt;Toops&lt;/span&gt; or &lt;span&gt;&lt;s
2 CONC pan&gt;Tups&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;, that&lt;/span&gt; being the way it sounded to English ears. Also it would appear more often as Jacob rather than Hans Jacob or John Jacob.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size
2 CONC : 10pt"&gt;As would be expected, Jacob settled near his brother, Henry (Heinrich), buying land from Thomas Penn in what was East Hanover Twp., &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Lancaster County&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size
2 CONC : 10pt"&gt;, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Pennsylvania&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;, but now &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Lebanon&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; &lt;/span
2 CONC &gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;County&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;. This was about seven miles west of the point on the &lt;span&gt;Swatara&lt;/span&gt; Creek in Bethel Twp where Henry lived. It was near what is still k
2 CONC nown as Harper&amp;#39;s Tavern. The warrant for 150 acres of land, dated &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;6 June 1737&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;, made out to Hans Jacob &lt;span&gt;Tupz&lt;/span&gt;, still rem
2 CONC ains in the State Land Office at &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Harrisburg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;PA.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; A
2 CONC t that time the Penn&amp;#39;s land sold at the rate of 15 pounds 10 shillings per 100 acres and required an annual quit-rent of half &lt;span&gt;a penny&lt;/span&gt; sterling per acre. The farm when surveyed many years later was found to contain 2
2 CONC 49 acres.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;The next evidence we have of Jacob &lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt; in &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;America&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&g
2 CONC t; is the notation in 1740 that he was a member of the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Swarata&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Reform&lt;/sp
2 CONC an&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Church&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; - a church that at that time was located on the farm of Henry &lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt; east o
2 CONC f what is now &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Jonestown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;PA.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; In 1742 he acted as spo
2 CONC nsor at the baptism of a child of John &lt;span&gt;Heil&lt;/span&gt;. But within the next few years he became a convert of the Moravian sect and belonged to the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Bethel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="fon
2 CONC t-size: 10pt"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Moravian&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Church&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;, located about tw
2 CONC o miles east of Jonestown. Both of his brothers remained in the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Swarata&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Chur
2 CONC ch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;In 1743 Jacob &lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt; and Rudolph &lt;span&gt;Haug&lt;/span&gt; returned to &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="f
2 CONC ont-size: 10pt"&gt;Switzerland&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; to bring back more of the family to &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;America&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;. They arrived in Switzerla
2 CONC nd 24 February and started back again on 2 May 1743 with a party of 52 people, including the younger brother, Hans &lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;, and two married sisters, Barbara and &lt;span&gt;Verena&lt;/span&gt;, with their husbands and children
2 CONC . Hans &lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt; arrived in &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;America&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; but the sisters have not been identified in &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;A
2 CONC merica&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;The year after he returned to America Jacob &lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt; married. Unfortunately none of the birth records o
2 CONC f his children gave his wife&amp;#39;s name. It is possible that he was married twice. Certainly Barbara &lt;span&gt;Wierich&lt;/span&gt; was his wife and mother of his last six children. In the probate record in the courthouse at &lt;/span&gt;&lt;
2 CONC span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Lancaster&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; her name is signed as both Rebecca and Barbara. Since she signed by mark this error was possible. But the decisive evidence that she was Barbara &lt;span&gt;
2 CONC Wierich&lt;/span&gt; comes from a receipt given by Hans &lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt; as guardian of the daughter, Anna Marie. This receipt was for Anna Marie&amp;#39;s share of her grandfather, George &lt;span&gt;Wierrich&amp;#39;s&lt;/span&gt; es
2 CONC tate, and was dated &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;14 March 1772&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;. (This receipt remained in the papers of Henry &lt;span&gt;Hautz&lt;/span&gt;, son-in-law of Hans &lt;span&gt;Dups&l
2 CONC t;/span&gt;, and the executor of this estate.) George &lt;span&gt;Wierich&amp;#39;s&lt;/span&gt; estate was settled in 1771-1772 though he died in 1751, leaving a number of children. Barbara was probably the oldest child but there seems to be som
2 CONC e doubt that she could have been old enough to have married and have had children by 1745. W. H. &lt;span&gt;Egle&lt;/span&gt; in his &amp;quot;History of Lebanon County&amp;quot; makes a guess at the ages of the &lt;span&gt;Wierich&lt;/span&gt; ch
2 CONC ildren which must be incorrect. The sister of Barbara &lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;, Maria Elizabeth &lt;span&gt;Wierich&lt;/span&gt; was a sponsor at the baptism of Barbara&amp;#39;s son Henry in 1754. Barbara &lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt; died so
2 CONC metime between 1767 and 1771.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;the&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; name of Jacob &lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt; (&lt;span&gt;Toops&lt;/span&g
2 CONC t;) appears regularly on such tax lists as still survive of East Hanover Twp. The years of the French-Indian War in &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;America&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; were a time of terror in t
2 CONC hat vicinity. In the year 1756 almost half of the names on the tax rolls are marked &amp;quot;killed&amp;quot; or &amp;quot;&lt;span&gt;flede&lt;/span&gt;&amp;quot; or &amp;quot;child taken&amp;quot; but there was no note of disaster appended to th
2 CONC e &lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt; brothers.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;In 1761 there was a note of Americanization in the appearance of Jacob &lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;&amp;#39; name among the roll of the to
2 CONC wnship officers. His job was Overseer of Roads and his was the only German name among the politically predominate Scotch-Irish ones. In the same year he died, perhaps unexpectedly, as he left no will or instructions for the care of &lt;span&gt;this
2 CONC &lt;/span&gt; eight minor children.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;When their father died, the elder sons, Jacob and John, being fourteen years old or above were allowed to choose their guardians.&lt;
2 CONC /span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; Jacob chose Adam Harper, an &lt;span&gt;inkeeper&lt;/span&gt; near the family farm, John chose Henry Waggoner, a blacksmith of &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Bethel&lt;/sp
2 CONC an&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Township&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;. Anna Maria, Daniel and Henry were placed under the guardianship of their uncle, John Dups. The th
2 CONC ree youngest were placed under the guardianship of Urban Long but evidently remained with their mother as she was allowed three pounds a year for Juliana, four pounds for Leonard and two pounds for Frederick. Jacob, the oldest son, having obtaine
2 CONC d his majority in 1766 petitioned the court to take over the plantation and the land at that time was appraised at 230 pounds in value.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Henry (Heinrich) &lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; 1699&lt;/spa
2 CONC n&gt; (&lt;span&gt;Alffoltern&lt;/span&gt;, SW) - 2 Nov 1753 (&lt;span&gt;Alffoltern&lt;/span&gt;, SW) Married (13 Dec 1692) Veronica &lt;span&gt;Schneebli&lt;/span&gt; 1670 (&lt;span&gt;Alffoltern&lt;/span&gt;, SW) 25 May 1733 (&lt;span&gt;Alffolt
2 CONC ern&lt;/span&gt;, SW)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;He was an &amp;quot;&lt;span&gt;Ehgaumer&lt;/span&gt;&amp;quot;, a member of the church council.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Hans Jacob &lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;&lt;
2 CONC span&gt;&amp;nbsp; Apr&lt;/span&gt; 1637 (&lt;span&gt;Alffoltern&lt;/span&gt;, SW)- ? (&lt;span&gt;Alffoltern&lt;/span&gt;, SW) Married (17 Oct 1664) Verona &lt;span&gt;Epprecht&lt;/span&gt; - Sep 1643 (&lt;span&gt;Alffoltern&lt;/span&gt;, SW) Afte
2 CONC r 1695 (&lt;span&gt;Alffoltern&lt;/span&gt;, SW)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Hans (&lt;span&gt;Dupps&lt;/span&gt;) &lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; Jun&lt;/span&gt; 1609 - ? Married (9 Feb 1630&lt;span&gt;)&lt;span&gt;Vreni&lt;/span&gt;&lt
2 CONC ;/span&gt; &lt;span&gt;Bumann&lt;/span&gt; Jun 1609 (SW) ? (&lt;span&gt;Alffoltern&lt;/span&gt;, SW)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;In 1634 was a member of the church council. Presumably his wife was not from &lt;span&gt;Affo
2 CONC ltern&lt;/span&gt; and the marriage took place at the residence of his bride. It was customary at that time that the wedding was held at the residence of the bride.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Michael &lt;span&gt;Dupps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&a
2 CONC mp;nbsp; May&lt;/span&gt; 1579 (&lt;span&gt;Alffoltern&lt;/span&gt;, SW) - 30 May 1633 (&lt;span&gt;Alffoltern&lt;/span&gt;, SW) Married (3 Feb 1600) Barbara &lt;span&gt;Sutterin&lt;/span&gt; - 1579 (&lt;span&gt;Alffoltern&lt;/span&gt;, SW) - 162
2 CONC 2 (&lt;span&gt;Alffoltern&lt;/span&gt;, SW)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;In his second marriage he was married to &lt;span&gt;Verena&lt;/span&gt; Lips, who at her death &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;24 Ja
2 CONC n 1640&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; is called &amp;quot;widow of Michael &lt;span&gt;Dupss&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span&gt;selig&lt;/span&gt;&amp;quot;. Only this Michael can be in question, for the other Michael lived at &lt;span&gt;A
2 CONC esch&lt;/span&gt; until 1662. Michel &lt;span&gt;Dupss&lt;/span&gt;, called German, shoemaker, died May 1633&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Jergman&lt;/span&gt; (&lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;) &lt;span&gt;Dupps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&
2 CONC amp;nbsp; 1550&lt;/span&gt; (&lt;span&gt;Alffoltern&lt;/span&gt;, SW) - 11 Jul 1621 (&lt;span&gt;Alffoltern&lt;/span&gt;, SW) Married (5 Dec 1573) &lt;span&gt;Vreni&lt;/span&gt; Gut 1555 (&lt;span&gt;Oberlennern&lt;/span&gt;, SW) - ?&amp;nbsp;&lt;/
2 CONC p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Since records were not required in &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Switzerland&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; before 1550, there is no record of his birth.&lt;/spa
2 CONC n&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;The elsewhere unknown name of &lt;span&gt;Jergman&lt;/span&gt; or &lt;span&gt;Jergman&lt;/span&gt; is possibly a development from &lt;span&gt;Jerg&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span&gt;Joerg&lt;/span&
2 CONC gt; - &lt;span&gt;Georg&lt;/span&gt;. In further variation of the name and to distinguish between other Dubs his sons were called &amp;quot;German&amp;quot;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;Jergman&lt;
2 CONC /span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt; &lt;span&gt;Dupss&lt;/span&gt; married &lt;span&gt;Vreni&lt;/span&gt; Gut from &lt;span&gt;Oberlunnernat&lt;/span&gt; at &lt;span&gt;Affolten&lt;/span&gt; on &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="fo
2 CONC nt-size: 10pt"&gt;5 December 1573&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;. He was a shoemaker and last cutter. He died &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;11 July 1621&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt"&gt;.&lt;/span
2 CONC &gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
