2 CONC is.&lt;/pre&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;According to Stephen White, the name of the wife or wives of Germain has never been known. Several early writers, such as Bona Arsenault, Histoir
2 CONC e et Genealogie des Acadiens, and Adrien Bergeron, Le Grand Arrangement des Acadiens au Quebec, have stated that the wife was Marie Bourgeios, given the documented fact that Germain Doucet and Jacques (Jacob) Bourgeois were brothers-in-law. In hi
2 CONC s notes, Stephen White states: &amp;ldquo;It is not possible that the mother of the children of Germain Doucet is a sister of Jacques Bourgeois' wife, as certain authors have proposed, being given that the in-laws of Jacques Bourgeois didn't get ma
2 CONC rried until 1627. There exists the possibility that Germain Doucet nevertheless married, in second nuptials, to a daughter of Guillaume Trahan who gave him no surviving children; but it is as possible that such a second wife is the sister of Jacque
2 CONC s Bourgeois and not the sister of his wife. (See SGCF Vol VI, p 372.)&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 10px 0px 15px; padding: 0px; line-height: 17px;"&gt;NOTE 3: The parentage of Germain Doucet (born about1641): Bona Arsenault and other
2 CONC s list Germain DOUCET (born about 1641) as the son of Pierre DOUCET (born about 1621). However, according to a dispensation granted by the Catholic Church, Germain has to be the brother or half-brother of Pierre rather than his son. Below is a summ
2 CONC ary from Michele Doucette's web site pertaining to this issue. Her web sites are listed below, where you may see her full article. In a letter from Stephen White, dated May 8, 1992, is where the evidence first comes to light that Germain DOUCET (bo
2 CONC rn about 1641 in Port Royal) is not the son of Pierre DOUCET (born about 1621 in France) as previously thought by many, including Adrien Bergeron and Bona Arsenault. Stephen White proves that Germain is the son of Germain DOUCET as a result of disp
2 CONC ensations granted by the Catholic Church when descendants of Germain (1641) intermarried with descendants of Pierre (1621). A dispensation in the 3rd degree was granted Nov 26, 1726, at Port-Royal to Germain DOUCET (grandson of Germain (1641)) to m
2 CONC arry Fran&amp;ccedil;oise COMEAU (granddaughter of Pierre). In reviewing Le Grand Arrangement Des Acadiens au Qu&amp;eacute;bec and Histoire et G&amp;eacute;n&amp;eacute;alogie des Acadiens, there are the following individuals named Germain DOUCET:
