2 CONC amily: 'calibri','sans-serif'"&gt;The Toups Family or&lt;br&gt; The Toups Clan and How It All Began&lt;br&gt; Ten Generations from 1721 to 1991&lt;br&gt; by: Neil Toups&lt;br&gt; &lt;br&gt; &amp;quot;The most ancient mention of the Toups name is i
2 CONC n connection with the Austrian coat of arms granted to a member of the &amp;quot;dubs&amp;quot; family in Switzerland in 1450. The Dubs family is noted in Switzerland in 1470 in the village of Wetzikon. They were also known as Thubs. A Rudulph Tupp
2 CONC s is noted in the village of Hausen am Albis in 1493. Casper Dubs emigrated to the Palatinate region of southwest Germany between 1700 and 1708, in 1711 he was in Eppingen. &lt;br&gt; In 1720, Caspar Dubs, in response to John Law&amp;rsquo;s glorio
2 CONC us accounts of Louisiana, Dubs left Eppingen, Germany for the French part of L&amp;rsquo;Orient. Unable to resist these opportunities offered by Law&amp;rsquo;s Company of the Indies, he and his wife Anna Hedinger, and sons George and Friederich de
2 CONC parted L&amp;rsquo;Orient in June of 1721 on the ship &amp;quot;La Durance&amp;quot;. The arrival date in Louisiana is given as October 1721. Usually there was a stopover in Santa Domingo. These sick and weakened settlers from Germany were literall
2 CONC y dumped on the banks of the Mississippi River, obviously in the wilderness and without shelter, carrying a few meager belongings with them. A less hardy and courageous people might not have survived. They existed on wild game and what grain and dr
2 CONC y beans they could get from the Indians. Houses had to be built and land had to be cleared for spring planting. Evidently, Bienville gave them what he could spare but it as so little it would not carry them through winter and early spring. &lt;br&g
2 CONC t; The census of 13 May 1722 gives the number of people established on the Mississippi River above New Orleans as far as Point Coupe as 413. The German village of Hoffen where there were 25 men, 29 women and 40 children is where Caspar is listed. T
2 CONC he German coast accounted for 60% of these settlers. &lt;br&gt; After getting partially settled, and in the midst of harvesting their few crops, they were struck by a terrible hurricane on 12 Sept 1722. In the summer of 1723, the city of New Orlean
2 CONC s and surrounding area was plagued with an epidemic known then as &amp;quot;Purple Fever&amp;quot;. &lt;br&gt; By 1724, the Germans were still in need of assistance from the Company of the Indies in the form of supplies and provisions. This aid wa
2 CONC s not free gratis but added to the already overburdened debt of a poverty sticken people. In the census of 12 September 1724, Gaspar Toups is listed as 40, a native of Aesch, Switzerland, a Protestant, farmer, a wife and two sons aged 10 and 12. H
2 CONC e owned three pigs and had been on his one and one-half arpents of cleared land for two years. This is the first evidence of Caspar Dubs later changed to Gaspard Toups. &lt;br&gt; Gaspard&amp;rsquo;s wife, Anna, was last mentioned in the 1726 censu
2 CONC s. We assume that she died just prior. On 24 May, 1728 at age 45, Gaspard married Marie Barbe Kitteler. &lt;br&gt; The German colonists were not given any slaves by the Company of the Indies as had been promised. They were usually given or sold t
2 CONC o the wealthy landowners of the area. The people were becoming more and more discouraged as the difficult and laborious life continued. They were at the point of leaving the and going to Natchez or Illinois for an easier way of life. Slaves were ve
2 CONC ry important to the success of the colony but the slaves and Indians kept things in constant turmoil. For many years the people of the German coast were &amp;quot;harassed&amp;quot; by the Chickasaw and Choctaw tribes. Between raids by the Indian
2 CONC s and unrest by the slaves, Gaspard Toups and many others of L&amp;rsquo;Anse aux Outardes decided to move across the Mississippi River to a safer place to live. Gaspard had been on his land for almost twenty years and was not 64 years old. He exch
2 CONC anged his four arpents of land and house for an equal amount of land on the right bank of the river. Five days later the renegade Choctaws struck the village he had just left killing one man, scalped his wife, and kidnapped his daughter and a slave
2 CONC . This caused most of the colonists to abandon their homes and go to New Orleans with their Negro slaves. They were sent back by threats but with a guarantee of protection by a detachment of troops. The friendly Choctaws came to their aid and in tw
2 CONC o months killed the renegade Choctaws. The troops then returned to New Orleans but seemingly too soon for another group of hostile Choctaws again raided, this time at Cheval Plantation. They captured all of the guns of the settlers and Negroes work
2 CONC ing in the fields. The frightened people crossed the river to safety. The colony was fast diminishing so Governor Kerlerec agreed to send these people troops to protect them and live permanently on the land. The colonists requested the troops be Sw
2 CONC iss which the government agreed to do. &lt;br&gt; More colonists arrived and an advance of slaves was given to each. The future would definitely improve. Gaspard&amp;rsquo;s wife, Marie, died 10 April 1754 and Gaspard apparently died in mid 1755 a
2 CONC t the age of 72. He had evidently transferred all of his property to his son, Frederic by act of sale on 12 March 1755. Had this man not been such a hard worker and of great courage and perseverance, our record of our ancestors would be very differ
2 CONC ent indeed.&amp;quot;&lt;/span&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
