2 TITL HIstory of Emperor Charlemagne
1 _OID 2e1dbc6a-0808-48f1-b897-0961e56c0ff1
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;hr&gt; &lt;strong&gt; &amp;quot;By the sword and   the cross,&amp;quot; Charlemagne (Charles the Great) became master of Western Europe. It was     falling into decay when Charlemagne became  joint king of the Frank
2 CONC s in 768. Except in  the monasteries, people had all but         forgotten education and the arts. Boldly    Charlemagne conquered barbarians and kings  alike. By restoring the roots of learning   and order, he preserved many political      right
2 CONC s and revived culture.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;p&gt; &lt;strong&gt;                                                                  Charlemagne&amp;#39;s grandfather was Charles  Martel, the warrior who crushed the         Saracens (see Charles Martel)
2 CONC . Charlemagne  was the elder son of Bertrade (&amp;quot;Bertha      Greatfoot&amp;quot;) and Pepin the Short, first      &amp;quot;mayor of the palace&amp;quot; to become king of the Franks. Although schools had almost         disappeared in the 8t
2 CONC h century, historians  believe that Bertrade gave young Charles    some education and that he learned to read. His devotion to the church became the great driving force of his remarkable life.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt; &lt;strong&gt
2 CONC ;                                                        Charlemagne was tall, powerful, and    tireless. His secretary, Eginhard, wrote    that Charlemagne had fair hair and a &amp;quot;face  laughing and merry . . . his appearance was always stat
2 CONC ely and dignified.&amp;quot; He had a     ready wit, but could be stern. His tastes   were simple and moderate. He delighted in   hunting, riding, and swimming. He wore the  Frankish dress: linen shirt and breeches, a silk-fringed tunic, hose wrapp
2 CONC ed with       bands, and, in winter, a tight coat of      otter or marten skins. Over all these       garments &amp;quot;he flung a blue cloak, and he     always had a sword girt about him.&amp;quot;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt; &lt;strong&gt
2 CONC ;                                                             Charlemagne&amp;#39;s character was         contradictory. In an age when the usual     penalty for defeat was death, Charlemagne   several times spared the lives of his       defeated f
2 CONC oes; yet in 782 at Verden, after  a Saxon uprising, he ordered 4,500 Saxons   beheaded. He compelled the clergy and       nobles to reform, but he divorced two of    his four wives without any cause. He forced kings and princes to kneel at his feet
2 CONC , yet his mother and his two favorite wives often overruled him in his own household.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt; &lt;strong&gt;                                                     &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Charlemagne Begins Hi
2 CONC s Reign &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;                                                               In 768, when Charlemagne was 26, he    and his brother Carloman inherited the      kingdom of the Franks. In 771 Carloman      died, and Charlemagn
2 CONC e became sole ruler of  the kingdom. At that time the northern half of Europe was still pagan and lawless. In   the south, the Roman Catholic church was    striving to assert its power against the    Lombard kingdom in Italy. In Charlemagne&amp;#39
2 CONC ;s  own realm, the Franks were falling back     into barbarian ways, neglecting their       education and religion.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;p&gt; &lt;strong&gt;                                                                      Charlemagne was determin
2 CONC ed to          strengthen his realm and to bring order to  Europe. In 772 he launched a 30-year        campaign that conquered and Christianized   the powerful pagan Saxons in the north. He  subdued the Avars, a huge Tatar tribe on    the Danube. H
2 CONC e compelled the rebellious     Bavarian dukes to submit to him. When       possible he preferred to settle matters     peacefully, however. For example,           Charlemagne offered to pay the Lombard king Desiderius for return of lands to the pop
2 CONC e, but, when Desiderius refused, Charlemagne   seized his kingdom in 773 to 774 and        restored the Papal States.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt; &lt;strong&gt;                                                              The key to Charlemag
2 CONC ne&amp;#39;s amazing       conquests was his ability to organize.      During his reign he sent out more than 50   military expeditions. He rode as commander  at the head of at least half of them. He    moved his armies over wide reaches of       c
2 CONC ountry with unbelievable speed, but every  move was planned in advance. Before a       campaign he told the counts, princes, and   bishops throughout his realm how many men   they should bring, what arms they were to   carry, and even what to loa
2 CONC d in the supply  wagons. These feats of organization and the swift marches later led Napoleon to study   his tactics.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt; &lt;strong&gt;                                                                                 O
2 CONC ne of Charlemagne&amp;#39;s minor campaigns   has become the most famous. In 778 he led   his army into Spain to battle the infidel   Saracens. On its return, Basques ambushed   the rear guard at Roncesvalles, in northern Spain, and killed &amp;quo
2 CONC t;Count Roland.&amp;quot; Roland    became a great hero of medieval songs and   romances (see Roland).&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt; &lt;strong&gt;                                                                       By 800 Charlemagne was th
2 CONC e undisputed  ruler of Western Europe. His vast realm     covered what are now France, Switzerland,   Belgium, and The Netherlands. It included   half of present-day Italy and Germany, part of Austria, and the Spanish March           (&amp;quot;bor
2 CONC der&amp;quot;). The broad March reached to the  Ebro River. By thus establishing a central  government over Western Europe, Charlemagne restored much of the unity of the old Roman Empire and paved the way for the            development of modern Eu
2 CONC rope.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt; &lt;strong&gt;                                                           &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Crowned Emperor&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt
2 CONC ;                                                                              On Christmas Day in 800, while         Charlemagne knelt in prayer in Saint        Peter&amp;#39;s in Rome, Pope Leo III seized a      golden crown from the altar and pl
2 CONC aced it   on the bowed head of the king. The throng   in the church shouted, &amp;quot;To Charles the      August, crowned by God, great and pacific   emperor, long life and victory!&amp;quot;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;p&gt; &lt;strong&gt
2 CONC ;                                                             Charlemagne is said to have been       surprised by the coronation, declaring that he would not have come into the church had  he known the pope&amp;#39;s plan. However, some     histori
2 CONC ans say the pope would not have      dared to act without Charlemagne&amp;#39;s          knowledge.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt; &lt;strong&gt;                                                                                   The coronation wa
2 CONC s the foundation of   the Holy Roman Empire. Though Charlemagne   did not use the title, he is considered the first Holy Roman emperor (see Holy Roman    Empire).&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt; &lt;strong&gt
2 CONC ;                                                                                                                           &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Reform and Renaissance &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt
2 CONC ;                                                                      Charlemagne had deep sympathy for the  peasants and believed that government       should be for the benefit of the governed.  When he came to the throne, various local   govern
2 CONC ors, called &amp;quot;counts,&amp;quot; had become lax  and oppressive. To reform them, he expanded the work of investigators, called missi     dominici. He prescribed their duties in     documents called capitularies and sent them out in teams o
2 CONC f two&amp;Auml;&amp;Auml;a churchman and a      noble. They rode to all parts of the realm, inspecting government, administering        justice, and reawakening all citizens to    their civil and religious duties.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;p&gt; &lt;strong
2 CONC &gt;                                                            Twice a year Charlemagne summoned the  chief men of the empire to discuss its      affairs. In all problems he was the final   arbiter, even in church issues, and he      largely unifi
2 CONC ed church and state.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt; &lt;strong&gt;                                                            Charlemagne was a tireless reformer    who tried to improve his people&amp;#39;s lot in    many ways. He set up money s
2 CONC tandards to     encourage commerce, tried to build a        Rhine-Danube canal, and urged better        farming methods. He especially worked to    spread education and Christianity in every  class of people.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt; &lt;s
2 CONC trong&gt;                                                                             He revived the Palace School at        Aachen, his capital. He set up other        schools, opening them to peasant boys as    well as nobles.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/
2 CONC p&gt;&lt;p&gt; &lt;strong&gt;                                                                              Charlemagne never stopped studying. He brought an English monk, Alcuin, and other  scholars to his court. He learned to read   Latin and som
2 CONC e Greek but apparently did not master writing. At meals, instead of having jesters perform, he listened to men reading from learned works.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt; &lt;strong&gt
2 CONC ;                                                                             To revive church music, Charlemagne had monks sent from Rome to train his       Frankish singers. To restore some           appreciation of art, he brought valuable    pi
2 CONC eces from Italy. An impressive monument   to his religious devotion is the cathedral  at Aachen, which he built and where he was  buried (see Aachen).&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;strong&gt
2 CONC ;                                                                         At Charlemagne&amp;#39;s death in 814 only one of his three sons, Louis, was living.       Louis&amp;#39;s weak rule brought on the rise of    civil wars and revolts. After h
2 CONC is death his three quarreling sons split the empire      between them by the Partition of Verdun in  843&lt;/strong&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
