2 TITL Nan Thatcher Ford Prescott
1 _OID 34d9da76-4158-4bfb-af0e-46abca747974
1 DATE 1907
1 PLAC Cameron, Texas
1 _DSCR A difficult life as a single mother of eight.
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;Nan Thatcher Ford was born 6-13, 1879, Tennessee.&amp;nbsp; Nan was the daughter of Charlie Franklin Ford and Americus Clementine Guinn and the second wife of John William Prescott.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&
2 CONC amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;John and Nan were married 8-26-1897 and they lived in Cameron, Texas.&amp;nbsp; Their first child was born 16 August, 1898 and next was William Conley born 8 December 1899.&amp;nbsp; At that time the family moved to Bill
2 CONC y&amp;#39;s home in Elba, Alabama, were he had been given land to farm.&amp;nbsp; The other children, Lola, 1 April, 1901, Lela, 14 December 1902, Daniel Lafayette, 23 March, 1904, John Terrol, 13 June, 1905 and Charlie Ford, 30 August, 1905 were b
2 CONC orn.&amp;nbsp; They lived about one forth a mile from Jackson Lafayette and Nancy Etta Prescott.&amp;nbsp; The grandfather of Jackson had built a log cabin near the creek and then in later years William Daniel had planted several rows of pecan tree
2 CONC s in front of the house he had built.&amp;nbsp; The pecan trees and the little family cemetery is all that is left now.&amp;nbsp; It was told that in 1907, Billy took his family by wagon to Mobile and to the sea shore.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;There the
2 CONC y lived out of their wagon and the children went wading and played in the sand right in their front door.&amp;nbsp; The adults fished and gathered oysters and packed then down in barrells of salt to preserve them for later use.&amp;nbsp; Late in Oc
2 CONC tober the family decided to return home.&amp;nbsp; On the way Billy found and gathered some walnuts and ate some on the way home.&amp;nbsp; He became very ill,&amp;nbsp;as he got home he died 26 October, 1907.&amp;nbsp; Nan was expecting their 8t
2 CONC h child and even though she was offered continued care and support by her father-in-law, Jackson Lafayette Prescott, she wanted to go back to her own family.&amp;nbsp; Nan wrote her father, Charlie Franklin Ford, to come and get her, which he did.&
2 CONC amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; They loaded her family and goods into his wagon and making the trip back to Texas, her baby, Reba Gwinn was born 26 Jan, 1908 in Cameron, Texas.&amp;nbsp; &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Nan got littl
2 CONC e or no help from her parents, possibly they had no way&amp;nbsp;to help.&amp;nbsp; It seems, as the children told of it,&amp;nbsp;they were&amp;nbsp;forbidden to go the short distance from the slave shack where they lived to the main house where N
2 CONC an&amp;#39;s parents lived.&amp;nbsp; By the spring of 1910, Nan in desperation, put her children in&amp;nbsp;an Orphan&amp;nbsp;Home in Dallas so she could try to make a living as a seamstress.&amp;nbsp; She sent money that she could spare and mad
2 CONC e and sent the children clothing she made for them.&amp;nbsp; The children were split up according to age and put into different buildings.&amp;nbsp; The children that were able, were made to work very hard.&amp;nbsp; In the fall, Reba Gwinn, becam
2 CONC e very ill.&amp;nbsp; Lois, being the oldest, did not hear of it until the home had notified Nan to come and get Reba as she was dying.&amp;nbsp; When Nan arrived and saw&amp;nbsp;her other children in rags and learned from the children that they h
2 CONC ad never recieved the money or clothing, she brought them all back home.&amp;nbsp; Nan was determined to do the best she could for them, however little it might be.&amp;nbsp; After struggling alone, for several years, Nan decided to marry Jim Dodso
2 CONC n, but the children objected.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Some of Nan&amp;#39;s children went to live with their Aunt Jenny and some to Uncle Gano, and refused to come home.&amp;nbsp; So Nan decided to not marry, or divorced,&amp;nbsp;Mr. Dodson.
2 CONC &amp;nbsp; She lived on a farm, with her children,&amp;nbsp;and&amp;nbsp;she and her children&amp;nbsp;worked as tenant farmers.&amp;nbsp; Some of Nan&amp;#39;s brothers and sister took in a few of the children, to help the family out.&amp;nbsp; I
2 CONC n 1935, Nan married her sister&amp;#39;s widowed husband, Roy Sample and lived with him until her death in September 1943.&amp;nbsp; Nan died of heart disease.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Written by Delbert Hess and Bonnelle Hess Perkins, Nan&amp;#39;s grand
2 CONC children.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
