2 CONC ="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;From there, Doucet returned to France where he met Aulnay aboard the&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt; font-style: italic;"&gt;Esperance-en-Dieu,&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;and they returned to Canada with new French settl
2 CONC ers.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 22.53999900817871px; margin: 5pt 0pt 5pt 36pt; text-indent: 36pt;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Later, in 1635, Aulnay was ordered t
2 CONC o take possession of Fort Penobscot, to the western boundary of Acadia, near present day Castin (Maine), Colombia.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Once again, Germain Doucet accompanied d'Aulnay, this time with his family.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&
2 CONC lt;span&gt;Aulnay returned to Port-Royal, after the fort was taken and Doucet left at the head of a small garrison.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;The British soon sent a detachment of Plymouth, Massachusetts, to try to take the fort, but the Fr
2 CONC ench under the command of Germain Doucet, successfully repelled the attack.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 22.53999900817871px; margin: 5pt 0pt 5pt 36pt; text-indent: 36
2 CONC pt;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Razilly was governor of a part of Acadia.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Charles de La Tour (1593-1666) governed the rest of the colony.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Tower and Razilly coexist in Canada for peaceful condi
2 CONC tions.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Razilly was resolved to establish a colony and the construction of the colony while La Tour was only interested in the fur trade.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Beaver pelt was highly prized goods in Fran
2 CONC ce where it was used for making felt for hats.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 22.53999900817871px; margin: 5pt 0pt 5pt 36pt; text-indent: 36pt;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;In late 1
2 CONC 635 Razilly died suddenly, leaving his post as governor of his side of the colony to his brother, Claude Razilly.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Refusing to leave France, Claude Razilly has delegated its powers to Charles de Menou d'Aulnay.&lt;/
2 CONC span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Shortly after Aulnay succeeded in this position, relations with La Tour deteriorated, partly due to confusion geographical division of the settlement between the two governors by the King of France and partly an aversi
2 CONC on between the two men.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Tower was outgoing, affable and engaging.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;It was common ancestry and lived among the Mi'kmaq, who had several children with Indian women.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;n
2 CONC bsp;&lt;span&gt;Aulnay was reserved and distant.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;He came from a noble family and felt that La Tour was not his equal in status or intelligence.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-family: 'times ne
2 CONC w roman'; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 22.53999900817871px; margin: 5pt 0pt 5pt 36pt; text-indent: 36pt;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;In 1636, this dispute has escalated to open war, and demanded that La Tour d'Aulnay abandon the post in Penobscot, commanded b
2 CONC y Doucet.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;Aulnay and Doucet has refused to do so and began making plans to build the fort.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;A small group from Fort Penobscot in Port Royal for provisions, which may well include the commande
2 CONC r, Doucet, was captured by the faithful to La Tour and held prisoner forces.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;But soon after, La Tour was defeated and captured after a brief naval engagement with the boat Aulnay.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;
2 CONC Capuchin priests convinced Aulnay not run Tower and instead refer the matter to the responsible of the French Government in Paris judication.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Tower and his wife were allowed to leave and return to Fort La Tour on t
2 CONC he St. John River.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 22.53999900817871px; margin: 5pt 0pt 5pt 36pt; text-indent: 36pt;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;In 1645, after the death of the comma
2 CONC nder, Isaac Pessely, Doucet was appointed commander of the garrison of Port Royal.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;1645 also saw the end of the armed conflict between Aulnay and La Tour.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size
2 CONC : 10pt;"&gt;Easter weekend 1645 Aulnay attacked Fort La Tour with 200 men and four ships led by the&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt; font-style: italic;"&gt;Grand&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Cardinal.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/
2 CONC span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;As commander of Port Royal and a captain experienced most certainly Doucet participated in the battle.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 10pt; line-
2 CONC height: 22.53999900817871px; margin: 5pt 0pt 5pt 36pt; text-indent: 36pt;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;After a three-day battle, the forces of Aulnay overran the remains of the fort and accepted the surrender of Fort La Tour's wife, Francoise Marie Jacqueline
2 CONC , who was in command of the fort at the point of sword.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;(Her husband was in Boston trying to convince the British to help in its conflict with D'Aulnay).&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size
2 CONC : 10pt;"&gt;Francoise Marie Jacqueline defeated an attack by Aulnay in March of the same year in which the&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt; font-style: italic;"&gt;Grand Cardinal&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style="font-size
2 CONC : 10pt;"&gt;was severely damaged forcing Aulnay retreat in Port Royal.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;In an act of brutality Aulnay executed the surviving men of the fort.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-family
2 CONC : 'times new roman'; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 22.53999900817871px; margin: 5pt 0pt 5pt 36pt; text-indent: 36pt;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;In 1647, the faithful in Aulnay forces had consolidated their power over the colony, and La Tour was forced to tak
2 CONC e refuge in Quebec.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;However, in December 1650, Aulnay drowned when his canoe capsized in the Bear River.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;The son of one of the Indian women Aulnay Tower was murdered in an act of r
2 CONC evenge.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;widow d'Aulnay, Jeanne Mottin and possession of Aulnay Germain Doucet executed.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 22.5399990081787
2 CONC 1px; margin: 5pt 0pt 5pt 36pt; text-indent: 36pt;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Germain enjoyed a particularly close relationship with Aulnay who in his will dated January 20, 1649, instructed his wife (Jeanne Mottin) to deal with Germain and his wife. He spok
2 CONC e of him as "Germain Doucet dict La Verdure, the Parish Couperna en Brie.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;"After the death of Aulnay Doucet became surrogate guardian of the children of Aulnay.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-
2 CONC family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 22.53999900817871px; margin: 5pt 0pt 5pt 36pt; text-indent: 36pt;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;In 1651, Jeanne de Mottin rival wife of her late husband, Charles de La Tour, and by this marriage, La Tour r
2 CONC eturned to power in the colony.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;Doucet signed as a witness to their marriage, and La Tour left him at the head of the garrison of Port Royal.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-family: 'times new roman'; fo
2 CONC nt-size: 10pt; line-height: 22.53999900817871px; margin: 5pt 0pt 5pt 36pt; text-indent: 36pt;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;In July 1654, despite the fact that France and England were at peace, Major Robert Sedgewick (1611-1656) of Boston and a military commande
2 CONC r of the militia of Massachusetts, with a force of 500 men attacked and took the fort La Tour Pentagouet and immediately began to besiege Port Royal.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;Doucet and his team of a hundred men resisted the attack for 16 days.&lt;/
2 CONC span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;However, faced with a superior opponent in number and armament, Doucet was finally forced to abandon the Port Royal Sedgewick and English.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-family: 'times new roman'
2 CONC ; font-size: 12pt; line-height: 24.53999900817871px; margin: 5pt 0pt 5pt 36pt; text-indent: 36pt;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Under the&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;agr
2 CONC eement to surrender, however, the settlers of Port Royal are&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;has the option of returning to France, or stay cultivate their&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;s
2 CONC pan&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;adopted country.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Several elected to stay.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt
2 CONC ;"&gt;The soldiers were forced to return to France in 1654, including&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Major Doucet.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-family: 'time
2 CONC s new roman'; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 22.53999900817871px; margin: 5pt 0pt 5pt 36pt; text-indent: 36pt;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Doucet and his wife were captured and returned to France, never to return to the new world.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span
2 CONC &gt;In France they disappeared from history.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 22.53999900817871px; margin: 5pt 0pt 5pt 36pt; text-indent: 36pt;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;The name o
2 CONC f the wife of Germain is uncertain, although some suggest it could genealogists Marie Bourgeois.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Although the number of records indicates Marie Bourgeois as the first wife of Germain Doucet and Marguerite's mothe
2 CONC r and Peter, Stephen White, genealogist renamed it contradicts.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;He says that Germain did, however, remarry before 1654. This second marriage could be the daughter of Guillaume Trahan (1611-1682), my grandfather, or the siste
2 CONC r of Jacques Bourgeois (1621-1701), my grandfather, and after the capitulation of Port Royal 16 August 1654, he left his brother, Jacques Bourgeois, as a hostage.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-
2 CONC size: 10pt; line-height: 22.53999900817871px; margin: 5pt 0pt 5pt 36pt; text-indent: 36pt;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Another theory connects Jacques Jacob Bourgeois as brother.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;It postulates that Germain Doucet was the fathe
2 CONC r of Marguerite-Louise Doucet and Marie Bourgeois Grandjehan was the mother.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Grandjehan Mary was the daughter of Nicolas and Marguerite Bourgeois Grandjehan.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;They are also listed a
2 CONC s the parents of Jacques Jacob Bourgeois.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;He took the surname of his mother or his son was with the unknown father.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 10pt; l
2 CONC ine-height: 22.53999900817871px; margin: 5pt 0pt 5pt 36pt; text-indent: 36pt;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;According to Stephen White of Acadian Studies Centre in Moncton, New Brunswick, his research, based on an exemption for the marriage of Claude Trahan Ann
2 CONC e LeBlanc, leads him to believe that the wife of Pierre Lejeune was a daughter of Germain Doucet, whose name is unknown.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;If this is true, then we must also conclude that Germain Doucet had at least two wives and no
2 CONC t one, since some of the descendants of his daughter, Marguerite, were tested for non - Native American haplogroup T2, but Jeanne Lejeune said Briart, which would also be his granddaughter after the dispensation had Amerindian haplogroup "A", indic
2 CONC ating that his mother and Marguerite Doucet were half-sisters with different mothers.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 22.53999900817871px; margin: 5pt 0pt 5pt 36pt;"&gt;&
2 CONC lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt; font-weight: bold;"&gt;Meaning of the name Doucet&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-family: 'times new roman'; font-size: 10pt; line-height: 22.53999900817871px; margin: 5p
2 CONC t 0pt 5pt 36pt;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Doucet is of French origin.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;It is a nickname for a gentle person mood.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Old French Doltz, dous 'sweet', 'pleasant', from Latin "dulcis".&lt;/span&gt;
