2 TITL The Deportation Period
1 _OID 460a336f-c876-46e0-8582-6350109cf7ed
1 _DSCR From The Girouard Family Website
2 CONT 
2 CONT www.girouard.org/cgi-bin/page.pl?file=deportation&amp;n=4
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;span style="font-family: arial,helvetica,sans-serif; font-size: small; color: #000000;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: small;"&gt;The Deportation period, 1755-1762&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt
2 CONC ;</line><line>&lt;p style="text-align: ;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="text-align: ;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: arial,helvetica,sans-serif; font-size: small; color: #000000;"&gt;The period between 1755 and 1762 was a ver
2 CONC y tragic time for Acadians, and the Girouard Family, for it was in those years that the British authorities decided to enforce a deportation order. Acadians were stripped of all their rights and placed in the holds of over-crowded ships bound for d
2 CONC estinations unknown. The traumatic events deeply scared the Acadians people. Memories of the terrors of the deportation and exile lasted for generations.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="text-align: ;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><
2 CONC line>&lt;p style="text-align: ;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: arial,helvetica,sans-serif; font-size: small; color: #000000;"&gt;After the fall of Beaus&amp;eacute;jour in the spring of 1755, events progressed rapidly toward deportation. The Brit
2 CONC ish authorities in Halifax continued to ban Acadians from using their guns and canoes and in the late summer the plan was put into motion. On Friday, September 5, 1755 Colonel John Winslow ordered that all males aged 10 years and up in the area wer
2 CONC e to gather in the Grand-Pr&amp;eacute; Church for an important message from His Excellency, Charles Lawrence, the Lieutenant-Governor of Nova Scotia. The decree that was read to the assembled and stated in part: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line
2 CONC >&lt;p style="text-align: ;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line /><line>&lt;table width="85%" border="1" cellspacing="2" cellpadding="0"&gt;</line><line>&lt;tbody&gt;</line><line>&lt;tr&gt;</line><line>&lt;td&gt;&lt;center&gt;&lt;span style="font-
2 CONC family: arial,helvetica,sans-serif; font-size: small; color: #000000;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;"Gentlemen, I have received from His Excellency&lt;a href="http://www.girouard.org/cgi-bin/page.pl?file=lawrence"&gt;&lt;span style="color: #000000;"&gt; Govern
2 CONC or Lawrence&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/a&gt; the King's commission which I have in my hand and by who's orders you are conveyed together to manifest to you His Majesty's final resolution to the French inhabitants of this his province of Nova Scotia who for a
2 CONC lmost half a century have had more indulgence granted them then any of his subjects in any part of his Dominions. What you have made of them you yourself best know."&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/center&gt;&lt;/td&gt;</line><line>&lt;/tr&gt;</lin
2 CONC e><line>&lt;/tbody&gt;</line><line>&lt;/table&gt;</line><line /><line>&lt;p style="text-align: ;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;span style="font-family: arial,helvetica,sans-serif; font-size: small; color: #000000;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;(quote
2 CONC d by John Winslow in : &lt;em&gt;Acadie; Esquisse d'un parcours; Sketches of a Journey. p.52)&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="text-align: ;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="text-align: ;"&gt;&lt;span s
2 CONC tyle="font-family: arial,helvetica,sans-serif; font-size: small; color: #000000;"&gt;The promise made to the Acadians that families were not to be divided during the process were not kept. Lawrence's motives were not at all sympathetic to the Acadi
2 CONC an's plight as evidenced in a letter he sent to Colonel Robert Monckton: " I would have you not wait for the wives and the children coming in but ship off the men without them.". &lt;span&gt;(quoted in Naomi Griffiths, "&lt;em&gt;The Acadian Deport
2 CONC ation; Causes and Development,&lt;/em&gt;" Ph.D thesis, p. 176)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="text-align: ;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="text-align: ;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: arial,helvet
2 CONC ica,sans-serif; font-size: small; color: #000000;"&gt;After Acadians had boarded the ships orders were given to destroy homes and to round up livestock. In this way Acadians who had escaped, or were planning to, would not have their former homes t
2 CONC o run to nor would they have their cattle and sheep to rely on. Today, the very site of the first deportation, at Grand-Pr&amp;eacute; is marked by a cross.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="text-align: ;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-fami
2 CONC ly: arial,helvetica,sans-serif; font-size: small; color: #000000;"&gt; In total over ten thousand Acadians were either deported, imprisoned or escaped, or hid in the wooded hillsides close by. For those who did run ingenious ways were contrived b
2 CONC y the authorities to force them out of hiding. Winslow had warned Acadians that "if within 2 days the absent ones are not delivered up, military execution would be immediately visited upon the next of kin." &lt;span&gt;(quoted in Dudley LeBlanc, &l
2 CONC t;em&gt;The Acadian Miracle, &lt;/em&gt;p.174)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
