1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p style="margin-: 0.4em; margin-bottom: 0.5em; line-height: 19.1875px; font-family: sans-serif; font-size: 13px;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Pepin&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;(also&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Peppin&lt;/strong&gt;,&amp
2 CONC ;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Pipin&lt;/strong&gt;, or&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Pippin&lt;/strong&gt;)&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;of Landen&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;(c. 580 &amp;ndash; 27 February 640), also called&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;the Elder&lt;/strong&gt;&am
2 CONC p;nbsp;or&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;the Old&lt;/strong&gt;, was the&amp;nbsp;Mayor of the Palace&amp;nbsp;of&amp;nbsp;Austrasia&amp;nbsp;under the&amp;nbsp;Merovingianking&amp;nbsp;Dagobert I&amp;nbsp;from 623 to 629. He was also the mayor for&amp;nbs
2 CONC p;Sigebert III&amp;nbsp;from 639 until his own death.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-: 0.4em; margin-bottom: 0.5em; line-height: 19.1875px; font-family: sans-serif; font-size: 13px;"&gt;Pepin's father is named Carloman by the&amp;nbsp;&l
2 CONC t;em&gt;Chronicle of Fredegar,&lt;/em&gt;&amp;nbsp;the chief source for his life. His byname comes from his probable birthplace:&amp;nbsp;Landen, modern&amp;nbsp;Belgium. He is sometimes called&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Pepin I&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp
2 CONC ;and his other nicknames (Elder and Old) come from his position at the head of the family called the&amp;nbsp;Pippinids&amp;nbsp;after him. Through the marriage of his daughter&amp;nbsp;Begga&amp;nbsp;to&amp;nbsp;Ansegisel, a son of&amp;nbsp;Arnul
2 CONC f of Metz, the clans of the&amp;nbsp;Pippinids&amp;nbsp;and the Arnulfings were united, giving rise to a family which would eventually rule the&amp;nbsp;Franks&amp;nbsp;as theCarolingians.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-: 0.4em; margin-b
2 CONC ottom: 0.5em; line-height: 19.1875px; font-family: sans-serif; font-size: 13px;"&gt;In 613, several leading magnates of Austrasia and&amp;nbsp;Burgundy&amp;nbsp;abandoned&amp;nbsp;Brunhilda, the great-grandmother and regent of their king,&amp;nbsp;
2 CONC Sigebert II, and turned to&amp;nbsp;Chlothar II&amp;nbsp;of&amp;nbsp;Neustria&amp;nbsp;for support, promising not to rise in defense of the queen-regent and recognizing Chlothar as rightful regent and guardian of the young king. Chief among these l
2 CONC eading men wereWarnachar II,&amp;nbsp;Rado,&amp;nbsp;Arnulf, and Pepin. The latter two were described by&amp;nbsp;Fredegar&amp;nbsp;as the "two most powerful barons of Austrasia" and they made some agreement with Chlothar atAndernach. However, whil
2 CONC e Rado was confirmed as mayor in Austrasia and Warnachar in Burgundy, Pepin did not receive his reward until 623, when he was appointed mayor in Austrasia after Chlothar made his young son Dagobert king there. Arnulf, his lifelong friend, was appoi
2 CONC nted adviser to the new king alongside him.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-: 0.4em; margin-bottom: 0.5em; line-height: 19.1875px; font-family: sans-serif; font-size: 13px;"&gt;Pepin was praised by his contemporaries for his good governme
2 CONC nt and wise counsel. Though some enemies tried to turn the king against him, their plots were foiled and Pepin remained on good terms with the king until 629, when, for reasons unknown, he retired (or was retired) to his estates, where he remaine
2 CONC d for the next decade, until Dagobert's death.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-: 0.4em; margin-bottom: 0.5em; line-height: 19.1875px; font-family: sans-serif; font-size: 13px;"&gt;On his death, Pepin came out of retirement to take on th
2 CONC e mayoralty in Austrasia for the heir Sigebert III and to oversee the distribution of the treasury between Sigebert and his brother,&amp;nbsp;Clovis II, and his stepmother&amp;nbsp;Nanthild, who was ruling on Clovis' behalf in Neustria and Burgundy
2 CONC . Sigebert's share of the inheritance was amicably surrendered, partly because of the friendship between Pepin and the Burgundian mayor of the palace,&amp;nbsp;Aega. Pepin and Arnulf's successor as chief counselor to the king,&amp;nbsp;Cunibert, Bi
2 CONC shop of Cologne, received the treasure at&amp;nbsp;Compi&amp;egrave;gne&amp;nbsp;and brought it back to&amp;nbsp;Metz. Not long after, both Pepin and Aega died. He was so popular in Austrasia that, though he was never&amp;nbsp;canonized, he was lis
2 CONC ted as a saint in some&amp;nbsp;martyrologies. His&amp;nbsp;feast day&amp;nbsp;was 21 February.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-: 0.4em; margin-bottom: 0.5em; line-height: 19.1875px; font-family: sans-serif; font-size: 13px;"&gt;He left t
2 CONC wo daughters and two sons by his equally famous wife,&amp;nbsp;Itta:&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;ul style="line-height: 19.1875px; margin-: 1.6em; margin-bottom: 0px; padding: 0px; list-style-image: url(data:image/png; base64,ivborw0kggoaaaansuheugaa
2 CONC aauaaaanaqmaaabb8jblaaaablbmvex///8auow5qsojaaaaaxrstlmaqobyzgaaabnjrefucb1jyeabbqw/wlcagqeagpidyt0ivcsaaaaasuvork5cyii=); font-family: sans-serif; font-size: 13px;"&gt;</line><line>&lt;li style="margin-bottom: 0.1em;"&gt;Begga, married the aforeme
2 CONC ntioned&amp;nbsp;Ansegisel&amp;nbsp;and later canonized&lt;/li&gt;</line><line>&lt;li style="margin-bottom: 0.1em;"&gt;Grimoald, later mayor of the palace like his father&lt;/li&gt;</line><line>&lt;li style="margin-bottom: 0.1em;"&gt;Bavo&amp;nbsp;
2 CONC (or Allowin), became a hermit and later canonized&lt;/li&gt;</line><line>&lt;li style="margin-bottom: 0.1em;"&gt;Gertrude, entered the convent of&amp;nbsp;Nivelles&amp;nbsp;founded by her mother, also later canonized&lt;/li&gt;</line><line>&lt;/ul&
2 CONC gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
