2 TITL The Melancon&#39;s in Acadia:
1 _OID 4eea679b-5094-4c87-8bec-abd291f7e7af
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;The Melancon&amp;#39;s in Acadia: Many a writer has tried to explain the  origins of this family. It was generally believed that the Melancon Family were  of Scottish origin, but this assumption wa
2 CONC s based on a historic mistake. There  is no document that demonstrates that the Melancons who came to Acadia were of  Scottish decent, in fact to the contrary there is proof that they were of  English decent. The&amp;nbsp;three Melancon brothers, P
2 CONC ierre, dit Laverdure, a  stonemason, born in 1632, the spouse of Marie Marguerite Muis d&amp;#39;Entremont, the  daughter of the Lord of Pomcoup, Philippe Mius d&amp;#39; Entremont,&amp;nbsp;his brother  Charles, born in 1643, the husband of Mari
2 CONC e Dugas, the daughter of Armourer,  Abraham Dugas and Marguerite Doucet of Port Royal, and his brother Jean, born in  1651, arrived in Port Royal accompanied by their parents aboard the ship &amp;quot;The  Satisfaction&amp;quot; under the command o
2 CONC f Captain Butler, with Sir Thomas Temple, the  new English Governor of Acadia, which arrived in 1657. During almost a century,  just until the deportation of the Acadians in 1755, the settlement was the place  of residence of Charles Melanson and h
2 CONC is wife Marie Dugas, and their descendents  and associates. &amp;quot;The Melancon Settlement&amp;quot; is one of the principle Canadian  archeological sites illustrating the way of rural life in Acadia in the 17th and  18th centuries. It is a mirr
2 CONC or of the various aspects of the daily life, their  homes, material culture and their prosperity. 1755:THE DEPORTATION: In the  Autumn of 1755, 1660 Acadians of the region of Port Royal embarked on ships  toward exile. Charles and Ambroise Melanso
2 CONC n took refuge in Quebec, while Jean  Melanson and his family were refugees in France with a group of Acadians from  Cape Sable. Others were deported to Maryland and other States. The Melanson like  hundreds of other Acadians were imprisoned in th
2 CONC e military fortresses which were  neither more or less than concentration camps. At gun point, English soldiers  forced them to work for them. The Melansons were imprisoned at Fort Cumberland  and Fort Edward. Today, this large Acadian family wit
2 CONC h the name of  Melanson-Melancon-Malanson-Malonson can be found in all of Canada, the United  States and in France. These Acadians all descendents of Pierre and Charles  Melanson arrived in Acadia during 3 1/2 centruies. The majority of Charles&amp
2 CONC ;#39;  descendents returned to the former Acadia, resettling in Nova Scotia and  Southern New Brunswick, and in the USA in the States of Maine and Massachusetts,  most continuing to spell their name Melanson, but some have changed to Malanson  an
2 CONC d Malonson. The majority of Pierre&amp;#39;s descendents however did not return,  resettling in Louisiana, Quebec, and Northern New Brunswick,in the Bathurst  region. It is the branch of the family that today predominately spells their  name MELANC
2 CONC ON. The only descendent of Pierre known to have returned to Nova  Scotia was Charles Marie who settled in Pomquet after returning with his mother  from exile in France. It is unknown why Pierre, Charles and Jean&amp;nbsp;Melancon took a  variatio
2 CONC n of&amp;nbsp;their mother&amp;#39;s maiden name, Priscilla Mallanson, instead of their  Father, Pierre Laverdure. Pierre Laverdure was born and Baptized in France. He  fled France for England after the fall of LaRochelle in 1628. There he met  Pri
2 CONC scilla Mallanson and fell in love. Pierre Laverdure married Priscilla  Mallanson in 1630 in England. They departed England in 1657 in route to Acadie.  They went through Boston in route to Fort St. John where they stopped. After the  Treaty of Bred
2 CONC a in 1667, Pierre, Priscilla and son, Jean fled to Boston to  escape the wrath of Papists in Fort St. John. His other two sons, Pierre and  Charles were left behind with their French wives and families. Pierre Laverdure  died of a heart attack in c
2 CONC 1677 in Boston as the result of his son, Jean&amp;#39;s  illegal slave trading of Indians. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
