2 CONC 9 or 685, and &lt;strong&gt;Begga&lt;/strong&gt; died in 693.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Pepin d&amp;rsquo;Heristal II&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;strong&gt;&amp;ldquo;the Fat&amp;rdquo;, &lt;/strong&gt;son of&lt;strong&gt; Ansegisel &lt;/strong&gt;an
2 CONC d &lt;strong&gt;Begga.&lt;/strong&gt; was born in 635. He inherited immense landholdings from his parents and was Mayor of the Palace. He married first Alpaida. Alpaida died in 705. &lt;strong&gt;Pepin d&amp;rsquo;Heristal&lt;/strong&gt; then marri
2 CONC ed Plecturd. &lt;strong&gt;Pepin d&amp;rsquo;Heristal II &lt;/strong&gt;also had a concubine named&lt;strong&gt; Chalpaida. &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Pepin d&amp;rsquo;Heristal II&lt;/strong&gt; died December 16, 714. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;stro
2 CONC ng&gt;Charles Martel, &lt;/strong&gt;son of &lt;strong&gt;Pepin d&amp;rsquo;Heristal II&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;Chalpaida,&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt; &lt;/strong&gt;was born in 688. After the death of his father, Plecturd imprisoned him
2 CONC , but he escaped in 715 and was proclaimed Mayor of the Palace by the Austrasians. A war between the Austrasians and the Frankish Kingdom of Neustria followed, and at the end of the war, &lt;strong&gt;Charles Martel&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt; &lt
2 CONC ;/strong&gt;was undisputed ruler of all the Franks. &lt;strong&gt;Charles Martel&amp;rsquo;s&lt;/strong&gt; greatest achievements were his defeats of the Muslims from Spain, who invaded France in 732 and again in 739. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&
2 CONC gt;Charles Martel&lt;/strong&gt; married first &lt;strong&gt;Rotrude de Treves&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;, &lt;/strong&gt;who died in 724. &lt;strong&gt;Pepin III&lt;/strong&gt; (or Pepin the Short) and Carolman were their sons. He married secon
2 CONC d Sunnichild, and he died on October 22, 741, at Quierzy on the Oise River. After his death, the kingdom was divided between his two sons, Carolman and &lt;strong&gt;Pepin the Short&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Pepin III&lt;/stro
2 CONC ng&gt; (or Pepin the Short), son of &lt;strong&gt;Charles Martel&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;Rotrude de Treves,&lt;/strong&gt; was born in 714 or 715. In 743 he married Bertrada II of Laon (died 783), and later he married &lt;strong&gt;Gersvin
2 CONC d&lt;/strong&gt;. In 751, &lt;strong&gt;Pepin III&lt;/strong&gt; deposed Childeric III and became the first king of the Merovingian Dynasty. Pope Stephen II crowned &lt;strong&gt;Pepin III&lt;/strong&gt; in 754, and &lt;strong&gt;Pepin&amp;rsquo;s&
2 CONC lt;/strong&gt; army protected the Pope from the Lombards. &lt;strong&gt;Pepin&lt;/strong&gt; donated territory to the Pope, which became the foundation of the Papal States. Among the children of &lt;strong&gt;Pepin III&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong
2 CONC &gt;Gersvind&lt;/strong&gt; was &lt;strong&gt;Charlemagne&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;. Pepin III&lt;/strong&gt; died on September 18, 768, and was buried in the Basilica of St. Dennis.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Charlemagne, King of &lt;/stro
2 CONC ng&gt;&lt;strong&gt;France&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Charlemagne, &lt;/strong&gt;son of&lt;strong&gt; Pepin III &lt;/strong&gt;and &lt;strong&gt;Gersvind,&lt;/strong&gt; was born April 2, 742, in Ingolheim. He became King of th
2 CONC e Franks in 768, and Emperor of the Romans in 800. He built a kingdom that included almost all of Western and Central Europe through military action against the Saxons, Lombards, and Moors, and through alliances with the Pope. &lt;strong&gt;Charlem
2 CONC agne&lt;/strong&gt; instituted administrative, educational, economic, legal, and cultural reforms in his empire. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;He married first &lt;strong&gt;Hildegard&lt;/strong&gt; (757-782) in 771 at Aix-La-Chapelle (Aachen), and among thei
2 CONC r children was &lt;strong&gt;Pepin I&lt;/strong&gt;, King of Italy &lt;span style="color: red"&gt;(See Chapter 10)&lt;/span&gt;. He married second Fastrada of East Frank in 783. He married third Liutgard of Alemannic in 794, and fourth Gersuinda o
2 CONC f Saxon. Some say that Charlemagne had five wives &amp;ndash; Himiltrude, Desiderata, &lt;strong&gt;Hildegarde&lt;/strong&gt;, Fastrada, and Luitgarde. &lt;strong&gt;Louis I&lt;/strong&gt;, the Pious, was &lt;strong&gt;Charlemagne&lt;/strong&gt;&lt
2 CONC ;strong&gt;&amp;rsquo;s&lt;/strong&gt; heir. &lt;strong&gt;Charlemagne&lt;/strong&gt; died on January 28, 814, in Aachen.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Children of Charlemagne were: &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Pepin the Hunchback&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Louis I
2 CONC &lt;/strong&gt;, the Pious&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Charles, King of Neustria&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Pepin, King of Italy&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Lothar&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;five daughters &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;NOTE: In another place, Familysearch.com says that Pharamond
2 CONC , above, was father of Clodio of Westphalia. Clodio was father of Merovich, who died in France in 458. Merovich was the father of Cloderic, who died in Tournai, France, in 481. Cloderic was the father of Clovis the Riparian, who died in Paris, Fran
2 CONC ce, in 511. Clovis the Riparian was father of Childebert, who died in Cologne, Germany. Childebert was father of Sigebert the Lame , who died in Cologne in 509. Sigebert the Lame was father of Clodereic the Parricide, who also died in Cologne in 50
2 CONC 9. Cloderic the Parricide was father of Munderic, who also died in 509. Munderic was the father of Mummolin. Mummolin was the father of Bodogisel, born before 567. Bodogisel was the father of Arnoul, and Arnoul was the father of Ansegisal. Beginnin
2 CONC g with Cloderic, this is the line described &lt;span style="color: red"&gt;in Chapter 24&lt;/span&gt;. So it appears that there is a divergence of opinion about the lineage from Pharmond to Ansegisal. &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div
2 CONC &gt;&lt;!-- SN:TREESUI06 --&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
