1 _OID 698b6870-6c86-49b1-835b-974b63908ad0
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;It is believed that Charles and Pierre Melanson, Protestants who may have emigrated from France to Scotland, came to Acadie with Sir Thomas Temple, aronet, governor of Acadia; b. January&amp;nbsp;1613/14 at Stow
2 CONC e, Buckinghamshire, England; d.&amp;nbsp;27&amp;nbsp;March&amp;nbsp;1674 at Ealing, Middlesex. He was the second son of Sir&amp;nbsp;John Temple of Stanton Bury and his first wife Dorothy, daughter of Edmund Lee, and a grandson of Sir&amp;nbsp;Thom
2 CONC as Temple, of Stowe.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Nothing is recorded of Thomas Temple until the year 1656 when he entered into partnership with Col.&amp;nbsp;William &lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;Crowne&lt;/span&gt; to buy most of &lt;span class
2 CONC ="SmallCaps"&gt;Charles de Saint&lt;/span&gt;-&amp;Eacute;tienne de La&amp;nbsp;Tour&amp;rsquo;s interest in Nova Scotia. Lord&amp;nbsp;Fiennes, an uncle of Thomas Temple and a member of Cromwell&amp;rsquo;s council, advised his nephew, who was sus
2 CONC pected of Royalist leanings, to take this opportunity to leave England. On 29&amp;nbsp;May&amp;nbsp;1656, the Council of State approved the joint petition of La&amp;nbsp;Tour, Temple, and Crowne, and ordered a patent to be granted; on 14&amp;nbsp;J
2 CONC uly, articles of agreement were drawn up and a month later on 9 or 10&amp;nbsp;August, the patent was granted. La&amp;nbsp;Tour gave up his title to Temple and Crowne on 20&amp;nbsp;Sept.&amp;nbsp;1656, in return for which Temple undertook to pay t
2 CONC he cost of the English troops which had earlier captured the fort on the Saint John River [&lt;em&gt;see&lt;/em&gt; &lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;Sedgwick&lt;/span&gt;]&amp;nbsp;&amp;ndash; &amp;pound;1,800 according to Temple&amp;rsquo;s petitio
2 CONC n of 1660&amp;nbsp;&amp;ndash; and Crowne and Temple, La&amp;nbsp;Tour&amp;rsquo;s debt to Maj.-Gen. Edward Gibbons of Boston.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;At the same time, Cromwell ordered the commander of Acadia, Leverett, to surrender the for
2 CONC ts of Saint John and &amp;ldquo;Pentacoit&amp;rdquo; (Pentagouet) to Temple. This Leverett did 1&amp;nbsp;May&amp;nbsp;1657 after Temple arrived with a party of settlers.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;On 12&amp;nbsp;Sept.&amp;nbsp;1657 an agreemen
2 CONC t was made between Temple and Crowne for a division of their property. Temple&amp;rsquo;s share extended from what is now Lunenburg in Nova Scotia to the River St.&amp;nbsp;George in Maine, including the whole coast of the Bay of Fundy (Baie Fran&a
2 CONC mp;ccedil;aise) on both sides and a hundred leagues inland. Crowne and Temple had many disputes over their property in Nova Scotia. In 1659, Col.&amp;nbsp;Crowne leased his share of the grant to Thomas Temple for four years. The rent from Temple wa
2 CONC s secured by a bond of &amp;pound;440. Another challenge to Temple&amp;rsquo;s assertion of authority over Acadia was &lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;Alexander Le&amp;nbsp;Borgne&lt;/span&gt; de Belle-Isle&amp;rsquo;s seizure of La&amp;nbsp;H&amp;egr
2 CONC ave;ve (now La&amp;nbsp;Have) in May&amp;nbsp;1658. Belle-Isle acted under his father&amp;rsquo;s concession in Acadia from the Compagnie de la Nouvelle-France. He was captured by Temple, taken to Boston, and there held prisoner for several years.&
2 CONC lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;In a letter to Lord&amp;nbsp;Fiennes dated 6&amp;nbsp;Sept.&amp;nbsp;1659 pleading for a new patent to Nova Scotia &amp;ldquo;with his name only on it,&amp;rdquo; Temple describes some of his work in this colony. He s
2 CONC ays he repaired the fort at Saint John as much as he was able, built a trading house 150 miles up the river (this refers to Jemseg although the distance is incorrect), and built another in the bottom of the bay [of Fundy]. After the restoration o
2 CONC f Charles&amp;nbsp;II in 1660 petitions for a grant of Acadia were placed before the king by Thomas Elliott, a groom of the bed-chamber, &lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;Sir&amp;nbsp;Lewis Kirke&lt;/span&gt;, the heirs of &lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt
2 CONC ;Sir&amp;nbsp;William&lt;/span&gt; Alexander, the French ambassador on behalf of Le&amp;nbsp;Borgne, and others. In 1662 Temple was created a baronet of Nova Scotia and given the governorship for which he paid Elliott &amp;pound;600 per year.&lt;/p
2 CONC &gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Temple&amp;rsquo;s possession of Acadia was short-lived. By the Treaty of Breda in 1667, Charles&amp;nbsp;II ceded Acadia to Louis&amp;nbsp;XIV and ordered Temple on 31&amp;nbsp;Dec.&amp;nbsp;1667 to surrender the five Aca
2 CONC dian forts to Morillon Du&amp;nbsp;Bourg. In October&amp;nbsp;1668, Temple, having been told in August to &amp;ldquo;forbear delivery&amp;rdquo; until further notice, showed Du&amp;nbsp;Bourg the king&amp;rsquo;s order and raised several objection
2 CONC s to immediate surrender. Du&amp;nbsp;Bourg accepted Temple&amp;rsquo;s argument that the island of St.&amp;nbsp;Christopher was to be surrendered to the English before Acadia was returned to the French and wrote Le&amp;nbsp;Borgne de Belle-Isle ac
2 CONC cordingly. Charles&amp;nbsp;II renewed his command to Temple to surrender Acadia on 8&amp;nbsp;March&amp;nbsp;1668/69 and 6&amp;nbsp;Aug.&amp;nbsp;1669. The transfer was effected in the summer of 1670 by Temple&amp;rsquo;s deputy-governor, Capt.&am
2 CONC p;nbsp;Richard &lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;Walker&lt;/span&gt;, after Temple and &lt;span class="SmallCaps"&gt;Andign&amp;eacute;&lt;/span&gt; de Grandfontaine had signed an agreement in Boston.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;During the uncertai
2 CONC n period from 1667 to 1670 Temple lived in Boston and continued to petition the king for recompense for his expenses and losses in Nova Scotia. He claimed that Acadia yielded only &amp;pound;900, of which (for seven years) he had to pay Elliott &am
2 CONC p;pound;600 and Boston merchants &amp;pound;180 to remit it to England, leaving him an annual income of &amp;pound;120, with which he had &amp;ldquo;supported our pigmy war with the French, and preserved the King&amp;rsquo;s country.&amp;nbsp;.&amp
2 CONC ;nbsp;.&amp;nbsp;.&amp;rdquo; It is unlikely that he received the &amp;pound;16,000 he requested to cover his losses and debts of &amp;pound;7,000.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;Temple seems to have prospered after he settled in Boston. He had beg
2 CONC un to acquire property there while still living in Nova Scotia and he was very active in commerce, particularly real estate. He was prominent among those who attempted to develop some of the islands in Boston Harbour.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt
2 CONC ;Temple had moved to London shortly before his death on 27&amp;nbsp;March&amp;nbsp;1674. He was buried at Ealing, Middlesex. His will bequeathed the bulk of his estate to his nephew, John Nelson*, of Boston.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp
2 CONC ;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;In collaboration with Huia Ryder, &amp;ldquo;TEMPLE, SIR THOMAS,&amp;rdquo; in &lt;em&gt;Dictionary of Canadian Biography&lt;/em&gt;, vol. 1, University of Toronto/Universit&amp;eacute; Laval, 2003&amp;ndash;, acces
2 CONC sed April 12, 2015, http://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/temple_thomas_1E.html.&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
