2 TITL Chateau Coucy built by Enguerrand III de Coucy
1 _OID 6af5c346-9189-49c4-99ce-01cee9830a89
1 _DSCR Source - wikipedia
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;Ch&amp;acirc;teau de Coucy&lt;/strong&gt; is a French castle in the commune of Coucy-le-Ch&amp;acirc;teau-Auffrique, in the &lt;em&gt;d&amp;eacute;partement&lt;/em&gt; of Aisne, built in the 13
2 CONC th century and renovated by Viollet-le-Duc in the 19th. In 1917, the German army dynamited the keep and the four towers using 28 tons of explosives.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;&amp;quot;King not I, nor prince nor duke nor count either, but I am the lord  o
2 CONC f Coucy!&amp;quot; Such was the proud motto adopted by Enguerrand III, feudal  lord of Coucy, Marle, Saint-Gobain and F&amp;egrave;re. To underline his influence  and show off his wealth, this noble spent colossal sums of money  building himsel
2 CONC f a castle to match his motto. At a time when the French  monarchy was on the lookout to extend its power, Coucy was itself almost  a challenge. Its immense keep was some 20 metres (60&amp;nbsp;ft) taller than  the contemporary Louvre royal castl
2 CONC e in a Paris just 100&amp;nbsp;km away (60  miles). The great tower at Coucy was a knight&amp;#39;s dream, an almost  timeless symbol of power for seven hundred years until World War I, a  cold March night in 1917.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;During its hey
2 CONC day it was famous for the size of its central tower and  the pride of its lords, who adopted the staunchly independent rhyme:  &amp;quot;Roi ne suis, ne Prince ne Duc ne Comte aussi; Je suis le sire de Coucy&amp;quot;  (&amp;quot;I am not a King, n
2 CONC or Prince nor Duke nor Count; I am the Lord of  Coucy&amp;quot;).&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The castle was constructed in the 1220s by Enguerrand III de Coucy. The castle proper occupies the tip of a bluff or &lt;em&gt;falaise&lt;/em&gt;.  It forms an irr
2 CONC egular trapezoid of 92 x 35 x 50 x 80 m. At the four  corners are cylindrical towers 20 m in diameter (originally 40 m in  height). Between two towers on the line of approach was the massive &lt;em&gt;donjon&lt;/em&gt; (keep).  The donjon was the l
2 CONC argest in Europe, measuring 35 meters wide and 55  meters tall. The smaller towers surrounding the court were as big as the  donjons being built at that time by the French monarchy. The rest of  the bluff is covered by the lower court of the castle
2 CONC , and the small  town.&lt;sup&gt;&lt;span&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;1&lt;span&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Coucy was occupied in September 1914 by German troops during World  War I. It became a military outpost and was frequented by Ger
2 CONC man  dignitaries, including Kaiser Wilhelm II himself.&lt;sup style="white-space: nowrap"&gt;[&lt;em&gt;citation needed&lt;/em&gt;]&lt;/sup&gt;  In March 1917 the German army destroyed the keep and the 4 towers. It  is not known whether this act ha
2 CONC d some military purpose or it was merely  an act of barbarism. The destruction caused so much public outrage that  in April 1917 the ruins were declared &amp;quot;a memorial to barbarity&amp;quot;. War  reparations were used to clear the towers an
2 CONC d to consolidate the walls  but the ruins of the keep were left in place.&lt;sup&gt;&lt;span&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;2&lt;span&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;One of its lords, Enguerrand VII de Coucy (1340&amp;ndash;1397) is the subjec
2 CONC t of historian Barbara Tuchman&amp;#39;s study of the fourteenth century, &lt;em&gt;A Distant Mirror&lt;/em&gt;. It also features extensively in British author Anthony Price&amp;#39;s 1982 crime/espionage novel &lt;em&gt;The Old Vengeful&lt;/em&gt;
2 CONC .&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Ch&amp;acirc;teau de Coucy has been listed as a &lt;em&gt;monument historique&lt;/em&gt; by the French Ministry of Culture since 1862, and is managed by the &lt;em&gt;Centre des monuments nationaux&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;spa
2 CONC n&gt;See also&lt;/span&gt; &lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;List of castles in France&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt; &lt;span&gt;References&lt;/span&gt; &lt;div&gt; &lt;ol&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;^&lt;/strong&gt; Charles-Laurent Salch, &lt;em&gt;Dictionnaire des ch&a
2 CONC mp;acirc;teaux et des fortifications du moyen-&amp;acirc;ge en France&lt;/em&gt; . Publisher: Editions Publitotal, Strasbourg (France); 1979.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;^&lt;/strong&gt; the coucy castle&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ol&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt
2 CONC ;span&gt;External links&lt;/span&gt; &lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;A description of the castle by Viollet-le-Duc &lt;span style="font-size: 0.95em; font-weight: bold; color: #555555"&gt;(French)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Centre des monuments nationau
2 CONC x website&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Ministry of Culture database entry for Ch&amp;acirc;teau de Coucy &lt;span style="font-size: 0.95em; font-weight: bold; color: #555555"&gt;(French)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Ministry of Culture photos&lt;/li&gt;
2 CONC &lt;li&gt;Collection of old postcards from Coucy &lt;span style="font-size: 0.95em; font-weight: bold; color: #555555"&gt;(French)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;   &amp;nbsp; &lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt; &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;br&gt
2 CONC ;        &lt;div&gt; Retrieved from &amp;quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ch%C3%A2teau_de_Coucy&amp;quot;&lt;/div&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
