2 TITL history on Melancon
1 _OID 7e7baf9e-4f3a-4d86-b886-b1d30d3c567e
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line> &amp;lt;iframe src=&amp;quot;http://view.atdmt.com/iaction/ancestry_canada_universal/v3/atc1.-lib-TinyMce_2_1_0-blank_htm&amp;quot; width=&amp;quot;1&amp;quot; height=&amp;quot;1&amp;quot; frameborder=&amp;quot;0&amp;qu
2 CONC ot; scrolling=&amp;quot;No&amp;quot; marginheight=&amp;quot;0&amp;quot; marginwidth=&amp;quot;0&amp;quot; topmargin=&amp;quot;0&amp;quot; leftmargin=&amp;quot;0&amp;quot;&amp;gt;&amp;lt;/iframe&amp;gt;     &lt;!-- SN:TREESUI04 --&gt; &lt;p&gt;Accor
2 CONC ding to Wikipedia article titled &amp;quot;History of the Acadians&amp;quot;, it states&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt; &amp;quot;By 1631, the English agreed to return Nova Scotia to the French crown and the Scottish settlement was dismantled. One Scotsman rema
2 CONC ined, married a French settler, and the name, &lt;em&gt;Melanson&lt;/em&gt; (also Melan&amp;ccedil;on), has since become an Acadian family name.&amp;quot;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Could Pierre Laverdure have been this Scotsman???  Research indicates tha
2 CONC t he took his wife&amp;#39;s maiden name (Priscilla Mallinson)rather than she taking his name which is most definitely the norm.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Also, in the Wikipedia article &amp;quot;Cajun&amp;quot; it states&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;quot;Many C
2 CONC ajuns also have ancestors who were not French. Many of the original settlers in French Acadia were actually English, for example the Melansons (originally Mallinson).&amp;quot;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
