2 TITL Pierre Laverdure
1 _OID 834b7f9e-31dc-4d9c-8f5f-0ba89fce76ca
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>It would appear that it was the result of the fall of La Rochelle, France&lt;br&gt;in 1628, when the death-blow was delivered to the Calvinists in France,&lt;br&gt;that Pierre (a French Hugenot) went to Yorkshire, Englan
2 CONC d, where he&lt;br&gt;married, around 1630-1631, Priscilla Malleson.&amp;nbsp; They had at least three&lt;br&gt;sons, all born in England, two of whom were Carles and Pierre Melanson. on&lt;br&gt;an Acadian census it shows Pierre born in 1632 or 163
2 CONC 3.&amp;nbsp; In the spring of&lt;br&gt;1657, the family embarked from England on board the vessel Satisfaction,&lt;br&gt;under the command of Capt. Peter Butler, forming part of the company he&lt;br&gt;had been named governor after its capture by t
2 CONC he English.&amp;nbsp; Having first&lt;br&gt;stopped in Boston, it appears that the Melanson family stopped at Fort St.&lt;br&gt;John.&amp;nbsp; After the Treaty of Breda, under which in 1677 England ceded Acadia&lt;br&gt;to France, Pierre Laverdure
2 CONC , with his family, went to seek refuge under&lt;br&gt;the Protestant government at Boston in order to escape from the wrath of&lt;br&gt;his countrymen Papists, at Johns fort and thereabouts. While moving to&lt;br&gt;Boston himself, he left behind i
2 CONC n Acadia his two sons, Pierre and Charles,&lt;br&gt;with their young families.&amp;nbsp; Pierre Laverdure died in Boston about 1676.&lt;br&gt;Priscilla married for a second time, Capt William Wright, April 8, 1680.&lt;br&gt;She died in Boston abou
2 CONC t 1691.&amp;nbsp; The two sons left in Acadia used their&lt;br&gt;mother&amp;#39;s maiden name, perhaps to avoid any problems with the French&lt;br&gt;Catholics.</line></content></metadataxml>
