2 TITL Cheniere Caminada--Buried At Sea-clement bouzigard,noces terrebonne,daize cheramie
1 _OID 87673b7e-e55a-4c67-8547-b9eb2e710abb
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;From the book "Cheniere Caminada--Buried At Sea" by Dale P Rogers (1981) - Daize Cheramie stated that after the hurricane the silver bell was found partly buried in the sand.&amp;nbsp; Mr. Clement Bouziga buil
2 CONC t a low tower for it of 6" x 6" timbers and it was rung each Sunday at 7 AM just as it had been rung for mass.&amp;nbsp; There were possibly 15 surviving families staying at Cheniere for lack of any place to go.&amp;nbsp; The bell was in easy reac
2 CONC h for everyone so children would pass near it and ring it then run off.&amp;nbsp; One day Nocess Terrebonne was going to cut poles with a hatchet and as he passed near the bell he gave it a solid hit with the hatchet and left a cut on the bell.&amp
2 CONC ;nbsp; He felt very bad about the incident and remembered it clearly in later years.&amp;nbsp; However, the bell was now marked and it could be identified.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;...It (the bell) was found on the beach partially buried by t
2 CONC wo brothers, the sons of Etienne Perrin and Eve Victorine Melancon.&amp;nbsp; The bell was cared for by this family and at some point it was installed on a low wooden stand as described earlier. ...Mr. Perrin had set the bell up on posts and when h
2 CONC e died his brother Marius cared for the bell.&amp;nbsp; Survivors from Westwego then arrived with papers of possession and took the bell.&amp;nbsp; Mr. Daize Cheramie stated that Mr. Clement Bouzigard brought a law suit and spent $4,000 to have th
2 CONC e bell returned.&amp;nbsp; It was then stolen off of a boat in Gretna and buried in the Westwego cemetery.&amp;nbsp; Mr. Theodore Collins claimed to have worked with Mr. Bouzigard who filed the suit.&amp;nbsp; As a child he had made several marks i
2 CONC nside the bell with a small ax and he indetified the bell in that way.&amp;nbsp; But the suit did not succeed as the bell stayed buried in Westwego for years.&amp;nbsp; Around 1916, land was donated for a new church on Grand Isle only 3 or 4 mile
2 CONC s from Cheniere Caminada...the local Sheriff made the right inquiries to locate the bell.&amp;nbsp; The protectors of the bell went to the Gretna cemetery and dug up the bell. It made a well guarded trip to Grand Isle by boat and was installed in t
2 CONC he waiting church tower.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;In 1918 the church was dedicated.&amp;nbsp; so 25 years later after the destruction of Cheniere Caminada, the bell came home and so did Father F. J. Grimaud who had survived the storm.&amp;nbs
2 CONC p; He arrived not knowing that the original bell had been returned.&amp;nbsp; At its first ringing he fell to his knees and shouted "Mon cloche, lu mam son" (my bell the same sound)&amp;nbsp; During the ceremony he cride and prayed whenever the bel
2 CONC l rang.&amp;nbsp; At one time during the day he said "I am very old, but the bell sounds the same.&amp;nbsp; How can we be sure?"&amp;nbsp; At this point Nocess Terrebonne stepped forward and told his story to Father Grimaud and all those about him
2 CONC .&amp;nbsp; This was the same Nocess who as a young boy had rung the bell with a hatchet 25 years ago and left a mark on it.&amp;nbsp; He now confessed his action and identified the cut he mad so many years ago.&amp;nbsp; Yes, this was the same bel
2 CONC l.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
