2 TITL a Canadian Biography of Philippe Mius d' Entremont
1 _OID 8853ca4b-ebe4-4917-870c-05832b519ae4
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;MIUS&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;strong&gt;(Muis)&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;strong&gt;D&amp;rsquo;ENTREMONT&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;PHILIPPE&lt;/strong&gt;, esquire, first baron of Pobomcoup (Pubn
2 CONC ico), near Cap de Sable, lieutenant-major, king&amp;rsquo;s attorney, settler, the first of the d&amp;rsquo;Entremonts of Nova Scotia; b. c. 1601 (or 1609) in Normandy (probably at Cherbourg); d. c. 1700 (or 1701).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;
2 CONC nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; According to Placide Gaudet, the Norman Philippe Mius was related by marriage to the Bourbon family and was made Sieur d&amp;rsquo;Entremont by Louis&amp;nbsp;XIV; but according to a descendant, H.&amp;nbsp;L&amp
2 CONC ;eacute;andre d&amp;rsquo;Entremont, the titles of nobility are said to go back to the 11th century in Savoy, and a branch of the Savoy family is thought to have emigrated to Normandy during the 16th century. In 1649 the Sieur d&amp;rsquo;Entremon
2 CONC t married Madeleine H&amp;eacute;lie (or &amp;Eacute;lie) Du&amp;nbsp;Tillet (b. 1626). He was then a captain in a regiment. It was in 1650 (Coll. de manuscrits relatifs &amp;agrave; la Nouv.-France, II, 329) or 1651 (R.&amp;nbsp;Le&amp;nbsp;Blant
2 CONC ) that he, with his wife and a daughter, was brought to Acadia by the new governor Charles de S&lt;span&gt;aint&lt;/span&gt;-&lt;span&gt;&amp;Eacute;tienne &lt;/span&gt;de La&amp;nbsp;Tour, a childhood friend, as lieutenant-major and commander of t
2 CONC he king&amp;rsquo;s troops. To reward him for his services, La&amp;nbsp;Tour offered d&amp;rsquo;Entremont in 1651 or 1653 the letters patent of the Pobomcoup fief, as a barony. The feudal rights conferred upon the baron a territory stretching fro
2 CONC m Cap N&amp;egrave;gre to Cap Fourchu (Yarmouth). The feudal castle was built near the entry to the natural harbour of Pubnico, on the east side.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; D&amp;rsquo;Entremont played an i
2 CONC mportant part in the colony&amp;rsquo;s history both because of what he did as an administrator and because he was one of the rare Acadian seigneurs to concern himself with cultivation and with clearing land; he attracted to his estate &amp;ldquo;s
2 CONC everal indentured workers and a few families from Port-Royal [now Annapolis Royal, N.S.] and this seigneury eventually formed a small centre of population.&amp;rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Besides thei
2 CONC r daughter whom they had brought from France, the d&amp;rsquo;Entremonts had four children who were born on Acadian soil: two of their sons, Jacques, b. 1659, and Abraham, de Plemazais (or Plemarch), b. 1661 or 1662, married respectively Anne and M
2 CONC arguerite, the daughters of Governor Charles de La&amp;nbsp;Tour and Jeanne M&lt;span&gt;otin&lt;/span&gt;; the third son, Philippe, whose life is more difficult to piece together, evidently married a daughter of Jean-Vincent d&amp;rsquo;Abbadie* d
2 CONC e Saint-Castin. As for the daughters, Marie-Marguerite, born in France, married Pierre Melanson, dit La&amp;nbsp;Verdure [see Charles M&lt;span&gt;elanson&lt;/span&gt;]; the other, Madeleine, seems to have remained a spinster.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&am
2 CONC p;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; Around 1670, at the time when the Treaty of Breda was being put into force, Governor A&lt;span&gt;ndign&amp;eacute; &lt;/span&gt;de Grandfontaine was establishing himself at Pentagouet on the Penobsco
2 CONC t. D&amp;rsquo;Entremont was named king&amp;rsquo;s attorney, an office which he held for 18 years despite his advanced age. We know of reports which were made by Mius d&amp;rsquo;Entremont and Jacques Bourgeois* on the subject of the Acadian front
2 CONC iers, and sent to the minister, Pontchartrain, by J&lt;span&gt;oseph &lt;/span&gt;R&lt;span&gt;obinau &lt;/span&gt;de Villebon. Towards the end of his life d&amp;rsquo;Entremont left his seigneurial estate, bequeathing the title of baron to his eld
2 CONC est son Jacques, and went to settle at Port-Royal with his wife and two of his children. He died at the end of 1700 or the beginning of 1701, a venerable patriarch more than 90 years old. According to tradition it was at Port-Royal that he died, bu
2 CONC t L&amp;eacute;andre d&amp;rsquo;Entremont indicates Grand-Pr&amp;eacute;, where d&amp;rsquo;Entremont may have gone to reside with his daughter Marie-Marguerite Melanson, as the more likely place of death. Philippe Mius d&amp;rsquo;Entremont has l
2 CONC eft a large number of descendants in Acadia; the barony of Pobomcoup remained in the family until the expulsion of the Acadians; and after more than three centuries some hundred families of the same name can still be counted at Pubnico.&lt;/p&gt;&l
2 CONC t;p align="right"&gt;C&lt;span&gt;l&amp;eacute;ment &lt;/span&gt;C&lt;span&gt;ormier&lt;/span&gt; &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Coll. de manuscrits relatifs &amp;agrave; la Nouv.-France, &lt;span&gt;II&lt;/span&gt;, 134, 329. Recensement de 1686 (Acadie). Pla
2 CONC cide Gaudet, notes preserved in the PAC and at the Universit&amp;eacute; de Moncton; &amp;eacute;tudes published in Moniteur Acadien (Shediac, N.B.), 17&amp;nbsp;Dec.&amp;nbsp;1886, 11 and 25&amp;nbsp;Jan.&amp;nbsp;1887. George S.&amp;nbsp;Brown, Y
2 CONC armouth, Nova Scotia&lt;span&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; a sequel to Campbell&amp;rsquo;s history (Boston, 1888), 151&amp;ndash;52. A.&amp;nbsp;Cameron, study published in the Halifax Herald, &lt;span&gt;1&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;Jan.&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;
2 CONC /span&gt;1886, of which the translation was published in the Moniteur Acadien, 21&amp;nbsp;Jan.&amp;nbsp;1886; see also issues of 7, 14&amp;nbsp;Jan.&amp;nbsp;1886, 17&amp;nbsp;Dec.&amp;nbsp;1886, 11&amp;nbsp;Jan.&amp;nbsp;1887. H.&amp;nbsp;L. d&am
2 CONC p;rsquo;Entremont, The Baronnie de Pombcoup and the Acadians, a history of the ancient &amp;ldquo;Department of Cape Sable,&amp;rdquo; now known&lt;span&gt; as&lt;/span&gt; Yarmouth and Shelburne counties, Nova Scotia (Yarmouth, 193&lt;span&gt;1&lt
2 CONC ;/span&gt;); The forts of Cape Sable of the seventeenth century (n.p., 1938); study on the genealogy of the Acadian families of Yarmouth county, published in the Yarmouth Herald beginning 20&amp;nbsp;Feb.&amp;nbsp;1940. Robert Le&amp;nbsp;Blant, &a
2 CONC mp;ldquo;Les trois mariages d&amp;rsquo;une Acadienne, Anne d&amp;rsquo;Entremont (1694&amp;ndash;1778),&amp;rdquo; NF, &lt;span&gt;VII&lt;/span&gt; (1932), 211. Rameau de Saint-P&amp;egrave;re, Une colonie f&amp;eacute;odale, &lt;span&gt;II&lt;/sp
2 CONC an&gt;, 320. P.-G.&amp;nbsp;Roy, &amp;ldquo;Les marquisats, compt&amp;eacute;s, baronnies et ch&amp;acirc;tellenies dans la Nouvelle-France,&amp;rdquo; BRH, &lt;span&gt;XXI&lt;/span&gt; (1915), 48. Webster, Acadia, 121.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;
2 CONC &lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
