2 TITL Enguerrand III Lord of Coucy
1 _OID 91063b97-ec7d-42b7-8efe-a10181a24eaf
1 _DSCR Source - Wikipedia
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Enguerrand III, Lord of  Coucy&lt;/strong&gt; (c. 1182 Ch&amp;acirc;teau de Coucy &amp;ndash; 1242) was the eldest son and successor  of Ralph I, Lord of Coucy. He succeeded  as Lord of Coucy  (&lt
2 CONC ;em&gt;sieur de Couci&lt;/em&gt;) in 1191, and held it until his death; he was  also lord of Marle and Boves.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Biography&lt;/span&gt; &lt;p&gt;Enguerrand III was one of the most ambitious and powerful of all the  French nobles
2 CONC , called by one historian &amp;quot;the greatest baron in all Picardy&amp;quot;,&lt;sup&gt;&lt;span&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;1&lt;span&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/sup&gt;  and earning himself his epithet, &lt;em&gt;Enguerrand le Grand&lt;/em&gt;, or  Enguerran
2 CONC d &amp;quot;the Great&amp;quot;.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Enguerrand had an illustrious military career, helping the King of the French Philip Augustus reduce the French territories of the  King of the English. Enguerrand campaigned in Anjou in 1205, an
2 CONC d in 1214  fought in the French victory over an Anglo-German alliance at the Battle of Bouvines. He was a notable member of the French  force which invaded the Kingdom of England (1216-1217) to depose King John. He also participated in the Albigens
2 CONC ian Crusade.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;After the death of Louis VIII of France, Enguerrand was chief among the  nobles who resisted the regency of Blanche of Castile for her son Louis IX of France, although he eventually returned to  the royal favour. Eng
2 CONC uerrand made his mark on the Picardy landscape by  constructing Coucy Castle, and he is said by tradition to have started  the famous rhyme associated with his successors:&lt;/p&gt; &lt;blockquote&gt;   &lt;em&gt;Je suis ni roi, ni prince aussi: J
2 CONC e suis le seigneur de Coucy!&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/em&gt; Neither king nor prince am I: I am the Lord of Coucy!&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;   &lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;span&gt;Relations&lt;/span&gt; &lt;p&gt;Through his mother Alice de Dreux,  Enguerrand I
2 CONC II was related to King Louis IX of France. Enguerrand also married into the  family of King Henry III of England, taking as his second wife the  latter king&amp;#39;s cousin, the granddaughter of Henry II of England. He married three times. His fir
2 CONC st  wife was Eustacia, his second Matilda (or Richenza) of  Saxony(1172-1208/09), the aforementioned granddaughter of Henry II, Duke of  Saxony and niece of Richard the  Lion-hearted, and the third Marie de  Montmirall. Enguerrand cemented his powe
2 CONC rful connections by marrying  his daughter Marie de Coucy to King Alexander II of Scotland.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Enguerrand died in 1242 by falling off of his horse onto his sword.  He was succeeded by his eldest son Raoul II, Lord of Coucy.&lt;/p&gt
2 CONC ; &lt;span&gt;Notes&lt;/span&gt; &lt;div&gt; &lt;ol&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;^&lt;/strong&gt;  Michael Brown, &lt;em&gt;The Wars of Scotland, 1214-1371&lt;/em&gt;, (Edinburgh,  2004), p. 141.&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ol&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;span&gt;Referen
2 CONC ces&lt;/span&gt; &lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;Brown, Michael, &lt;em&gt;The Wars of Scotland, 1214-1371&lt;/em&gt;, (Edinburgh,  2004)&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Barbara Tuchman. A Distant Mirror. Alfred A. Knopf, New York (1978), pp10-11&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt; &lt
2 CONC ;span&gt;External links&lt;/span&gt; &lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 0.95em; font-weight: bold; color: #555555"&gt;(English)&lt;/span&gt; A History of Coucy Castle&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;   &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp;     &lt;!-
2 CONC -  NewPP limit report Preprocessor node count: 206/1000000 Post-expand include size: 1250/2048000 bytes Template argument size: 206/2048000 bytes Expensive parser function count: 0/500 --&gt;  &lt;!-- Saved in parser cache with key enwiki:pcache:id
2 CONC hash:4753393-0!1!0!default!!en!4 and timestamp 20100703181741 --&gt; &lt;div&gt; Retrieved from &amp;quot;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Enguerrand_III,_Lord_of_Coucy&amp;quot;&lt;/div&gt; 				&lt;!-- /bodytext --&gt; 								&lt;!-- catlinks --&gt
