2 TITL Melancon-aka-LaVerdure pioneers
1 _OID 961c60dd-7a55-4fa8-8f17-0c04d9118d23
1 _DSCR cited source:
2 CONT http://genforum.genealogy.com/melancon/messages/586.html
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: tinos; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;Descendants of Philippe/Pierre Laverdure, (pre Melancon)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br style="font-family: tinos; font-size: medium; line-heig
2 CONC ht: normal;"&gt;&lt;br style="font-family: tinos; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: tinos; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;Generation No. 1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br style="font-family: tinos; font-size
2 CONC : medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;br style="font-family: tinos; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: tinos; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;
2 CONC nbsp;&amp;nbsp;1. Philippe/Pierre1 Laverdure, (pre Melancon) was born about 1608 in Charente Maritime, La Rochelle, France, and died about 1676 in Boston, Mass.. He married Priscilla Mallanson about 1630 in Yorkshire, England. She was born about 16
2 CONC 10 in Bradford, York, England, and died about 1691 in Boston, Mass..&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br style="font-family: tinos; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;br style="font-family: tinos; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span st
2 CONC yle="font-family: tinos; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;Notes for Philippe/Pierre Laverdure, (pre Melancon):&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br style="font-family: tinos; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: tinos
2 CONC ; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;He is referred to as Philippe in the notes that come with the 1678 census of Acadien.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br style="font-family: tinos; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-famil
2 CONC y: tinos; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;During this time period (as well as before and after this) surnames were not known as we know them today. When an individual became an adult, he took on a "dit" name that suited him. There wer
2 CONC e referred to as "so &amp;amp; so the son of ". As a result brothers from the same family often had different names. Leblond if he was blond, LePetit if he was little, Boucher if he was a butcher, etc. In the case of the sons of Pierre, Philippe to
2 CONC ok one of the names of both parents and Charles took his mother's name and another dit name. As a result the descendants of this family are now known as "Melancon" a spelling variation of Mallanson. During this period before surnames were establish
2 CONC ed a wife never changed her name to take on that of her husband's. She was always referred to by name and as the daughter of "so &amp;amp; so" or the wife of "so &amp;amp; so".&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br style="font-family: tinos; font-size: medium; line-h
2 CONC eight: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: tinos; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;Pierre La Verdure was born 1608 in La Rochelle, Charente-Maritime, France, and died 1676 in Boston, MA. He married Priscilla Mallanson Abt. 1630 in Y
2 CONC orkshire, England.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br style="font-family: tinos; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: tinos; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;Notes for Pierre La Verdure:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br style="font
2 CONC -family: tinos; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: tinos; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;C.T. Breaux, UNE LIGNEE NOBLE EN FRANCE, QUBEC, ACADIA, LOUSIANE, Revised 2 Jun 1986. "As a result of the fa
2 CONC ll of La Rochelle in 1628, when the death blow was delivered to the Calvinists in France, Pierre went to England where he married. He and Priscilla had three sons at least, all born in England. In the spring of 1657 the family embarked in England o
2 CONC n board the vessel Satisfaction, under the command of Captain Peter Butler, forming part of the company which Thomas Temple was transporting to Acadie, over which he had been named Governor after its capture by the English. Having first stopped a
2 CONC t Boston, Captain Butler next went to the fort on the St. John River, where a group of his passengers disembarked, and finally to Port Royal. It appears the LAVERDURE (or Melanson, as they were also known) family stopped at Fort St. John. After th
2 CONC e Treaty of Breda, under which in 1667 England ceded Acadie to France, Pierre and Priscilla went to seek refuge under the Protestant government at Boston, leaving behind in Acadie two of his sons, Pierre and Charles, with their young children."&lt;
2 CONC /span&gt;&lt;br style="font-family: tinos; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;br style="font-family: tinos; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: tinos; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;&am
2 CONC p;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br style="font-family: tinos; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: tinos; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;Children o
