2 CONC t: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: tinos; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;Research Bulletin 250, The Melancon Settlement (1664-1755), Canadian Minister of the Environment. "Charles Melanson, age 14, and his parents arrived in A
2 CONC cadie from England in 1657. His father, Pierre LaVerdure, was a Huguenot who had moved to England from France before 1632. His mother, Priscilla, was English, a fact that is well accepted by historians, although generations of die-hard descendants
2 CONC , who will not admit an ounce of English blood, continue to insist that she was Scottish. The family, which included at least two other children, Pierre and John, sailed from England on the Satisfaction with Thomas Temple, the newly appointed Gover
2 CONC nor of Acadie.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br style="font-family: tinos; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: tinos; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;The family is thought to have settled originally on the Saint J
2 CONC ames River. Then in 1667, when Acadie was returned to France by the Treaty of Breda, Charles' parents and brother John moved to Boston. Charles and Pierre remained in Acadie. Charles had renounced Protestantism in 1664 and married Marie Dugas, daug
2 CONC hter of Port Royal armorer, Abraham Dugas and Marguerite Douchet. Following their marriage, they probably settled on the land which we now know as the Melanson Settlement in the Port Royal area. Although no concession has been found, a British docu
2 CONC ment of 1734 states that this land was granted originally to Charles Melanson and "honest" Marie Dugas.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br style="font-family: tinos; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: tinos; font-size: medium
2 CONC ; line-height: normal;"&gt;By 1671, the Melancon were majors landholders at Port Royal. The first census, taken that year listed 20 arpents of workable land, not all of it under cultivation, 40 head of cattle and 6 sheep. Charles was described a
2 CONC s a "labourer", and the family had 4 daughters at the time. In total, 5 sons and 9 daughters were born ca. 1664 - ca. 1693. The eldest, Marie, was brought up in Boston by her grandmother, strengthening Charles' ties with that colony. Of the other c
2 CONC hildren, 8 established households in the Melancon settlement, where they remained for their adult lives. One daughter settled elsewhere in the Port Royal are, one settled at Beaubassin, and one apparently died."&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br style="font-famil
2 CONC y: tinos; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: tinos; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;The next two generations of Melancons were not as fortunate. On Sept. 5, 1755 the British summoned all the boys ov
2 CONC er 10 and all the men to the local church for a town meeting. Once there, they were all made prisoners and sent by ship in exile all over the the eastern coast of what is now the USA. In Grand Pre alone, 12 men were recorded as having been shippe
2 CONC d out. This entire event makes for great history reading.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br style="font-family: tinos; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: tinos; font-size: medium; line-height: normal;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;
