2 TITL Lois Prescott Hess
1 _OID 9fe34677-edb2-4718-b3d2-f804ae280cb9
1 DATE 1917-1944
1 PLAC Cameron, Texas
1 _DSCR A short story written by my mother, Evelyn Beatrice Parker Hess, also a nurse.
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;Lois, the&amp;nbsp;oldest daughter of Nan Thatcher Ford Prescott, decided she wanted to make her own way and not continue farming with the family.&amp;nbsp; Lois wanted to go into nurses training at the Camero
2 CONC n Hospital, Cameron, Texas,&amp;nbsp;at age 17.&amp;nbsp; Her mother begged her not to go, but she was determined.&amp;nbsp; Nan gave her one dollar, which may have been all she could scrape together, and Lois took a bus to Cameron and began her ch
2 CONC osen work.&amp;nbsp; She graduated in 1919.&amp;nbsp; After she married Ernest Pinkney Hess, 8 November, 1919, she continued to work in the hospital all of her life, except to have her two children, Bonnelle and Delbert, until 1943.&amp;nbsp; &lt;/
2 CONC p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;During the &amp;quot;great depression&amp;quot;, she worked all the time, 24 hours a day in patient&amp;#39;s homes and may times they could not pay her money, but gave her food.&amp;nbsp; She canned the f
2 CONC ood for other people for half of the product.&amp;nbsp; Any extra she got was sold to stores for cash.&amp;nbsp; Her husband Ernest could not find a job, but hunted rabbits, birds, squirrels, and fished.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;When the goverment opene
2 CONC d the Civilian Conservation Corps, known as the CCC Camps, he went to have a job.&amp;nbsp; It housed the men and gave them jobs and&amp;nbsp;small pay to work on public building projects.&amp;nbsp; This was one of the many ways President Delano Ro
2 CONC osevelt used to get the people work, a pay check, and allowed them to keep their pride and help them build their lives again.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;April 10, 1943, when Lois and Ernest lived in Hood Village, Lois decided t
2 CONC o take a bus to go to Temple to visit her daughter, Bonnelle.&amp;nbsp; A short time after she boarded the bus, she was seen to stand up suddenly and fell on the floor.&amp;nbsp; The bus driver and other passengers rushed to her aid.&amp;nbsp; Seei
2 CONC ng that she had evidently had a heart attack, the bus driver sped the bus to Killen which was only 2 miles from her home.&amp;nbsp; All efforts to revive her were unsuccessful.&amp;nbsp; She was buried in Rosebut, Texas, Cemetery, 12 April.&amp;nbs
2 CONC p; She was&amp;nbsp;44 years old.&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
