1 _DSCR Clovis I
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: arial, verdana"&gt;Clovis was the first King of the Franks to unite all Frankish (West Germanic tribal confederation first attested in the third century as living north and east of t
2 CONC he Lower Rhine River - today Franken) tribes under one ruler. He was also the first Catholic King to rule over Gaul (France).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: sans-serif; line-height: 19px"&gt;He was the son of&amp;nbsp;C
2 CONC hilderic I&amp;nbsp;and&amp;nbsp;Basina. In 481, when he was fifteen, he succeeded his father.&amp;nbsp;The&amp;nbsp;Salian Franks&amp;nbsp;were one of two Frankish tribes who were then occupying the area west of the lower&amp;nbsp;Rhine, with thei
2 CONC r center in an area known asToxandria, between the&amp;nbsp;Meuse&amp;nbsp;and&amp;nbsp;Scheldt&amp;nbsp;(in what is now the Netherlands and Belgium). Clovis&amp;#39;s power base was to the southwest of this, around&amp;nbsp;Tournai&amp;nbsp;and&am
2 CONC p;nbsp;Cambrai&amp;nbsp;along the modern frontier between France and Belgium. Clovis conquered the neighboring Salian Frankish kingdoms and established himself as sole king of the Salian Franks before his death. The small church in which he was bap
2 CONC tized is now named&amp;nbsp;Saint-Remi, and a statue of him being baptized by&amp;nbsp;Saint Remigius&amp;nbsp;can be seen there. Clovis and his wifeClotilde&amp;nbsp;are buried in the&amp;nbsp;St. Genevieve church&amp;nbsp;(St. Pierre) in Paris. A
2 CONC n important part of Clovis&amp;#39;s legacy is that he reduced the power of the Romans in 486 by beating the Roman ruler&amp;nbsp;Syagrius&amp;nbsp;in the&amp;nbsp;battle of Soissons.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: sans
2 CONC -serif; line-height: 19px"&gt;Clovis was converted to&amp;nbsp;Catholicism, as opposed to the&amp;nbsp;Arian Christianity&amp;nbsp;common among the&amp;nbsp;Goths&amp;nbsp;who ruled most of Gaul at the time, at the instigation of his wife, Clotilde
2 CONC , a&amp;nbsp;Burgundian&amp;nbsp;Gothic princess who was a Catholic in spite of the Arianism which surrounded her at court. He was baptized in a small church which was on or near the site of the&amp;nbsp;Cathedral of Rheims, where most future&amp;n
2 CONC bsp;French kings&amp;nbsp;would be crowned. This act was of immense importance in the subsequent history of Western and Central Europe in general, for Clovis expanded his dominion over almost all of the old Roman province of&amp;nbsp;Gaul&amp;nbsp;
2 CONC (roughly modern France). He is considered the founder of the&amp;nbsp;Merovingian dynasty&amp;nbsp;which ruled the Franks for the next two centuries.&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;Wikipedia&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadatax
2 CONC ml>
