2 TITL Arnoald, also called Arnoldus or Arnual
1 _OID aac73a4d-8bb9-49ef-aa7e-2051adde17fa
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p style="margin-: 0.4em; margin-bottom: 0.5em; line-height: 19.1875px; font-family: sans-serif; font-size: 13px;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Arnoald&lt;/strong&gt;, also called&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Arnoldus&lt;/strong&gt;&am
2 CONC p;nbsp;or&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Arnual&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;(ca. 540/560 &amp;ndash; ca 611), was a&amp;nbsp;Bishop of Metz&amp;nbsp;between 601 and 609 or 611, the successor of his uncle&amp;nbsp;Agilulf. He was the son ofAnsbertus, a&amp;nbsp
2 CONC ;Senator, and wife Blithilde.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;span id="Life" class="mw-headline"&gt;Life&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mw-editsection" style="-webkit-user-select: none; font-size: small; margin-left: 1em; vertical-align: baseline; line-heig
2 CONC ht: 1em; display: inline-block;"&gt;[&lt;a style="text-decoration: none; color: #0b0080; background-image: none; background-position: initial initial; background-repeat: initial initial;" title="Edit section: Life" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/w/
2 CONC index.php?title=Arnoald&amp;amp;action=edit&amp;amp;section=1"&gt;edit&lt;/a&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-: 0.4em; margin-bottom: 0.5em; line-height: 19.1875px; font-family: sans-serif; font-size: 13px;"&gt;Virtually no informa
2 CONC tion survives concerning the acts of Arnoald prior to his becoming Bishop of Metz or during his occupation of the episcopal seat. From Paulus Diaconius we learn that he was Roman and of senatorial extraction suggesting that he came from one of th
2 CONC e families in southern Gaul whose members had held senatorial rank during the empire.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-: 0.4em; margin-bottom: 0.5em; line-height: 19.1875px; font-family: sans-serif; font-size: 13px;"&gt;The Commemoratio Ge
2 CONC nealogiae Domni Karoli Glorissimi Imperatoris which although it is clearly incorrect in some respects (see below) suggest strongly that Arnoald was one of the Ferreoli, a family dating from the 4th century who were based in the Midi but appear to h
2 CONC ave switched their loyalties from the Visigothic Kingdom to the Frankish Kingdom ofAustrasia&amp;nbsp;with its capital at Metz during the two decades following the&amp;nbsp;Battle of Vouill&amp;eacute;&amp;nbsp;in 507. Modern arguments have been ma
2 CONC de that this relationship was invented by contemporaries to give the Carolingians a Roman pedigree. However this is unlikely to have been the case inasmuch as (a) the Ferreoli, though significant, were far from the senior senatorial family in Gau
2 CONC l and (b) it would have been too easy for the Carolingians to obtain whatever Roman bloodline they wished legitimately by marrying into remnants of those families.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-: 0.4em; margin-bottom: 0.5em; line-height
2 CONC : 19.1875px; font-family: sans-serif; font-size: 13px;"&gt;Still the interest taken by the Austrasians in the Ferreoli is enigmatic and significant and may have to do with their potential use in a conquest of Visigothic Septimania. Several member
2 CONC s of the family were elevated to the rank of Patrician (senior Roman official) of Provence during the 6th century. Arnoald's acquisition of the bishopric of Metz is unsurprising inasmuch as his uncle Agilulf had held it. The reason Agilulf was gran
2 CONC ted the position is more obscure. There do not appear to be any predecessors of this family in the bishopric of Metz which prior to 590 was not important enough to warrant a single mention from Gregory of Tours. Prior to Agilulf, the most powerfu
2 CONC l bishops in the Austrasian kingdom were Egidius of Rheims and Gregory of Tours.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-: 0.4em; margin-bottom: 0.5em; line-height: 19.1875px; font-family: sans-serif; font-size: 13px;"&gt;Egidius was convicted o
2 CONC f treason in 590 and the rise of the Bishops of Metz from that time certainly is in part due to those events. Even after these events, Metz cannot truly be said to have become the most powerful bishopric until the time of&amp;nbsp;Arnulf. It is o
2 CONC f some interest that the name of the diplomatically adept deacon of Gregory of Tours in 590 was also an Agilulf.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;span id="Relation_to_St._Arnulf_of_Metz" class="mw-headline"&gt;Relation to St. Arnulf of Metz&lt;/span&gt;&l
2 CONC t;span class="mw-editsection" style="-webkit-user-select: none; font-size: small; margin-left: 1em; vertical-align: baseline; line-height: 1em; display: inline-block;"&gt;[&lt;a style="text-decoration: none; color: #0b0080; background-image: none
2 CONC ; background-position: initial initial; background-repeat: initial initial;" title="Edit section: Relation to St. Arnulf of Metz" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Arnoald&amp;amp;action=edit&amp;amp;section=2"&gt;edit&lt;/a&gt;]&lt;
2 CONC /span&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin-: 0.4em; margin-bottom: 0.5em; line-height: 19.1875px; font-family: sans-serif; font-size: 13px;"&gt;The Commemoratio Genealogiae Domni Karoli Glorissimi Imperatoris written originally in the early 9th cent
2 CONC ury states that&amp;nbsp;Arnulf of Metz&amp;nbsp;was the son of predecessor Arnoaldus of Metz, himself a son of Ansbertus and thus the Ferreoli of the Midi. J. Depoin in 'Grand Figures Monacales Du Temps Merovingiens. St Arnoul de Metz, Etudes de C
2 CONC ritique Historique' Revue Mabillon 21, (1921) observed that chronolgically and for other reasons - Arnulf was identified as a Frank in contemporary documents whereas Arnoald was identified by&amp;nbsp;Paulus Diaconus&amp;nbsp;as a Roman&amp;nbsp;&l
2 CONC t;em&gt;ex nobilia senatorum familia orto&lt;/em&gt;- this construction was incorrect and proposed an alternative to the effect that Arnulf's father wasBodegisel, based in significant part on the Vita Gundolphi.&amp;nbsp;David Humiston Kelley&amp;n
2 CONC bsp;then proposed that Arnoaldus was likely an ancestor of the Carolingians through a daughter Itta, wife of Pepin of Landin&amp;nbsp;Christian Settipani&amp;nbsp;carefully revisited and expanded upon the work of Depoin and Kelley. He concurred i
2 CONC n Arnulf's descent from Bodegisel instead of Arnoald, and that there was a connection between the Ripuarian Frankish royal house and the Carolingians but argued (without dismissing the possibility of Itta's being Arnoald's daughter) that there wa
2 CONC s a connection through Arnulf's wife, Doda who he posited as a daughter of Arnoald. Kelly considered Settipani's proposed connection between the Carolingians and Arnoald and regarded it as probable.&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
