1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: arial,helvetica,sans-serif; font-size: small; color: #000000;"&gt;Historians agree neither on his [Charles's] ethnic origin nor on the date of his arrival in Canada. Undeniably, he &
2 CONC amp;ldquo;came from Scotland&amp;rdquo;; but, as a notarial contract designated him &amp;ldquo;Sieur de La&amp;nbsp;Ram&amp;eacute;e, and as his brother Pierre was nicknamed &amp;ldquo;La&amp;nbsp;Verdure,&amp;rdquo; Placide Gaudet concluded that t
2 CONC he family might have been of French origin, and that, because it was Huguenot, it might have emigrated to Scotland, whence it went to Acadia. Some writers claim that the Melansons, belonged to the settlement founded by Sir&amp;nbsp;William &lt;spa
2 CONC n class="SmallCaps" style="color: #000000;"&gt;Alexander,&lt;/span&gt; the younger, According to Placide Gaudet, the family arrived in the colony in 1657 with Governor &lt;span class="SmallCaps" style="color: #000000;"&gt;Temple&lt;/span&gt;; it se
2 CONC ttled at Port-Royal; later it is thought to have emigrated to Boston, leaving in Acadia Pierre and Charles, the only members of the family whose names have been preserved in history.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-
2 CONC family: arial,helvetica,sans-serif; font-size: small; color: #000000;"&gt;The elder, Pierre, &lt;em&gt;dit&lt;/em&gt; La&amp;nbsp;Verdure, a tailor, husband of Marie-Marguerite Mius d&amp;rsquo;Entremont, was one of the founders of Grand-Pr&amp;eac
2 CONC ute;. Charles, a &lt;em&gt;&amp;ldquo;laboureur&amp;rdquo;&lt;/em&gt; (ploughman), according to the 1671 census, worked the paternal estate and became prosperous; in 1664, after renouncing Protestantism, he married Marie Dugas, by whom he had sever
2 CONC al children. Their descendants have been numerous.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: arial,helvetica,sans-serif; font-size: small; color: #000000;"&gt;Cl&amp;eacute;me
2 CONC nt Cormier, &amp;ldquo;MELANSON, CHARLES,&amp;rdquo; in &lt;em&gt;Dictionary of Canadian Biography&lt;/em&gt;, vol. 1, University of Toronto/Universit&amp;eacute; Laval, 2003&amp;ndash;, accessed April 12, 2015, http://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/melan
2 CONC son_charles_1E.html. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
