2 CONT http://home.earthlink.net/~jimandmartha/Toops_Notes.htm
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p style="font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14pt;"&gt;The&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Dubbs&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;-&amp;nbsp;&lt;sp
2 CONC an&gt;Toops&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;Families Descendents of Hans Jacob&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14pt;"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-size: medi
2 CONC um; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal;"&gt;Compiled by Roger Bruce&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Dubbs&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;of&amp;nbsp;Universal City,&amp;nbsp;Texas&amp;nbsp;dated May 1995&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;div style="font-famil
2 CONC y: tinos; font-size: medium; border-style: none none double; border-bottom-color: windowtext; border-bottom-width: 1.5pt; padding: 0in 0in 1pt;"&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-size: 12pt; font-family: 'times new roman'; border: none; padding: 0i
2 CONC n;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 8pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;/div&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal;"&gt;John Dups 22 Sept 1746 (East Hanover Township
2 CONC , PA) - 1 Apr 1818&amp;nbsp; (Ross Cty, OH) Married (1773) Christiana ? 1746 -&amp;nbsp;27 Jul 1819&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal;"&gt;Children - Daniel 1774, Henry 1777, J
2 CONC ohn ?, Elizabeth ?, Catherine ?, Jacob ?, Barbara ?, Samuel 1786, Leonard 1790, Adam 1793 &amp;amp; Christiana 1795&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-
2 CONC size: 10pt;"&gt;Since John&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;, on the death of his father, chose as guardian, Henry Waggoner, a blacksmith, it is believed he became a blacksmith as well as a farmer. The only tradition has come down about his ge
2 CONC neration is that the sons were bound out to learn a trade. This was the custom of the time and not confined to orphans.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;
2 CONC span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;1776 John&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;was a drummer in the Company of Captain Thomas&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Ko
2 CONC ppenheffer&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;of the Lancaster County Militia and his name appears on various muster rolls of the Revolutionary period. A note is appended to one stating that John&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;and several other
2 CONC s had deserted - the whole group of them "Living in&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Hanover&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Township&lt;/span&gt
2 CONC ;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;near Adam Harper's." This must have a temporary AWOL for he is listed again in 1778 and 1781.&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;(Pa. archives, 5th Series, Vol. 7, pp 153, 545, 1054 and 1055.)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span
2 CONC &gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Besides his birth record and his militia service, the only other record of John&amp;nbsp;&lt;sp
2 CONC an&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;in&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Pennsylvania&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;is that of a son, Henry, born to John and Christine&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;
2 CONC /span&gt;&amp;nbsp;in 1777 and recorded in the&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Swatara&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;Church Book. John&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;is believed to have left&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;
2 CONC Lancaster&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;County&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;in 1781 since his son John, born 1871, was born in&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Virginia&lt;/
2 CONC span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;. A&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Virginia&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;census of 1784 shows him with a family of five living in&amp;nbsp;&lt;/s
2 CONC pan&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Hampshire County&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Virginia&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;(now&amp;
2 CONC nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;West Virginia&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;). By 1800 the family was in Ross County, Ohio where the eldest son married in 1799.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p styl
2 CONC e="font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Ross County, Ohio was a raw frontier in 1800 and the records kept were few and scanty. The first tax record found lists John&amp;nbs
2 CONC p;&lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;paying tax on 100 acres of land in&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Twin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt
2 CONC ;Township&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;on Waugh's Run in 1808. He paid his tax (something like 75 cents per year) up until 1818 the year he died. The probate record is very brief and appears under the name of John&amp;
2 CONC nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Toops&lt;/span&gt;, although the tombstone (which still exists and is legible in the&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Moore&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;spa
2 CONC n style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Cemetery&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;on the Cincinnati Pike, Twin&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;township&lt;/span&gt;, Ross County, Ohio) lists it correctly as Dups. Samuel&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;To
2 CONC ops&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;was listed as administrator and Daniel&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Toops&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;as his bondsman. But there is no record of the disposal of the estate or any deed transferring land.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><lin
2 CONC e>&lt;p style="font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal;"&gt;Hans Jacob&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;3&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;Mar 1715&amp;nbsp;(&lt;span
2 CONC &gt;Affoltern&lt;/span&gt;,&amp;nbsp;Switzerland) - 1761 (Lebanon,&amp;nbsp;PA) Married (1744) Barbara&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Wierick&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;1717 (Lebanon,&amp;nbsp;PA) 1769&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-size: medium; font-fa
2 CONC mily: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Hans Jacob&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;was born&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;3 March 1715&lt;/span&gt;&lt;spa
2 CONC n style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;in&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Affoltern&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;at&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Mt.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&l
2 CONC t;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Albis&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;near&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Zurich&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;,&amp;nbsp;&lt;
2 CONC /span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Switzerland&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;. He was the sixth child with three older sisters and two older and one younger brother. His older brother, Heinrich, had gone to&amp;nbsp;&l
2 CONC t;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Pennsylvania&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;in 1729 and, as soon as he was 21, Jacob followed.&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Leaving his home on&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&
2 CONC lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;4 April 1736&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;together with Rudolph&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Haug&lt;/span&gt;, a young man of 25 years.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;
2 CONC /span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;They arrived in&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Philadelphia&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;on the ship "Princess Augusta", on&amp;nbsp;&
2 CONC lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;16 September 1736&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;. The route by which they traveled was up the&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Rhine&lt;/span&gt;&lt;spa
2 CONC n style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;River&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;in small boats to the mouth of the&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt
2 CONC ;Rhine&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;at&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Rotterdam&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;where they waited for an English ship bound for&amp
2 CONC ;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;America&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;. Another&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;passenger&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;on this same ship was&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Jost&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&l
2 CONC t;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;, who had no direct relationship to Jacob as he was later proved to have come from&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Germany&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&g
2 CONC t;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;At the end of the journey the passengers were taken to the courthouse in Philadelphia where they took the o
2 CONC ath of allegiance to the King of England and at that time Hans Jacob&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;signed his name in legible German script, using the spelling&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;, though in the Swiss records th
2 CONC e name of his family had been as often recorded as Dubs as Dups. In&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;America&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;, however, it would be spelled much more often&amp;nbsp;&lt;span
2 CONC &gt;Toops&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;or&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;Tups&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;, that&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;being the way it sounded to English ears. Also it would appear more often as Jacob rather than Hans Jacob o
2 CONC r John Jacob.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;As would be expected, Jacob settled near his brother, Henry (Heinrich), b
2 CONC uying land from Thomas Penn in what was East Hanover Twp.,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Lancaster County&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Pen
2 CONC nsylvania&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;, but now&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Lebanon&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Cou
2 CONC nty&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;. This was about seven miles west of the point on the&lt;span&gt;Swatara&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;Creek in Bethel Twp where Henry lived. It was near what is still known as Harper's Tavern. The warra
2 CONC nt for 150 acres of land, dated&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;6 June 1737&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;, made out to Hans Jacob&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Tupz&lt;/span&gt;, still remains in the State Lan
2 CONC d Office at&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Harrisburg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;PA.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&am
2 CONC p;nbsp;At that time the Penn's land sold at the rate of 15 pounds 10 shillings per 100 acres and required an annual quit-rent of half&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;a penny&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;sterling per acre. The farm when surveyed many years later wa
2 CONC s found to contain 249 acres.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;The next evidence we have of Jacob&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;D
2 CONC ups&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;in&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;America&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;is the notation in 1740 that he was a member of the&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;spa
2 CONC n style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Swarata&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Reform&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;spa
2 CONC n style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Church&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;- a church that at that time was located on the farm of Henry&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;east of what is now&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;
2 CONC &lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Jonestown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;PA.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;In 1742 he acted as sponso
2 CONC r at the baptism of a child of John&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Heil&lt;/span&gt;. But within the next few years he became a convert of the Moravian sect and belonged to the&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Bethel&lt;/span&gt;&lt
2 CONC ;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Moravian&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Church&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="f
2 CONC ont-size: 10pt;"&gt;, located about two miles east of Jonestown. Both of his brothers remained in the&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Swarata&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/
2 CONC span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Church&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-
2 CONC size: 10pt;"&gt;In 1743 Jacob&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;and Rudolph&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Haug&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;returned to&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Switzerland&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=
2 CONC "font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;to bring back more of the family to&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;America&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;. They arrived in Switzerland 24 February and started back agai
2 CONC n on 2 May 1743 with a party of 52 people, including the younger brother, Hans&lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;, and two married sisters, Barbara and&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Verena&lt;/span&gt;, with their husbands and children. Hans&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;
2 CONC Dups&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;arrived in&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;America&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;but the sisters have not been identified in&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="fo
2 CONC nt-size: 10pt;"&gt;America&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;The yea
2 CONC r after he returned to America Jacob&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;married. Unfortunately none of the birth records of his children gave his wife's name. It is possible that he was married twice. Certainly Barbara&amp;nbsp;&lt;spa
2 CONC n&gt;Wierich&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;was his wife and mother of his last six children. In the probate record in the courthouse at&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Lancaster&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&a
2 CONC mp;nbsp;her name is signed as both Rebecca and Barbara. Since she signed by mark this error was possible. But the decisive evidence that she was Barbara&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Wierich&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;comes from a receipt given by Hans&amp;nbsp;
2 CONC &lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;as guardian of the daughter, Anna Marie. This receipt was for Anna Marie's share of her grandfather, George&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Wierrich's&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;estate, and was dated&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt
2 CONC ;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;14 March 1772&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;. (This receipt remained in the papers of Henry&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Hautz&lt;/span&gt;, son-in-law of Hans&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;, an
2 CONC d the executor of this estate.) George&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Wierich's&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;estate was settled in 1771-1772 though he died in 1751, leaving a number of children. Barbara was probably the oldest child but there seems to be some doub
2 CONC t that she could have been old enough to have married and have had children by 1745. W. H.&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Egle&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;in his "History of Lebanon County" makes a guess at the ages of the&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Wierich&lt;/span&gt;
2 CONC &amp;nbsp;children which must be incorrect. The sister of Barbara&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;, Maria Elizabeth&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Wierich&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;was a sponsor at the baptism of Barbara's son Henry in 1754. Barbara&amp;nb
2 CONC sp;&lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;died sometime between 1767 and 1771.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"
2 CONC &gt;the&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;name of Jacob&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;(&lt;span&gt;Toops&lt;/span&gt;) appears regularly on such tax lists as still survive of East Hanover Twp
2 CONC . The years of the French-Indian War in&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;America&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;were a time of terror in that vicinity. In the year 1756 almost half of the name
2 CONC s on the tax rolls are marked "killed" or "&lt;span&gt;flede&lt;/span&gt;" or "child taken" but there was no note of disaster appended to the&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;brothers.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="
2 CONC font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;In 1761 there was a note of Americanization in the appearance of Jacob&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;' name among the roll of t
2 CONC he township officers. His job was Overseer of Roads and his was the only German name among the politically predominate Scotch-Irish ones. In the same year he died, perhaps unexpectedly, as he left no will or instructions for the care of&amp;nbsp;&l
2 CONC t;span&gt;this&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;eight minor children.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;When their fathe
2 CONC r died, the elder sons, Jacob and John, being fourteen years old or above were allowed to choose their guardians.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;Jacob chose Adam Harper, an&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;inkeeper&lt;/
2 CONC span&gt;&amp;nbsp;near the family farm, John chose Henry Waggoner, a blacksmith of&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Bethel&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Township&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10
2 CONC pt;"&gt;. Anna Maria, Daniel and Henry were placed under the guardianship of their uncle, John Dups. The three youngest were placed under the guardianship of Urban Long but evidently remained with their mother as she was allowed three pounds a yea
2 CONC r for Juliana, four pounds for Leonard and two pounds for Frederick. Jacob, the oldest son, having obtained his majority in 1766 petitioned the court to take over the plantation and the land at that time was appraised at 230 pounds in value.&lt;/sp
2 CONC an&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal;"&gt;Henry (Heinrich)&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; 1699&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;(&lt;span&gt;Alffoltern&l
2 CONC t;/span&gt;, SW) -&amp;nbsp;2 Nov 1753&amp;nbsp;(&lt;span&gt;Alffoltern&lt;/span&gt;, SW) Married (13 Dec 1692) Veronica&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Schneebli&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;1670 (&lt;span&gt;Alffoltern&lt;/span&gt;, SW)&amp;nbsp;25 May 1733&amp;nb
2 CONC sp;(&lt;span&gt;Alffoltern&lt;/span&gt;, SW)&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;He was an "&lt;span&gt;Ehgaumer&lt;/span&gt;", a membe
2 CONC r of the church council.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal;"&gt;Hans Jacob&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; Apr&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;1
2 CONC 637 (&lt;span&gt;Alffoltern&lt;/span&gt;, SW)- ? (&lt;span&gt;Alffoltern&lt;/span&gt;, SW) Married (17 Oct 1664)&amp;nbsp;Verona&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Epprecht&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;- Sep 1643 (&lt;span&gt;Alffoltern&lt;/span&gt;, SW) After 1695 (&l
2 CONC t;span&gt;Alffoltern&lt;/span&gt;, SW)&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal;"&gt;Hans (&lt;span&gt;Dupps&lt;/span&gt;)&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp
2 CONC ; Jun&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;1609 - ? Married (9 Feb 1630&lt;span&gt;)&lt;span&gt;Vreni&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Bumann&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;Jun 1609 (SW) ? (&lt;span&gt;Alffoltern&lt;/span&gt;, SW)&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;
2 CONC p style="font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;In 1634 was a member of the church council. Presumably his wife was not from&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Affoltern&lt;/span&gt;&amp;n
2 CONC bsp;and the marriage took place at the residence of his bride. It was customary at that time that the wedding was held at the residence of the bride.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'
2 CONC ; line-height: normal;"&gt;Michael&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Dupps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; May&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;1579 (&lt;span&gt;Alffoltern&lt;/span&gt;, SW) -&amp;nbsp;30 May 1633&amp;nbsp;(&lt;span&gt;Alffoltern&lt;/span&gt;, SW) Marr
2 CONC ied (3 Feb 1600) Barbara&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Sutterin&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;- 1579 (&lt;span&gt;Alffoltern&lt;/span&gt;, SW) - 1622 (&lt;span&gt;Alffoltern&lt;/span&gt;, SW)&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-size: medium; font-family: 'time
2 CONC s new roman'; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;In his second marriage he was married to&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Verena&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;Lips, who at her death&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt
2 CONC ;24 Jan 1640&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;is called "widow of Michael&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Dupss&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;selig&lt;/span&gt;". Only this Michael can be in question, for the other Michael live
2 CONC d at&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Aesch&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;until 1662. Michel&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Dupss&lt;/span&gt;, called German, shoemaker, died May 1633&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new rom
2 CONC an'; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;Jergman&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;(&lt;span&gt;Dups&lt;/span&gt;)&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Dupps&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp; 1550&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;(&lt;span&gt;Alffoltern&lt;/span&gt;, SW) -&amp;nbsp;1
2 CONC 1 Jul 1621&amp;nbsp;(&lt;span&gt;Alffoltern&lt;/span&gt;, SW) Married (5 Dec 1573)&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Vreni&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;Gut 1555 (&lt;span&gt;Oberlennern&lt;/span&gt;, SW) - ?&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-size: medi
2 CONC um; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Since records were not required in&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Switzerland&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;
2 CONC "&gt;&amp;nbsp;before 1550, there is no record of his birth.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal;"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;The elsewhere unknown nam
2 CONC e of&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Jergman&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;or&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Jergman&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;is possibly a development from&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Jerg&lt;/span&gt;,&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Joerg&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;-&amp;nbsp;&lt;spa
2 CONC n&gt;Georg&lt;/span&gt;. In further variation of the name and to distinguish between other Dubs his sons were called "German".&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal;"&
2 CONC gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;Jergman&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Dupss&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;married&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Vreni&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;Gut from&amp;nbsp;&
2 CONC lt;span&gt;Oberlunnernat&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;at&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;Affolten&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;on&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;5 December 1573&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;. He was a shoemake
2 CONC r and last cutter. He died&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;11 July 1621&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt;"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'
2 CONC ; line-height: normal;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
