1 _OID b12eccd8-e404-4a00-9f42-2d77e3c08f43
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>Germain Doucet dit Laverdure came to Acadia from France about 1639, he was&lt;br&gt;a Major under d&amp;#39;Aulnay and a trusted associate of his at Penobscot. After&lt;br&gt;d&amp;#39;Aulnay&amp;#39;s death Germain rece
2 CONC ived his estate jointly with Jeanne Motin.He&lt;br&gt;surrendered the garrison at Port Royal to the british under Major Sedgwick&lt;br&gt;in 1674.&lt;br&gt;He arrived in North America at what is now LaHave, Nova Scotia in 1632. He&lt;br&gt;was a so
2 CONC ldier, and in 1640 he honored with the title of Captain-at-Arms of&lt;br&gt;Pentagoute. In the decade that followed he was in action against British&lt;br&gt;soldiers from the American colonies and was forced to surrender his force&lt;br&gt;of 10
2 CONC 0 men when outnumbered by 500 soldiers from Boston. As part of the&lt;br&gt;agreement to capitulate, however, he and the colonists of port royal were&lt;br&gt;given a choice of returning to France or staying to till the soil of their&lt;br&gt;adopt
2 CONC ed country. Some lelected to return to France in 1654, including&lt;br&gt;Captain Doucet. However, he left behind a daughter and his son, Pierre.&lt;br&gt;Germain and his son, Francois were know to have connections with the&lt;br&gt;Micmac Indians
2 CONC , possibly intermarriage.&lt;br&gt;Children:&lt;br&gt;1. Marguerite Louise b. 1634&lt;br&gt;2. Francois b. 1709&lt;br&gt;3. Pierre </line></content></metadataxml>
