1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>Melanson Settlement National Historic Site of Canada     Revelations from a 17th century Acadian settlement&amp;nbsp;&lt;br&gt;     Interview with Brenda Dunn, historian and author &lt;br&gt;     &lt;p&gt;       &lt;br&g
2 CONC t;Interviewed by Andi Rierden for Parks Canada &lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;     &lt;div style="float: right; width: 209px"&gt;        &lt;span&gt;Brenda Dunn at the Melanson settlement &lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;copy; Parks Canada/A. Rierden &
2 CONC lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;     &lt;p&gt;Few places in North America can tell us as much about Acadian  family and community life in the 17th and 18th centuries than the  Melanson Settlement National Historic Site. Archaeological digs in the  1980
2 CONC s - aided by historic documentation - revealed ruins of a village  left untouched, apart from light farming, since 1755. As historian  Brenda Dunn details in a conversation below, the site emerges as a  prominent and important early Acadian settlem
2 CONC ent, whose members were key  in helping to create a distinct society based primarily on family  kinships and a unique form of agriculture. Located on the upland  overlooking the entrance to the Dauphin (Annapolis) River en route to  the epicentre o
2 CONC f government for &lt;em&gt;Acadie&lt;/em&gt;, the settlement managed  to thrive for many decades under the weight of a long and unremitting  struggle for control over the region. &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Before retiring from Parks  Canada in 1998, Ms. D
2 CONC unn served as an historian for the Nova Scotia  National Historic Sites of Canada at Louisbourg, Fort Anne, Grand-Pr&amp;eacute;  and Melanson Settlement. She has published widely on Nova Scotia&amp;rsquo;s  colonial history and is the author of &l
2 CONC t;em&gt;A History of Port-Royal/Annapolis Royal : 1605-1800 &lt;/em&gt;(Nimbus Publishing, 2004). Here are some excerpts from our conversation: &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What is the link between Melanson Settlement and the Fort An
2 CONC ne and Port-Royal National Historic Sites?&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;br&gt;The  settlement is an important part of the larger history of European  settlement in the Annapolis Royal area. It was founded by Charles  Melanson and his wife Marie Dugas, follow
2 CONC ing their marriage in about  1664. Marie Dugas was among the first generation of Acadians to be born  at Port-Royal; her father and her maternal grandfather had settled at  Port-Royal with Charles de Menou d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay in the early years of t
2 CONC he  colony. Charles Melanson arrived from England with his parents in 1657  when Acadia was under English rule. His father was a French Huguenot and  his mother was English. Through his marriage into the Dugas family,  Charles gained connections t
2 CONC o many other Acadian families. In addition,  Charles&amp;rsquo; brother, Pierre Melanson, married Marguerite Mius d&amp;rsquo;Entremont  and they eventually moved up to the upper Bay of Fundy and founded  Grand-Pr&amp;eacute;. As their network grew
2 CONC , the Melansons became one of the most  influential families in Acadia. &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;It&amp;rsquo;s important as well that the  fort [present-day Fort Anne National Historic Site] was the seat of  government for the region and so that ha
2 CONC d a definite impact on the  Melansons economically and politically. As one example, in 1720 [a  decade after the British gained control], Alexandre Robichaud, the  second husband of Anne Melanson, who came from an established Acadian  family and li
2 CONC ved at the Melanson settlement, was selected as one of the  first deputies to represent the Acadians in their dealings with the  British government at Annapolis Royal. &lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;     &lt;div style="float: right; width: 314px"&gt;       &l
2 CONC t;strong&gt;          &lt;span&gt;From  the upland of the settlement visitors can view Goat Island (on the  right), and the opening to the Annapolis (formerly Dauphin) River from  the Annapolis (formerly Port-Royal) Basin &lt;/span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt
2 CONC ;span&gt;&amp;copy; Parks Canada/A. Rierden &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;     &lt;/div&gt;            &lt;strong&gt;Why was the Melanson Settlement so well documented very early on? &lt;br&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;Under  the French and later the British, v
2 CONC arious engineers at the fort  [present-day Fort Anne National Historic Site] prepared several maps of  the approach to the fort from Goat Island [formerly l&amp;rsquo;&amp;icirc;le aux Ch&amp;egrave;vres],  providing a valuable record of the evolut
2 CONC ion of the Melanson Settlement  and the other settlements on the lower river. No other area of Acadian  settlement is so well documented. &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;A map drawn in 1708 by  Pierre-Paul Delabat, engineer at the fort, showed the Melanson  Se
2 CONC ttlement on the upland point overlooking the St. Charles&amp;rsquo; marsh [now  the Queen Anne marsh]. The map shows five houses in the Melanson  settlement. &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;The most important details on the map are the names  of the occupant
2 CONC s of the houses. Although only one name is Melanson,  members of the Melanson family lived in four of the five houses. An  Acadian family rarely lived in isolation; its members were related by  marriage to others in the community, often in severa
2 CONC l ways. &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Dotted  areas adjacent to four of the houses indicate gardens where crops such  as turnips, cabbages, herbs and flax would have grown. Small trees on  the slope running down to the marsh belonged to the Melansons&amp;rsq
2 CONC uo; orchard.  In the one census that included orchards, taken in 1698, Charles  Melanson had 76 trees, one of the largest orchards in Port-Royal. He had  the second largest amount of arable land. &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;The 1708 map and  another draw
2 CONC n in 1710 provide a direct correlation between the people of  the settlements along the lower river and the physical setting. We are  also very fortunate that the French census of 1707 links to Delabat&amp;rsquo;s  1708 map. It is not possible to m
2 CONC ake this link for any other settlement  area in pre-Deportation Acadia. &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How did the Acadians evolve? &lt;br&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;In  the 1630s, Isaac de Razilly brought settlers from France to La Have.  After his deat
2 CONC h, Charles d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay [Razilly&amp;rsquo;s lieutenant] moved the  settlers to Port-Royal and brought new colonists from France. Under  d&amp;rsquo;Aulnay, they built the first dykes, beginning Acadia&amp;rsquo;s distinctive  dykeland agricu
2 CONC lture. During the period of English rule, which lasted  from 1654 to 1670, the settlers began to leave the town site and extend  settlement along the Dauphin [now Annapolis] River from Goat Island, the  island at the entrance to the river from th
2 CONC e Port-Royal [now Annapolis]  Basin, to above the present town of Bridgetown.&lt;strong&gt; &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;One  of the earliest references to the term comes from Fran&amp;ccedil;ois Du Pont  Duvivier of Louisbourg. Hoping his Ac
2 CONC adian connections would be  influential in urging the Acadians to join his expedition prior to his  attack on the fort in 1744, he told them, &amp;ldquo;Je suis Acadien.&amp;rdquo; By the late  17th century, these settlers had evolved into a distin
2 CONC ct Acadian  society. They were removed from France and had developed a great  affinity to the land in this area. &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;A grass fire  uncovered the Melanson site in the early eighties, which eventually led  to the archaeo
2 CONC logical excavations in which you were involved as an  historian. What were the overall findings? &lt;br&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;The findings led  to the conclusion that the site was not used as domestic settlement  after the deportation in 1755. We hav
2 CONC e assumed that most, if not all, of  the features identified date to the period of the Acadian occupation of  the area. The historical record also supported this conclusion. It is  the only pre-expulsion Acadian settlement that has extended ruins w
2 CONC ith  detailed historical documentation&lt;strong&gt;. &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;The  archaeological team uncovered quite a range of artifacts such as  ceramics from England, Germany and China, scissors, coins and glass  beads. This discove
2 CONC ry added much to the understanding of the Melanson  Settlement and Acadian life in the 17th and 18th centuries. &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;The  excavations on one feature showed that four buildings had been built in  succession on the same site. The firs
2 CONC t two were of picquet or vertical  post construction and the second two of charpente, heavy timber framing  with clay and marsh hay fill, built on a fieldstone foundation. The  charpente houses both had a substantial fireplace and an exterior bak
2 CONC e  oven on the west wall. The first of these two houses had lead-camed  windows - one caming was stamped with the date 1740. Bake ovens and a  similar cycle of rebuilding were also found in excavations at Belleisle,  further up the river. &lt;br&gt
2 CONC ;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Prior to the Deportation, how did  the battles between the British and the French impact the daily life of  the Melanson settlement? &lt;br&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;It was very stressful for people  living on the lower river becau
2 CONC se of all the expeditions anchoring off  Goat Island. The settlement was the first Acadian settlement that came  into view as vessels sailed up the Dauphin, now Annapolis, River after  passing through the channel on the north side of Goat Island, a
2 CONC t the  entrance to the river from the Port-Royal, now Annapolis Basin. On at  least five occasions while Acadia was under French rule, enemy fleets  anchored off Goat Island, near the Melanson Settlement. On a few  occasions, raiders burned Acadia
2 CONC n buildings and killed their livestock. &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What happened to the Melansons after the Deportation? &lt;br&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;During  the Deportation, most of the residents were put on the Pembroke  destined to go to Nort
2 CONC h Carolina. Charles Belliveau, who had grown up in  the Melanson Settlement, and others managed to overpower the crew and  took the ship to the St. John River. From there, most of them made their  way to Quebec. Subsequently some died from a smallp
2 CONC ox epidemic  including Charles Melanson and his brother Ambroise and Ambroise&amp;rsquo;s wife,  Marguerite Comeau. Both men were elderly. Two years after the  Deportation, a British officer described the scene along the lower river  as he approach
2 CONC ed Annapolis Royal with Acadian villages in ruins, with  large numbers of pear and apple trees bending under the weight of their  unpicked fruit. &lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What do you want visitors to the site to come away with? &lt;br&gt;&
2 CONC lt;/strong&gt;I  want them to come away with a sense of place and an understanding of  the history of the Acadians who settled along the Dauphin River (now  Annapolis) and undertook a form of dykeland agriculture unique in North  America. The settl
2 CONC ement is a distinct example of Acadian settlement  before the Deportation and links to the wider history of French and  British colonization in the 17th and 18th centuries. </line></content></metadataxml>
