2 TITL Facts About Philippe Mius d' Entremont
1 _OID b8d7d785-4e37-42c0-b734-cee1874771b7
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;In 1653 at the age of 44, Philippe Mius d&amp;#39;Entremont, the grandson of an Admiral of France set sail for the New World.&amp;nbsp; Sometimes called Sieur de Landremont, this title of gentlemanly respect ind
2 CONC icates that he hailed from the northeast of France in the Cherbourg Lorraine region, near the Meuse river.&amp;nbsp; Philippe is also thought to be Francois-Virgine d&amp;#39;Entremont who had to flee from the clutches of Cardinal Richelieu, Minist
2 CONC er of State to King Louis XIII.&amp;nbsp; Philippe&amp;#39;s grandfather, the Admiral, had been killed by the order of Catherine de&amp;#39;Medici during the massacre she instigated known as the St. Bartholomew&amp;#39;s Day Massacre.&amp;nbsp; Tha
2 CONC t&amp;#39;s when a wave of Catholic mob violence erupted against the Huguenots.(Calvinist Protestants.)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Once in the New World, Philippe served as a&amp;nbsp;military aide to Governor Charles de la Tour.&amp;nbsp; He was given a ba
2 CONC rony in southwest Nova Scotia, in the area now known as Pubnico.&amp;nbsp; Pubnico is a derivation of the native name for the area, Pobomcoup, meaning &amp;#39;a place where holes have been made through the ice to fish.&amp;#39;&amp;nbsp; He marrie
2 CONC d Madeleine Helie de Thillet, also originally from Cherbourg, and lived well into his 90&amp;#39;s.&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
