2 CONC yal, Acadia; d: 7 July 1737&amp;nbsp; Port Royal, Acadia&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt; font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal; background-color: #99ffcc;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line>
2 CONC <line>&lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt; font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal; background-color: #99ffcc;"&gt;Notes for Charles Melanson:&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt; font-size
2 CONC : medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal; background-color: #99ffcc;"&gt;- "Charles Melanson, age 14, and his parents arrived in Acadia from England in 1657. His father, Pierre LaVerdure, was a Huguenot who had moved to Englan
2 CONC d from France before 1632.&amp;nbsp; His mother, Priscilla, was English, a fact that is well accepted by historians, although generations of die-hard descendants, who will not admit an ounce of English blood, continue to insist that she was Scottis
2 CONC h. The family, which included at least two other children, Pierre and John, sailed from England on the&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;Satisfaction&lt;/em&gt;&amp;nbsp;with Thomas Temple, the newly appointed Governor of Acadia.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; The family i
2 CONC s thought to have settled originally on the Saint James River.&amp;nbsp; Then in 1667, when Acadia was returned to France by the Treaty of Breda, Charles' parents and brother John moved to Boston. Charles and Pierre remained in Acadia.&amp;nbsp; Ch
2 CONC arles had renounced Protestantism in 1664 and married Marie Dugas, daughter of Port Royal armorer, Abraham Dugas and Marguerite Douchet. Following their marriage, they probably settled on the land which we now know as the Melanson Settlement in th
2 CONC e Port Royal area. Although no concession has been found, a British document of 1734 states that this land was granted originally to Charles Melanson and "honest" Marie Dugas.&amp;nbsp; By 1671, the Melansons were majors landholders at Port Royal
2 CONC . The first census, taken that year listed 20 arpents of workable land, not all of it under cultivation, 40 head of cattle and 6 sheep. Charles was described as a "labourer", and the family had 4 daughters at the time. In total, 5 sons and 9 daught
2 CONC ers were born ca. 1664 - ca. 1693. The eldest, Marie, was brought up in Boston by her grandmother, strengthening Charles' ties with that colony.&amp;nbsp; Of the other children, 8 established households in the Melanson settlement, where they remain
2 CONC ed for their adult lives. One daughter settled elsewhere in the Port Royal are, one settled at Beaubassin, and one apparently died." Research Bulletin 250, The Melanson Settlement (1664-1755), Canadian Minister of the Enviroment&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
2 CONC </line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt; font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal; background-color: #99ffcc;"&gt;- 1671 Port Royal: Charles Melanson 29, wife Marie Dugas 23, Marie 6, Marguerite 4, Anne 2; 4
2 CONC 0 cattle, 6 sheep, 20 arpents&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt; font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal; background-color: #99ffcc;"&gt;- 1686 Port Royal: Charles Melanson 44, Marie Dugas 38
2 CONC , Isabelle 13, Charles 11, Madeleine 9, Marie 7, Francoise 4, twins Pierre &amp;amp; Ambroise 10 months; 20 cattle, 12 sheep, 6 hogs, 6 arpents, 1 gun.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt; font-size: medium; font-family: 'ti
2 CONC mes new roman'; line-height: normal; background-color: #99ffcc;"&gt;- 1693 Port Royal: Charles Melancon 46, wife Marie Dugats 42, Charles 18, Madeleine 16, Marie 12, Francoise 10, twins Pierre &amp;amp; Ambroise 8, Claude 5, Jeanne 3; 20 cattle, 2
2 CONC 5 sheep, 12 hogs, 35 arpents, 4 guns.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt; font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal; background-color: #99ffcc;"&gt;- 1698 Port Royal: Charles Melanson 57, wife M
2 CONC arie Dugast 52, Charles 23, Marie 18, twins Ambroise &amp;amp; Pierre 13, Claude 11, Jean 8, Marguerite 5; 15 cattle, 9 sheep, 9 hogs, 35 arpents, 76 fruit trees, 2 guns.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt; font-size: mediu
2 CONC m; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal; background-color: #99ffcc;"&gt;- 1700 Port Royal: Charles Melanson 53, wife Marie Dugast 49, twins Pierrre &amp;amp; Ambroise 15, Claude 12, Jeanne 10; 30 cattle, 22 sheep, 35 arpents, 2 guns.
2 CONC &lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt; font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal; background-color: #99ffcc;"&gt;- 1701 Port Royal: Widow Marie Dugas 50, twins Ambroise &amp;amp; Pierre 15, Claud
2 CONC e 12, Jean 10, Marguerite 8; 9 cattle, 8 sheep, 8 hogs, 10 arpents, 2 guns.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt; font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal; background-color: #99ffcc;"&gt;- 1703 P
2 CONC ort Royal: Widow Charles Melanson, 4 boys, 1 girl&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt; font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal; background-color: #99ffcc;"&gt;- 1707 Port Royal: Widow Melanson
2 CONC , 3 boys 14 or older, 1 girl 12 or older; 18 cattle, 15 sheep, 12 hogs, 6 arpents, 2 guns.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt; font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal; background-color: #99ffc
2 CONC c;"&gt;- 1714 Port Royal (near the fort): Widow Melanson&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt; font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal; background-color: #99ffcc;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line>
2 CONC <line>&lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt; font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal; background-color: #99ffcc;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3- Jean (John) Melleson dit Laverdure&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp
2 CONC ;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nb
2 CONC sp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;
2 CONC nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt; font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal; background-color: #99ffcc;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&a
2 CONC mp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;+Sarah -------&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; m: Abt. 1680&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt; font-size: medium; font-family: 'times ne
2 CONC w roman'; line-height: normal; background-color: #99ffcc;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt; font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal; background-color: #99ffcc;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&
2 CONC lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt; font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal; background-color: #99ffcc;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14pt;"&gt;Notes for Pierre Melans
2 CONC on:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt; font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal; background-color: #99ffcc;"&gt;- The ancestor in Acadia, Pierre Laverdur
2 CONC e, was a French Huguenot who went to England where he married an Englishwoman named Prescila Melanson.&amp;nbsp; Pierre Laverdure came to Acadia with Sir Thomas Temple during the English occupation. Two of his sons adopted their mother's surname, M
2 CONC elanson, and stayed in Acadia while a third, John, kept the surname Laverdure and moved to Boston.&amp;nbsp; After Acadia was returned to France following the treaty of Br&amp;eacute;da, in 1667, the family settled in Boston with the exception of h
2 CONC is two sons, Pierre and Charles Melanson who had married Acadians and converted to Roman Catholicism.&amp;nbsp; Pierre Melanson married Marie Mius d'Entremont and, along with Pierre Th&amp;eacute;riault, helped to found the settlement of Grand-Pr&a
2 CONC mp;eacute;. The 1671 census describes him as a tailor but he refused to give his age and the number of his cattle.&amp;nbsp; Charles Melanson married Marie Dugas and settled at Port-Royal. The site of his establishment is well known and was studie
2 CONC d by archeologists, during the 1980s. Charles Melanson was fairly well educated. In 1695, he wrote to the governor of Massachussets giving him information on French activities in Acadia. He also went regularly to Boston, possibly to visit his daugh
2 CONC ter, Marie, who lived there where she had married a French Huguenot, David Basset.&amp;nbsp; Three of Charles' letters to governor Stoughton are preserved in the Massachusetts archives.&amp;nbsp; Despite the family connections and collaboration wit
2 CONC h the Boston colonists, the British authorities deported the families of his grandsons and their descendants.&amp;nbsp; They were shipped to several New England colonies as well as to prisons in the south of England.&amp;nbsp; After the 1764 peac
2 CONC e treaty, several of the family members found a home in France (Belle-&amp;Icirc;le-en-mer) and in Louisiana.&amp;nbsp; A certain number of Melanson families managed to escape their persecutors and found refuge in Quebec, particularly in the Trois-
2 CONC Rivi&amp;egrave;res region. Several returned to Acadia and settled in northern New Brunswick and in the Baie-Sainte-Marie area of Nova Scotia.&amp;nbsp; Several descendants of the family left their mark in Acadian history, notably the Most Reveren
2 CONC d Arthur Melanson who became the first archbishop of Moncton and who founded the teaching order of the Filles de Marie-de-l'Assomption.&amp;nbsp; [Fidele Theriault of Fredericton, New Brunswick]&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;
2 CONC /p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt; font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal; background-color: #99ffcc;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt; font-size: medi
2 CONC um; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal; background-color: #99ffcc;"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 14pt;"&gt;Notes for Priscilla Mallison:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p st
2 CONC yle="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt; font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal; background-color: #99ffcc;"&gt;-&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;Second husband&lt;/em&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;Capt. William Wright&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp
2 CONC ;was born (unknown), and died (unknown).&amp;nbsp; They were married 18 April 1680 in Boston, Massachusetts.&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt; font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal; backgr
2 CONC ound-color: #99ffcc;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt; font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal; background-color: #99ffcc;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="ma
2 CONC rgin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt; font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal; background-color: #99ffcc;"&gt;Parents of Pierre Melanson&amp;nbsp; -&amp;nbsp; unknown&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt; fon
2 CONC t-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal; background-color: #99ffcc;"&gt;Parents of Priscilla Mallison&amp;nbsp; -&amp;nbsp; unknown&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt; font-size: medium; font-fam
2 CONC ily: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal; background-color: #99ffcc;"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt; font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal; background-color: #99ffcc;"&
2 CONC gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p style="margin: 0in 0in 0.0001pt; font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; line-height: normal; background-color: #99ffcc;"&gt;Back to Wendy&amp;rsquo;s Ancestral Tree&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p s
