2 TITL Pierre Laverdure marriage and trip to Acadia
1 _OID bc281b62-582e-44ac-8b8d-c217b6d240fd
1 DATE spring of 1657 
1 PLAC England to Acadia trip
1 _DSCR the fall of La Rochelle in 1628, Pierre went to England, married Priscilla, had three sons, family joined Thomas Temple travelling to Ft. St. John and Port Royal on the ship Satisfaction, went to Boston leaving sons Pierre and Charles in Acadia.
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;Notes for Philippe/Pierre Laverdure, (pre Melancon): Thanks to Janet Manseau on GenForum.com&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;He is referred to as Philippe in the notes that come with the 1678 census of Acadien.&lt;br&gt;Durin
2 CONC g this time period (as well as before and after this) surnames were not known as we know them today. When an individual became an adult, he took on a &amp;quot;dit&amp;quot; name that suited him. There were referred to as &amp;quot;so &amp;amp; s
2 CONC o the son of &amp;quot;. As a result brothers from the same family often had different names. Leblond if he was blond, LePetit if he was little, Boucher if he was a butcher, etc. In the case of the sons of Pierre, Philippe took one of the names o
2 CONC f both parents and Charles took his mother&amp;#39;s name and another dit name. As a result the descendants of this family are now known as &amp;quot;Melancon&amp;quot; a spelling variation of Mallanson. During this period before surnames were esta
2 CONC blished a wife never changed her name to take on that of her husband&amp;#39;s. She was always referred to by name and as the daughter of &amp;quot;so &amp;amp; so&amp;quot; or the wife of &amp;quot;so &amp;amp; so&amp;quot;.&lt;br&gt;Pierre La Ver
2 CONC dure was born 1608 in La Rochelle, Charente-Maritime, France, and died 1676 in Boston, MA. He married Priscilla Mallanson Abt. 1630 in Yorkshire, England.&lt;br&gt;Notes for Pierre La Verdure:&lt;br&gt;C.T. Breaux, UNE LIGNEE NOBLE EN FRANCE, QUBEC
2 CONC , ACADIA, LOUSIANE, Revised 2 Jun 1986. &amp;quot;As a result of the fall of La Rochelle in 1628, when the death blow was delivered to the Calvinists in France, Pierre went to England where he married. He and Priscilla had three sons at least, al
2 CONC l born in England. In the spring of 1657 the family embarked in England on board the vessel Satisfaction, under the command of Captain Peter Butler, forming part of the company which Thomas Temple was transporting to Acadie, over which he had bee
2 CONC n named Governor after its capture by the English. Having first stopped at Boston, Captain Butler next went to the fort on the St. John River, where a group of his passengers disembarked, and finally to Port Royal. It appears the LAVERDURE (or Mela
2 CONC nson, as they were also known) family stopped at Fort St. John. After the Treaty of Breda, under which in 1667 England ceded Acadie to France, Pierre and Priscilla went to seek refuge under the Protestant government at Boston, leaving behind in Aca
2 CONC die two of his sons, Pierre and Charles, with their young children.&amp;quot;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
