1 _OID c0150ea6-937d-4c90-9251-46743389b0cc
1 DATE 1667-1670
1 PLAC Acadia 
1 _DSCR Acadia was returned to France. Thomas Temple, the proprietor, residing in Boston, had been given a charter by Cromwell, which was ignored in the Treaty, and the actual handing off was delayed at the site until 1670.
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treaty_of_Breda_(1667)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;Treaty of Breda&lt;/strong&gt; was signed at the Dutch city of Breda, 31 July, 1667, by England, the United Provinces (Neth
2 CONC erlands), France, and Denmark. It brought a hasty and inconclusive end to the Second Anglo-Dutch War (1665&amp;ndash;1667), as Louis XIV&amp;#39;s forces began invading the Spanish Netherlands as part of the War of Devolution, but left many territo
2 CONC rial disputes unresolved. It was thus a typical quick &lt;em&gt;uti possidetis&lt;/em&gt; treaty. In the latter stages of the war, the Dutch had prevailed. Lieutenant-Admiral Michiel de Ruyter virtually controlled the seas around the south coast o
2 CONC f England, following his successful Raid on the Medway, and his presence encouraged English commissioners to sue for peace quickly. Negotiations, which had been long protracted, and had actually begun in Breda before the raid, took only ten days t
2 CONC o conclude after resumption of talks.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;In North America, Acadia was returned to France, without specifying what territories were actually involved on the ground. Thomas Temple, the proprietor, residing in Boston, had bee
2 CONC n given a charter by Cromwell, which was ignored in the Treaty, and the actual handing off was delayed at the site until 1670. In addition, the conquest of New Netherland by the English was confirmed on 21 July 1667, producing the Colonies of New Y
2 CONC ork, New Jersey, Pennsylvania, and Delaware&lt;sup&gt;&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
