2 TITL I will not be a NUN!
1 _OID c37da330-ec75-44ac-b5ca-c65d9f22171a
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;div&gt;I will not be a NUN! 								 								 								 								 							&lt;/div&gt; 						&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt; 							&lt;div&gt; 								 								 							&lt;/div&gt; 						&lt;br&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
2 CONC &lt;li&gt; 							&lt;div&gt; 							&lt;div&gt; 									 									  									 									 									 											&lt;div&gt; 												&lt;p&gt; 												Charlemagne&amp;#39;s attitude toward his daughters has been the  subject of much discussio
2 CONC n. He kept them at home with him, and refused  to allow them to contract sacramental marriages &amp;ndash; possibly to prevent  the creation of cadet branches of the family to challenge the main line,  as had been the case with Tassilo of Bavaria &
2 CONC amp;ndash; yet he tolerated their  extramarital relationships, even rewarding their common-law husbands,  and treasured the bastard grandchildren they produced for him. He also,  apparently, refused to believe stories of their wild behaviour. Afte
2 CONC r  his death the surviving daughters were banished from the court by their  brother, the pious Louis, to take up residence in the convents they had  been bequeathed by their father. At least one of them, Bertha, had a  recognised relationship, if n
2 CONC ot a marriage, with Angilbert, a member of  Charlemagne&amp;#39;s court circle. 												&lt;/p&gt; 											&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
