1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p style="line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;The largest group of  &lt;strong&gt;Melanson&lt;/strong&gt;s--22 of them, most of them part of an extended family from  Grand-Pr&amp;eacute; that ended up at Snow Hill, Mar
2 CONC yland--reached Louisiana in September  1766 after a three-month voyage from Baltimore--the first contingent of Acadian  exiles from that British colony.  Four of the families were headed by widows.   Spanish authorities sent them to Cabanoc&amp;eac
2 CONC ute;/St.-Jacques, where they settled near  their cousins already there:  &lt;/p&gt; &lt;p style="line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;Madeleine &lt;strong&gt;LeBlanc&lt;/strong&gt; of Grand-Pr&amp;eacute;  age 54, widow of Jean-Baptiste &lt;strong&gt
2 CONC ;Melanson&lt;/strong&gt;, came with three  children--Jean-Baptiste, age 25, Charles, age 23, and Marie-Rose, age 21.   Marie-Rose married a &lt;strong&gt;LeBlanc&lt;/strong&gt; cousin, remained on the river, and died at  Ascension, upriver from St.
2 CONC -Jacques, in December 1781, in her late 30s.  Her  older brothers also married and remained on the river.  &lt;/p&gt; &lt;p style="line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;Paul &lt;strong&gt;Melanson&lt;/strong&gt;, age 36, Madeleine  &lt;strong&gt;LeBla
2 CONC nc&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;#39;s oldest son, came with wife Marie &lt;strong&gt;Th&amp;eacute;riot&lt;/strong&gt;, age 30, and  four children--Philippe, age 16, Marie-Madeleine, age 10, Jean-Baptiste, age 7,  and Marie-Anne, age 5.  They had no more chi
2 CONC ldren in Louisiana.  Paul died  probably at Ascension by August 1770, when his wife was listed as a widow in the  census there.  Marie remarried to Amand, daughter of fellow Acadian Joseph  &lt;strong&gt;Pr&amp;eacute;jean&lt;/strong&gt;, of Chepou
2 CONC dy and widower of Madeleine &lt;strong&gt;Martin&lt;/strong&gt;, at Ascension  in c1773 and followed him to the Attakapas District.  Her &lt;strong&gt;Melan&amp;ccedil;on&lt;/strong&gt;  daughters married into the &lt;strong&gt;Breaux&lt;/strong&gt
2 CONC ; and &lt;strong&gt;Landry&lt;/strong&gt; families; one of them  settled at Attakapas, but the other remained on the river.  Son Philippe  remained on the river, but younger son Jean-Baptiste settled at Attakapas.  &lt;/p&gt;</line></content></meta
