2 CONC rgin-right: 0in; margin-: 0in; margin-bottom: 0.0001pt; font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; background-color: #99ffcc"&gt;- The ancestor in Acadia, Pierre Laverdure, was a French Huguenot who went to England where he married an Engli
2 CONC shwoman named Prescila Melanson.&amp;nbsp; Pierre Laverdure came to Acadia with Sir Thomas Temple during the English occupation. Two of his sons adopted their mother&amp;#39;s surname, Melanson, and stayed in Acadia while a third, John, kept the su
2 CONC rname Laverdure and moved to Boston.&amp;nbsp; After Acadia was returned to France following the treaty of Br&amp;eacute;da, in 1667, the family settled in Boston with the exception of his two sons, Pierre and Charles Melanson who had married Acadi
2 CONC ans and converted to Roman Catholicism.&amp;nbsp; Pierre Melanson married Marie Mius d&amp;#39;Entremont and, along with Pierre Th&amp;eacute;riault, helped to found the settlement of Grand-Pr&amp;eacute;. The 1671 census describes him as a tailo
2 CONC r but he refused to give his age and the number of his cattle.&amp;nbsp; Charles Melanson married Marie Dugas and settled at Port-Royal. The site of his establishment is well known and was studied by archeologists, during the 1980s. Charles Melanso
2 CONC n was fairly well educated. In 1695, he wrote to the governor of Massachussets giving him information on French activities in Acadia. He also went regularly to Boston, possibly to visit his daughter, Marie, who lived there where she had married a F
2 CONC rench Huguenot, David Basset.&amp;nbsp; Three of Charles&amp;#39; letters to governor Stoughton are preserved in the Massachusetts archives.&amp;nbsp; Despite the family connections and collaboration with the Boston colonists, the British authoriti
2 CONC es deported the families of his grandsons and their descendants.&amp;nbsp; They were shipped to several New England colonies as well as to prisons in the south of England.&amp;nbsp; After the 1764 peace treaty, several of the family members foun
2 CONC d a home in France (Belle-&amp;Icirc;le-en-mer) and in Louisiana.&amp;nbsp; A certain number of Melanson families managed to escape their persecutors and found refuge in Quebec, particularly in the Trois-Rivi&amp;egrave;res region. Several returne
2 CONC d to Acadia and settled in northern New Brunswick and in the Baie-Sainte-Marie area of Nova Scotia.&amp;nbsp; Several descendants of the family left their mark in Acadian history, notably the Most Reverend Arthur Melanson who became the first archb
2 CONC ishop of Moncton and who founded the teaching order of the Filles de Marie-de-l&amp;#39;Assomption.&amp;nbsp; [Fidele Theriault of Fredericton, New Brunswick]&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin-: 0in; mar
2 CONC gin-right: 0in; margin-: 0in; margin-bottom: 0.0001pt; font-size: medium; font-family: 'times new roman'; background-color: #99ffcc"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style="margin-: 0in; margin-right: 0in; margin-: 0in; margin-bottom: 0.0001pt; font-si
