2 CONC ond Sunnichild,          and he died on October 22, 741, at Quierzy on the Oise River.&amp;nbsp; After his          death, the kingdom was divided between his two sons, Carolman and &lt;strong&gt;Pepin          the Short&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt
2 CONC ;       &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Pepin III&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;(or Pepin the          Short), son of &lt;strong&gt;Charles Martel&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;and &lt;strong&gt;Rotrude de Treves,&lt;/strong&gt; was          born in 714 or 715.&amp;nbs
2 CONC p; In 743 he married Bertrada II&amp;nbsp;of Laon (died 783),          and later he married &lt;strong&gt;Gersvind&lt;/strong&gt;.&amp;nbsp; In 751, &lt;strong&gt;Pepin III&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;deposed          Childeric III&amp;nbsp;and becam
2 CONC e the first king of the Merovingian Dynasty.&amp;nbsp; Pope          Stephen II&amp;nbsp;crowned &lt;strong&gt;Pepin III&lt;/strong&gt; in 754, and &lt;strong&gt;Pepin&amp;rsquo;s&lt;/strong&gt; army protected          the Pope from the Lombards.&a
2 CONC mp;nbsp; &lt;strong&gt;Pepin&lt;/strong&gt; donated territory to the Pope,          which became the foundation of the Papal States.&amp;nbsp; Among the children of          &lt;strong&gt;Pepin III&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;and &lt;strong&gt;Gersvind
2 CONC &lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;was &lt;strong&gt;Charlemagne&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;.&amp;nbsp; Pepin          III&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;died on September 18, 768, and was buried in the Basilica of St.          Dennis.&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;p&gt;&lt
2 CONC ;strong&gt;Charlemagne, King of &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;France&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Charlemagne, &lt;/strong&gt;son of&lt;strong&gt;          Pepin III &lt;/strong&gt;and &lt;strong&gt;Gersvind,&lt;/strong&gt;&
2 CONC amp;nbsp;was born April 2, 742, in Ingolheim.&amp;nbsp;          He became King of the Franks in 768, and Emperor of the Romans in 800.&amp;nbsp;          He built a kingdom that included almost all of Western and Central Europe          through mi
2 CONC litary action against the Saxons, Lombards, and Moors, and through          alliances with the Pope.&amp;nbsp; &lt;strong&gt;Charlemagne&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;instituted administrative,          educational, economic, legal, and cultural reform
2 CONC s in his empire.&amp;nbsp; &lt;/p&gt;       &lt;p&gt;He married first &lt;strong&gt;Hildegard&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;(757-782)          in 771 at Aix-La-Chapelle (Aachen), and among their children was &lt;strong&gt;Pepin          I&lt;/strong&gt;
2 CONC , King of Italy &lt;span style="color: red"&gt;(See Chapter 10)&lt;/span&gt;.&amp;nbsp; He married second Fastrada of East          Frank&amp;nbsp;in 783.&amp;nbsp; He married third Liutgard of Alemannic&amp;nbsp;in 794, and fourth          Gersuin
2 CONC da of Saxon.&amp;nbsp; Some say that Charlemagne had five wives &amp;ndash; Himiltrude,          Desiderata, &lt;strong&gt;Hildegarde&lt;/strong&gt;, Fastrada, and Luitgarde.&amp;nbsp; &lt;strong&gt;Louis I&lt;/strong&gt;,          the Pious, was &
2 CONC lt;strong&gt;Charlemagne&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&amp;rsquo;s&lt;/strong&gt; heir. &lt;strong&gt;Charlemagne&lt;/strong&gt; died          on January 28, 814, in Aachen.&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;p&gt;Children of Charlemagne were:        &lt;/p&gt
2 CONC ;       &lt;p&gt;Pepin the Hunchback&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Louis I&lt;/strong&gt;, the Pious&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;p&gt;Charles, King of Neustria&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;p&gt;Pepin, King of Italy&lt;/p&gt;       &lt;p&gt;Lothar&lt;/p&gt
2 CONC ;       &lt;p&gt;five daughters&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/p&gt;       &lt;p&gt;NOTE:&amp;nbsp; In another place, Familysearch.com          says that Pharamond, above, was father of Clodio&amp;nbsp;of Westphalia.&amp;nbsp; Clodio          was fathe
2 CONC r of Merovich, who died in France in 458.&amp;nbsp; Merovich was the father of Cloderic, who died in          Tournai, France, in 481.&amp;nbsp; Cloderic was the father of Clovis the Riparian,          who died in Paris, France, in 511.&amp;nbsp; C
2 CONC lovis the Riparian was father of Childebert, who          died in Cologne, Germany.&amp;nbsp; Childebert was father of Sigebert the Lame , who died in          Cologne in 509.&amp;nbsp; Sigebert the Lame was father of Clodereic the Parricide
2 CONC ,          who also died in Cologne in 509.&amp;nbsp; Cloderic the Parricide was father of          Munderic, who also died in 509.&amp;nbsp; Munderic was the father of Mummolin.&amp;nbsp;          Mummolin was the father of Bodogisel, born befor
2 CONC e 567.&amp;nbsp; Bodogisel was          the father of Arnoul, and Arnoul was the father of Ansegisal.&amp;nbsp; Beginning          with Cloderic, this is the line described &lt;span style="color: red"&gt;in Chapter          24&lt;/span&gt;.&amp;nbs
2 CONC p; So it appears that there is a divergence of opinion about          the lineage from Pharmond to Ansegisal. &lt;/p&gt;       &lt;p style="margin-bottom: 0pt"&gt;References: 97, 260, 344 &lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
