1 _OID d55e818f-522a-4ba6-995d-90b0c8ac3e72
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #000000; font-family: arial,helvetica,sans-serif; font-size: small;"&gt;The original French settlement at present-day &lt;span style="text-decorat
2 CONC ion: underline;"&gt;&lt;span style="color: #000000; text-decoration: underline;"&gt;Port Royal, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;on the north bank of the &lt;span style="color: #000000;"&gt;Annapolis Basin&lt;/span&gt; known as the &lt;span style="color
2 CONC : #000000;"&gt;Habitation at Port-Royal&lt;/span&gt;, was settled in 1605 by &lt;span style="color: #000000;"&gt;Fran&amp;ccedil;ois Grav&amp;eacute; Du Pont&lt;/span&gt;, &lt;span style="color: #000000;"&gt;Samuel de Champlain&lt;/span&gt;, with a
2 CONC nd for &lt;span style="color: #000000;"&gt;Pierre Dugua, Sieur de Mons&lt;/span&gt;. (Annapolis Royal is twinned with the town of &lt;span style="color: #000000;"&gt;Royan&lt;/span&gt; in France, birthplace of &lt;span style="color: #000000;"&gt;Si
2 CONC eur de Mons&lt;/span&gt;.) The Port-Royal site is approximately 10&amp;nbsp;km (6.2&amp;nbsp;mi) west of present-day Annapolis Royal at the mouth of the &lt;span style="color: #000000;"&gt;Annapolis River&lt;/span&gt; on the &lt;span style="color
2 CONC : #000000;"&gt;Annapolis Basin&lt;/span&gt;. The first settlement was abandoned after being destroyed by British-American attackers in 1613.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #000000; font-family: arial,helvetica,sa
2 CONC ns-serif; font-size: small;"&gt;The Habitation at Port-Royal was France's first successful settlement in North America. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: arial,helvetica,sans-serif; font-size: small;"&gt;Pierre Melanson, an Acadian of Frenc
2 CONC h Huguenot and English extraction, had arrived in Port Royal with Sir Thomas Temple in the 1650s when Acadia was under English control. In about 1680, Pierre and his wife Marguerite traveled east with their five youg children to establish the Melan
2 CONC son Settlement at &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="font-family: arial,helvetica,sans-serif; font-size: small;"&gt;Grand-Pr&amp;eacute; (French for &lt;em&gt;great meadow&lt;/em&gt;), located on the shore of the Minas Basin, an area of tidal marshland.&
2 CONC lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style="color: #000000; font-family: arial,helvetica,sans-serif; font-size: small;"&gt;Port-Royal served as the capital of &lt;span style="color: #000000;"&gt;Acadia&lt;/span&gt; until its destruc
2 CONC tion by British military forces in 1613. France relocated the settlement and capital 8&amp;nbsp;km (5.0&amp;nbsp;mi) upstream and to the south bank of the Annapolis River (see &lt;span style="color: #000000;"&gt;Port-Royal (Acadia)&lt;/span&gt;); t
2 CONC he site of the present-day town of &lt;span style="color: #000000;"&gt;Annapolis Royal&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style="color: #000000; font-family: arial,helvetica,sans-serif; font-size: small;"&g
2 CONC t;The relocated settlement kept the same name "Port-Royal" and served as the capital of Acadia for the majority of the 17th century until the British conquest of the colony in 1710, at which time the (relocated) settlement was renamed to Annapoli
2 CONC s Royal.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</line><line>&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
