2 CONC id not remain there.&amp;nbsp; After she came of age, she crossed the  	Atchafalaya Basin and married Jean-Baptiste, son of fellow Acadian  	&lt;span style="background-color: #c0c0c0"&gt;Jean-Athanase &lt;/span&gt; &lt;strong&gt; 	&lt;span style="b
2 CONC ackground-color: #c0c0c0"&gt;Trahan&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;, at Attakapas in July 1802. They settled at La  	Grosse &amp;Icirc;le du Vermilion.&amp;nbsp; Perhaps a victim of the rigors of childbirth,  	Martine died in St. Martin Parish in Octob
2 CONC er 1807; she was only 23 years  	old.&amp;nbsp; &lt;/p&gt;     &lt;p style="line-height: 150%" align="center"&gt;CONCLUSION&lt;/p&gt;     &lt;p style="line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;Jacques &lt;strong&gt;Mius     d&amp;#39;Entremont&lt;/strong&
2 CONC gt;  IV, the captain, and his wife  	&lt;span style="background-color: #c0c0c0"&gt;Marie &lt;/span&gt; &lt;strong&gt; 	&lt;span style="background-color: #c0c0c0"&gt;Herve&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;, came to  	Louisiana in 1785 but had no more chil
2 CONC dren there.&amp;nbsp; Son  	Jacques-Ferdinand probably died in childhood.&amp;nbsp; Daughter Martine  	survived childhood and married but died in her early 20s.&amp;nbsp; This proud, old Acadian family,  	then, except for its blood, did not     sur
