2 TITL Introduction to the Melansons
1 _OID da24900b-5f6d-4af4-a6d7-5313315a9967
1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td width="100%" valign="top" bgcolor="#FFFFFF"&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="3"&gt;Introduction                  to the Melansons&l
2 CONC t;/font&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;               &lt;p align="left"&gt;&lt;font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2"&gt;The                  &lt;strong&gt;Melanson&lt;/strong&gt; family surname is quite unique in the fact tha
2 CONC t                  it is one of the only surnames that&amp;#39;s use can be traced to originating                  with one particular generation. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;               &lt;table border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="8" width="34%
2 CONC " align="left"&gt;                 &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt;                    &lt;td height="159"&gt;                      &lt;div align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="http://www.gregors-gathering.ca/Images/Pics/melanson-crest.gif" alt="Melanson family c
2 CONC rest: designed by Margaret C. Melanson" width="181" height="177"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;                   &lt;/td&gt;                 &lt;/tr&gt;               &lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt;               &lt;p align="left"&gt;&lt;font face="Arial, Helvetic
2 CONC a, sans-serif" size="2"&gt;The                  progenitors of all Melansons, Pierre Laverdure and his wife Priscilla,                  were not known by the Melanson surname but rather by the name,                  or title, of Laverdure. However
2 CONC , two of their sons, Pierre and                  Charles, appear to be the first to have begun using &amp;quot;Mellanson&amp;quot;                  - in well documented, written form.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;               &lt;p align="left"&gt;&lt;f
2 CONC ont face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;font size="3"&gt;Pierre                  Laverdure&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;               &lt;p align="left"&gt;&lt;font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-se
2 CONC rif" size="2"&gt;The                  reason behind the use of &lt;strong&gt;Laverdure&lt;/strong&gt; as an apparent surname                  for Pierre is not known for certain. It is likely that the name                  was not actually his surn
2 CONC ame but rather a title or nickname that                  may have referred to an area of France that he came from, families                  ties in France or property that he may have owned in France. Since                  two of his sons went b
2 CONC y the Mellanson surname, societal logic                  would dictate that this was their father&amp;#39;s surname. However, no                  record has been found to put the Mellanson surname with Pierre                  senior.&lt;/font&gt;&l
2 CONC t;/p&gt;               &lt;p align="left"&gt;&lt;font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2"&gt;Of                  the Laverdure name, Pierre was not alone in using it in Acadia.                  There were several men of French origin who a
2 CONC lso used the Laverdure/La                  Verdure name, although in these cases the name was clearly a title.                  A good example is that of Germain Doucet, once the Commander-in-Chief                  at Port Royal, who was known as &
2 CONC amp;quot;Germain Doucet dit La Verdure&amp;quot;.                  Coincidentally, Germain was the father of Pierre Mellanson&amp;#39;s wife&amp;#39;s                  mother, Marguerite Doucet.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;               &lt;p align="lef
2 CONC t"&gt;&lt;font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2"&gt;In                  the case of the Mellanson brothers, Pierre Mellanson would become                  known as &amp;quot;La Verdure&amp;quot; while Charles Mellanson woul
2 CONC d                  become known as &amp;quot;La Ram&amp;eacute;e&amp;quot;. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;               &lt;p align="left"&gt;&lt;font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2"&gt;The                  French recorded such titles and ni
2 CONC cknames by using the French                  word &amp;quot;dit&amp;quot; before the title, i.e.: &amp;quot;dit La Verdure&amp;quot;,                  or in English, &amp;quot;called La Verdure&amp;quot;. When referring to                  a female
2 CONC , the French would use the word &amp;quot;dite&amp;quot;. Both                  &amp;quot;dit&amp;quot; and &amp;quot;dite&amp;quot; are pronounced &amp;quot;dee&amp;quot;.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;               &lt;p align="left"&gt;&lt;font face="A
2 CONC rial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2"&gt;During                  their time in Boston, Pierre Laverdure, his wife Priscilla and                  their son John were known by the name &amp;quot;Laverdure&amp;quot;. This                  name was als
2 CONC o to be consistently used as a surname for Pierre                  and Priscilla&amp;#39;s granddaughter, Marie Melanson (first child and                  eldest daughter of Charles dit La Ram&amp;eacute;e Mellanson and Marie Dugas
2 CONC )                  who went to Boston to live with her grandmother at a young age.                  Marie became known as Mary Laverdure until later marrying David                  Basset.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;               &lt;p align="left"&gt;
2 CONC &lt;strong&gt;&lt;font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="3"&gt;Priscilla&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;               &lt;p align="left"&gt;&lt;font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2"&gt;In                  regards to Pierr
2 CONC e Laverdure&amp;#39;s wife Priscilla, we simply do &lt;em&gt;not&lt;/em&gt;                  know for certain what her maiden name was. Many have suggested                  that it was &lt;strong&gt;Mallinson&lt;/strong&gt; (or a variation thereof
2 CONC ) but there                  are no records to indicate this in any official sense that would                  serve to accurately enhance any historical or genealogical research.                  In an effort to present the most factual data avail
2 CONC able, most                  professional researchers and genealogists omit any suggestion                  of a maiden name for Priscilla from their work.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;               &lt;p align="left"&gt;&lt;font face="Arial, Helvetica, s
2 CONC ans-serif" size="2"&gt;Many                  spelling variations resembling the Mellanson name did exist in                  England during the 1500&amp;#39;s and 1600&amp;#39;s but it seems unlikely that                  Pierre and Charles, both a
2 CONC pparently well educated and obviously                  literate, would go on to consistently misspell their surname when                  they settled in the New World. This and other details surrounding                  the origin of the name ha
2 CONC s gone on to create many theories and                  possibilities, but it is &lt;em&gt;not&lt;/em&gt; known for certain why or from                  where Pierre and Charles started to use the Mellanson surname.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt
2 CONC ;               &lt;p align="left"&gt;&lt;font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2"&gt;Of                  interesting note is that the Mellanson/Melanson name is not found                  in England prior to the year 1755. It is only afte
2 CONC r the 1755 expulsions                  of the Acadian people from Nova Scotia that the name begins to                  show up on records in England, where some of Pierre and Charles&amp;#39;                  descendants had been deported to.&lt;/f
2 CONC ont&gt;&lt;/p&gt;               &lt;p align="left"&gt;&lt;font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="3"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Family                  Arrival in Acadia&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;               &lt;table border="0" cellspac
2 CONC ing="0" cellpadding="8" width="35%" align="right"&gt;                 &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt;                    &lt;td&gt;                      &lt;div align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="http://www.gregors-gathering.ca/Images/Pics/MelansonSettlement/m
2 CONC elanson-settlement-1.gif" border="1" alt="Lands of the Melanson Settlement" width="200" height="145"&gt;&lt;br&gt;                       &lt;font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" color="#990000"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;font size="1"&gt;
2 CONC A                        portion of dykeland adjacent to the Melanson Settlement&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;                   &lt;/td&gt;                 &lt;/tr&gt;               &lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt
2 CONC ;               &lt;p align="left"&gt;&lt;font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2"&gt;Of                  the family&amp;#39;s arrival in Acadia, it is widely accepted that Pierre                  and Priscilla landed in 1657 after sailin
2 CONC g from England with their                  sons onboard the ship&lt;em&gt; Satisfaction&lt;/em&gt;. It is also generally                  accepted that the family disembarked at St. John&amp;#39;s fort at the                  mouth of the St. Joh
2 CONC n River. The family had sailed to Acadia with                  the newly appointed English Governor of Acadia, Sir Thomas Temple                  and a group of other settlers. Pierre and Priscilla, however,                  were to reside in Acadi
2 CONC a for only 10 years.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;               &lt;p align="left"&gt;&lt;font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2"&gt;A                  Boston court document from 1677 (Priscilla&amp;#39;s petition of May 3,                  167
2 CONC 7) recorded Priscilla&amp;#39;s late husband, &amp;quot;Peter Leverdure&amp;quot;,                  as being a Frenchman and a Protestant and &amp;quot;Priscilla Leverdure&amp;quot;                  as being an Englishwoman. This document helped ea
2 CONC rly researchers,                  such as Father Clarence d&amp;#39;&amp;Eacute;ntremont, establish a connection                  between Pierre Laverdure, his wife Priscilla and sons Pierre and                  Charles. The document also served t
2 CONC o clear up the persistent claim                  that the origins of the Melanson family were Scottish. On the                  same note, about refuting the claims of Scottish birth (this time                  for Pierre and Charles Mellanson)
2 CONC , a second document, written                  by John Adams in 1720, decisively describes Pierre Laverdure&amp;#39;s                  son Pierre as being &amp;quot;an aged English Gentleman...&amp;quot;.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;               &lt;p a
2 CONC lign="left"&gt;&lt;font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2"&gt;The                  petition goes on to state that Priscilla&amp;#39;s husband had left &amp;quot;[St.]                  John&amp;#39;s fort to escape the wrath of his country
2 CONC men Papists&amp;quot;.                  This latter statement clearly suggests that Pierre was a French                  Huguenot who might have left France as the Catholic government&amp;#39;s                  tolerance for the Protestant Huguenot
2 CONC s began to rapidly deteriorate                  during the 1620&amp;#39;s. Either due to the problems unwinding in France                  or for some other reason, Pierre ended up in England were he and                  his Priscilla were marrie
2 CONC d about 1630 (SW).&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;               &lt;p align="left"&gt;&lt;font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2"&gt;Ten                  years before Priscilla&amp;#39;s petition the 1667 &lt;em&gt;Treaty of Breda&lt;/em&gt
2 CONC ;                  between the English and the French had ceded Acadia back to France.                  Pierre and Priscilla, both Protestants, were probably unable to                  fathom the idea of living under a French Catholic government an
2 CONC d                  thus departed for Protestant ruled Boston, Massachusetts, sometime                  between 1667 and 1770 (Sir Thomas Temple had managed to delay                  the actual handing over of Acadia to French until 1670).&lt;/font&
2 CONC gt;&lt;/p&gt;               &lt;p align="left"&gt;&lt;font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;font size="3"&gt;Pierre                  and Charles Mellanson&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt
2 CONC ;               &lt;table border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="5" width="32%" align="right"&gt;                 &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt;                    &lt;td&gt;                      &lt;div align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="http://www.gregors-
2 CONC gathering.ca/Images/Pics/grand-pre-memorial-chapel-2.gif" border="1" alt="Memorial Chapel at Grand Pr&amp;eacute;" width="145" height="194"&gt;&lt;br&gt;                       &lt;font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;font s
2 CONC ize="1" color="#990000"&gt;Memorial                        Chapel at Grand Pr&amp;egrave; built on or near the site of                        the original St. Charles des Mines church&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/font&gt;                      &l
2 CONC t;/div&gt;                   &lt;/td&gt;                 &lt;/tr&gt;               &lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt;               &lt;p align="left"&gt;&lt;font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2"&gt;Two                  of Pierre and Priscill
2 CONC a&amp;#39;s sons, Pierre dit Laverdure Mellanson                  and Charles dit La Ram&amp;eacute;e Mellanson, having already converted to                  Catholicism and married Acadian girls, remained in Acadia to raise                  thei
2 CONC r families. Pierre and Priscilla&amp;#39;s third known son, John Laverdure,                  either accompanied his parents when they departed for Boston or                  joined them there at a later date.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;               &l
2 CONC t;p align="left"&gt;&lt;font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2"&gt;Eldest                  son Pierre dit Laverdure married Marie-Marguerite Muis d&amp;#39;&amp;Eacute;ntremont,                  daughter of Philippe Muis d&amp;#39;&amp;Ea
2 CONC cute;ntremont &amp;amp; Madeleine H&amp;eacute;lie                  while his younger brother Charles dit La Ram&amp;eacute;e married Marie Dugas,                  daughter of Abraham Dugas &amp;amp; Marguerite-Louise Doucet. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt
2 CONC ;               &lt;p align="left"&gt;&lt;font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2"&gt;Pierre                  and his wife Marie-Marguerite would eventually go on to found                  Grand Pr&amp;eacute; at Les Mines (Minas), along w
2 CONC ith Pierre Terriot, where                  they would raise their family. Grand Pr&amp;eacute; became a favorite site                  for many young Acadians to relocate to as it was abundant in prime                  marshland and it was a good d
2 CONC istance away from the central English                  authorities at Annapolis Royal. Very soon, the population would                  be thrice that as the population at Port Royal/Annapolis Royal.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;               &lt;p align
2 CONC ="left"&gt;&lt;font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2"&gt;Pierre                  would later be designated the Captain of the Militia, while Acadia                  was under French control, and was also to be named a seigneuria
2 CONC l                  agent (collecting rents) which placed him in a position of authority                  in the Minas Basin region. Pierre it seems, was also to become                  a spy for the French.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;               &lt;
2 CONC p align="left"&gt;&lt;font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2"&gt;Charles                  dit La Ram&amp;eacute;e and his wife Marie would establish their family near                  the old Port Royal habitation in the Port Royal basi
2 CONC n at what                  is today known as the Melanson Settlement (sometimes referred                  to as the &amp;quot;Melanson Village&amp;quot; in old records and maps).                  The settlement grew quite large over the years wit
2 CONC h a total of                  nine households being located on the land during its peak times.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;               &lt;p align="left"&gt;&lt;font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2"&gt;Charles                  and his wif
2 CONC e Marie seem to have done reasonably well as the census&amp;#39;                  show their cleared land expanding and their livestock increasing.                  They also had a large section of dyked marshland along the Rivi&amp;egrave;r
2 CONC e                  Daupin (the Annapolis River) adjacent to their property. It was                  from this dyke that archaeologists recently retrieved two intact                  aboiteax, one of which is the largest and oldest aboiteau foun
2 CONC d                  to date.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;               &lt;p align="left"&gt;&lt;font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2"&gt;Other                  archaeological digs at the Melanson Settlement discovered the                  fo
2 CONC undations of many of the homes and buildings that once stood                  on the site, including the structure that housed Charles Melanson                  (son of Charles dit La Ram&amp;eacute;e) and his wife Anne Bourg. The dig
2 CONC s                  within these stone foundations have produced numerous artifacts                  that include an earthen cooking pot, a sword hand guard, beads,                  musket shot, and a variety of utensils, all of which are on displa
2 CONC y                  at the Memorial Chapel at Grand Pr&amp;eacute;. &lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;               &lt;p align="left"&gt;&lt;font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2"&gt;Besides                  farming, in his elder years on the oppo
2 CONC sing side of his brother                  Pierre, Charles became a spy for the English.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;               &lt;p align="left"&gt;&lt;font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2"&gt;Pierre                  and Charles&amp;#39
2 CONC ; descendants would remain on the lands of the Melansons                  Settlement and Grand Pr&amp;eacute; until the fateful year of 1755 that saw                  the Melansons and their families dispersed, along with an estimate
2 CONC d                  9,000 of their fellow Acadians, to various parts of England, France                  and the British colonies of New England during the Expulsions.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;               &lt;p align="left"&gt;&lt;font face="Arial
2 CONC , Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2"&gt;Of                  the Melanson Acadians that were deported to or remained in English                  territories after the Expulsions, the original spelling of the                  &amp;quot;Melanson&amp;quot
2 CONC ; surname was, for the most part, retained.                  For those that were deported to or otherwise ended up in French                  territories, the name was widely frenchinized with the &amp;quot;s&amp;quot;                  in &amp;quot
2 CONC ;son&amp;quot; being replaced with a &amp;quot;c&amp;quot; or cedilla                  according to the french pronunciation of the soft &amp;quot;s&amp;quot;                  on the word &amp;quot;son&amp;quot;.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt
2 CONC ;               &lt;p align="left"&gt;&lt;font face="Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" size="2"&gt;&lt;img src="http://www.gregors-gathering.ca/Images/Icons/hand-point.gif" alt="" width="22" height="13"&gt;                  To continue from here to th
2 CONC e first generation of this family to                  settle in Acadia, please click &lt;a href="http://www.gregors-gathering.ca/Acadia/Melanson/melansons-gen1.htm#Gen1"&gt;here&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt;               &lt;/td&gt
2 CONC ;             &lt;td width="25" height="424" bgcolor="#FFFFFF"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/td&gt;           &lt;/tr&gt;           &lt;tr bgcolor="#FFFFFF"&gt;             &lt;td width="799" height="51" valign="bottom" bgcolor="#FFFFFF"&gt;&lt;p align="right"
2 CONC &gt;&lt;a href="http://www.gregors-gathering.ca/Acadia/Melanson/melansons-intro-gen1.htm#Top"&gt;&lt;img src="http://www.gregors-gathering.ca/Images/Buttons/top.gif" border="0" alt="Return to top of page" width="43" height="18"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&
2 CONC gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/tbody&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
