1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;APPENDICES&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;Acadians Who Found Refuge in Louisiana, February 1764-early 1800s&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p a
2 CONC lign="center"&gt;&lt;font size="5"&gt;Mius D&amp;#39;ENTREMONT&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p align="center"&gt;[MY-ooz donh-tray-MONH]&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p align="center"&gt;ACADIA&lt;/p&gt; &lt;blockquote&gt;   &lt;blockquote&gt;     &lt;p style="line-h
2 CONC eight: 150%" align="left"&gt;Philippe &lt;strong&gt; Mius d&amp;#39;Entremont&lt;/strong&gt; of Cherbourg, Normandy, came to Acadia in 1651 as a lieutenant of Charles La     Tour, a childhood friend.&amp;nbsp;   Philippe was 50, a lieutenant-major
2 CONC , married to Madeleine &lt;strong&gt;H&amp;eacute;lie du Tillet&lt;/strong&gt;,     and father of a daughter when he was named La Tour&amp;#39;s adjutant.&amp;nbsp; In 1653, during     his second   tenure as governor of the colony, La Tour awarde
2 CONC d the trusty &lt;strong&gt;Mius     d&amp;#39;Entremont&lt;/strong&gt; the   seigneury of Pobomcoup, now Pubnico, near Cap-Sable, where Philippe and   Madeleine settled for most of their time in Acadia; Philippe thus became the sieur     &lt;strong
2 CONC &gt;d&amp;#39;Entremont&lt;/strong&gt;, baron de Pobomcoup, &lt;em&gt;lieutenant-major et commandant     des troupes&lt;/em&gt;.&amp;nbsp; His barony ran from Cap-N&amp;egrave;gre, northeast of     Cap-Sable, around to Cap-Fourchu near present-da
2 CONC y Yarmouth.&amp;nbsp; He built     his feudal house near the entry to the harbor at Pobomcoup.&amp;nbsp; One     biographer asserts:&amp;nbsp; &amp;quot;&lt;strong&gt;D&amp;#39;Entremont&lt;/strong&gt; played an important part     in the colony&amp
2 CONC ;#39;s history both because of what he did as an administrator and     because he was one of the rare Acadian seigneurs to concern himself with     cultivation and with clearing land; he attracted to his estate &amp;#39;several     indentured worke
2 CONC rs and a few families from Port-Royal ... and this seigneury     eventually formed a small centre of population.&amp;#39;&amp;quot;&amp;nbsp; In   1670, at age 69, upon the restoration of the colony to France, Philippe   became the King&amp;#39;s a
2 CONC ttorney in Acadia.&amp;nbsp; He served in this capacity until   1688, when old age and infirmity (he was 87!) compelled him to relinquish the   post.&amp;nbsp; In his final days he lived for a time at Minas with his older     daughter and died in c
2 CONC 1700 at age 99, &amp;quot;with     all his teeth,&amp;quot;&amp;nbsp;either at Minas or Port-Royal.&amp;nbsp; He and his     wife Madeleine&lt;strong&gt; &lt;/strong&gt;had four more     children in Acadia, including three sons who created familie
2 CONC s of their     own.&amp;nbsp; Their older daughter married into the &lt;strong&gt;Melanson&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;em&gt;dit&lt;/em&gt;     Laverdure family.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;     &lt;p style="line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;Oldest son Ja
2 CONC cques &lt;strong&gt;Mius&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;strong&gt;d&amp;#39;Entremont&lt;/strong&gt;, sieur et baron de     Pobomcoup, co-seigneur of Port-Royal and Acadia, born at Pobomcoup in c1658,     married Anne, daughter of Charles &lt;strong&gt;La Tour
2 CONC &lt;/strong&gt; and Jeanne &lt;strong&gt;Motin&lt;/strong&gt;     de Reux, Charles d&amp;#39;Aulnay&amp;#39;s widow, in c1678.&amp;nbsp; Jacques and Anne had nine     children, including four sons who married into the &lt;strong&gt;Amireau&lt;/stro
2 CONC ng&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;d&amp;#39;Abbadie     de Saint-Castin&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;Landry&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;Boudreaux&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;Molaison&lt;/strong&gt;     families.&amp;nbsp; Their five daughters marrie
2 CONC d into the &lt;strong&gt;Dupont Duvivier&lt;/strong&gt;,     &lt;strong&gt;Dupont Duchambon&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;Landry&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;Boulais de Saillans&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;Pastour     de Costebelle&lt;/strong&gt
2 CONC ;, &lt;strong&gt;Navailles de Labatut&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;Lafitte&lt;/strong&gt;     families.&amp;nbsp; Philippe &lt;strong&gt;Pastour de Costebelle&lt;/strong&gt; was governor of     Newfoundland when he married Jacque&amp;#39;s dau
2 CONC ghter Anne at Port-Dauphin,     Newfoundland, in     February 1716.&amp;nbsp; Anne remarried--her third marriage--to French baron     Chevalier Laurent &lt;strong&gt;de Navailles de Labatut&lt;/strong&gt; at St.-Eustache de Paris in     France i
2 CONC n     August 1719.&amp;nbsp; She lived at her husband&amp;#39;s family estate, the Ch&amp;acirc;teau de     Navailles-Labatut, which still stands near the village of Labutat-Figui&amp;egrave;res     in the B&amp;eacute;arn hills north of the Pyren&
2 CONC amp;eacute;es, in the far southwest corner of     France.&amp;nbsp; She died at the ch&amp;acirc;teau in October 1778, in her 80s, when her     &lt;strong&gt;D&amp;#39;Entremont &lt;/strong&gt;kinsmen were languishing far to the north at the port o
2 CONC f     Cherbourg, to which they had been deported 20 years before.&amp;nbsp; Jacques died in 1735 or 1736 probably at Pobomcoup.&amp;nbsp;     His     descendants used the surname &lt;strong&gt;Mius d&amp;#39;Entremont&lt;/strong&gt; or &lt;strong&g
2 CONC t;D&amp;#39;Entremont&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;     &lt;p style="line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt; Abraham &lt;strong&gt;Mius&lt;/strong&gt;,     sieur de Pleinmarais, born at Pobomcoup in c1658, married Marguerite, another     daughter of Charle
2 CONC s &lt;strong&gt;La Tour&lt;/strong&gt; and sister of his brother&amp;#39;s wife Anne, in     c1676.&amp;nbsp; They had nine children also.&amp;nbsp; Four of their     daughters married into the &lt;strong&gt;Bourgeois&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;
2 CONC Cr&amp;eacute;peau&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;Channitteau&lt;/strong&gt;,     and &lt;strong&gt;Landry &lt;/strong&gt;families.&amp;nbsp; None of Abraham&amp;#39;s three sons seems to have     survived childhood, so this line of the family, exce
2 CONC pt for its blood, did     not continue.&amp;nbsp; Abraham died in September     1704, in his mid-40s.&amp;nbsp; His daughters used the surname &lt;strong&gt;Mius&lt;/strong&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;     &lt;p style="line-height: 150%" alig
2 CONC n="left"&gt; Youngest son Philippe &lt;strong&gt;Mius     d&amp;#39;Azy&lt;/strong&gt;, born at Pobomcoup in c1660, married first an Indian woman whose name has been     lost to history, in c1678.&amp;nbsp; Philippe also lived for a time at La H&am
2 CONC p;egrave;ve,     up the coast from Cap-Sable.&amp;nbsp; He and his first wife had five children, including a son who married into the &lt;strong&gt;Amireau&lt;/strong&gt;     &lt;em&gt;dit&lt;/em&gt; Tourangeau family and settled at Port-Royal, an
2 CONC d two sons who     also married Indian women.&amp;nbsp; One of those sons lived at Mouscoudabouet,     now Musquodoboit Harbor near present-day Halifax&amp;nbsp; Philippe&amp;#39;s two daughters married into the &lt;strong&gt;Viger&lt;/strong&gt; a
2 CONC nd &lt;strong&gt;Bonnevie&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;em&gt;dit&lt;/em&gt;     Beaumont families.&amp;nbsp; Philippe remarried to another Indian woman,     Marie, in c1687.&amp;nbsp; They had nine children, including five sons, four of     whom married.&amp
2 CONC ;nbsp; One son married into the &lt;strong&gt;Lapierre&lt;/strong&gt;     family.&amp;nbsp; The surnames of three of the other married sons&amp;#39; wives have been lost, so they     probably married Indian women.&amp;nbsp; Philippe and Marie&amp;#
2 CONC 39;s four daughters married into the     &lt;strong&gt;Thomas&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;Gu&amp;eacute;dry&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;em&gt;dit&lt;/em&gt; Gravois, &lt;strong&gt;Grand-Claude&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;Cellier&lt;/strong&gt;     &
2 CONC lt;em&gt;dit&lt;/em&gt; &lt;strong&gt;Char&amp;ecirc;t &lt;/strong&gt;families.&amp;nbsp; Philippe, &lt;em&gt;fils&lt;/em&gt;&amp;#39;s descendants used the surname &lt;strong&gt;Mius     d&amp;#39;Azy&lt;/strong&gt;.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Some of the
2 CONC m left peninsula Acadia for &amp;Icirc;le St.-Jean,     today&amp;#39;s Prince Edward Island, by the 1750s.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;     &lt;p style="line-height: 150%" align="center"&gt; &lt;em&gt;LE GRAND D&amp;Eacute;RANGEMENT&lt;/em&gt;&lt
2 CONC ;/p&gt;     &lt;p style="line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;&lt;em&gt;Le Grand     D&amp;eacute;rangement &lt;/em&gt;scattered this family even farther:&lt;/p&gt;     &lt;p style="line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;While the British were gatherin
2 CONC g     up the Acadians in Nova Scotia in the fall of 1755, Marguerite &lt;strong&gt;Mius d&amp;#39;Azy&lt;/strong&gt;     of Port Lajoie, &amp;Icirc;le St.-Jean, and her new husband, Jean &lt;strong&gt;Del&amp;acirc;ge&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;em&gt;dit&l
2 CONC t;/em&gt;     Langlois, left the island for Qu&amp;eacute;bec, where Marguerite died in early     October 1755, age 36.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;     &lt;p style="line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;There were descendants of Philippe     &lt;str
2 CONC ong&gt;Mius d&amp;#39;Azy&lt;/strong&gt; still at Port-Royal in 1755.&amp;nbsp; One of them,     granddaughter Marie-Jos&amp;egrave;phe, married to Jean-Baptiste &lt;strong&gt;Raymond&lt;/strong&gt;, was     deported to North Carolina aboard the &l
2 CONC t;em&gt;Pembroke&lt;/em&gt; in December.&amp;nbsp; Soon after the ship left Goat Island in the lower     Annapolis River, a storm in the lower Bay of Fundy separated the &lt;em&gt;Pembroke&lt;/em&gt;     from the other transports filled with Port-R
2 CONC oyal Acadians.&amp;nbsp; The exiles aboard the ship, led by Charles &lt;strong&gt;Belliveau&lt;/strong&gt;, a pilot,&lt;strong&gt; &lt;/strong&gt;and     including the Jean-Baptiste &lt;strong&gt;Raymond&lt;/strong&gt;, saw their     opportunity.&a
2 CONC mp;nbsp; They overwhelmed the officers and crew of the &lt;em&gt;Pembroke&lt;/em&gt;,     who numbered only eight, seized the vessel, sailed it to Baie Ste.-Marie on     the western shore of Nova Scotia, hid there for nearly a month, and then     s
2 CONC ailed across the Bay of Fundy to the lower Rivi&amp;egrave;re St.-Jean in January     1756.&amp;nbsp; There, in early February, they were discovered by a boatload of     British soldiers and sailors disguised as French troops.&amp;nbsp; The &lt;str
2 CONC ong&gt;Raymond&lt;/strong&gt;     and     the others managed to drive off the British force, burn the ship, and make     their way with the ship&amp;#39;s officers and crew to the upper Rivi&amp;egrave;re St.-Jean     settlement of Ste.-Anne-du-Pay
2 CONC -Bas, today&amp;#39;s Fredericton, New Brunswick,     where they spent the rest of the winter.&amp;nbsp; When food ran short at     Ste.-Anne-du-Pay-Bas in the summer of 1756, Jean-Baptiste took his family to the     St. Lawrence valley.&amp;nbsp
2 CONC ; Marie-Jos&amp;egrave;phe died in a smallpox epidemic at     Qu&amp;eacute;bec in December 1757 two weeks before her husband died.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;     &lt;p style="line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;Most of the &lt;strong&gt;Mius d&a
2 CONC mp;#39;Azy&lt;/strong&gt;s at     Port-Royal fell into British hands.&amp;nbsp; Philippe &lt;strong&gt;Mius d&amp;#39;Azy&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;#39;s grandson     Joseph, married to Marie-Jos&amp;egrave;phe &lt;strong&gt;Pr&amp;eacute;jean&lt;/strong&
2 CONC gt;, ended up at Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, in late 1755.&amp;nbsp; His younger     brothers Charles, married to Marie-Marthe &lt;strong&gt;H&amp;eacute;bert&lt;/strong&gt;, Francois, married     to Jeanne &lt;strong&gt;Duon&lt;/strong&gt;, and Je
2 CONC an-Baptiste, married to Marie-Jos&amp;egrave;phe &lt;strong&gt;Surette&lt;/strong&gt;,     and their families went to Massachusetts.&amp;nbsp; In October 1761, Joseph     remarried to widow Marie &lt;strong&gt;Vincent&lt;/strong&gt; at Philadelphia
2 CONC .&amp;nbsp; In 1763,     after the French and Indian War finally ended, Joseph took his family to     Massachusetts, where his younger brothers and some of his &lt;strong&gt;D&amp;#39;Entremont &lt;/strong&gt;cousins     were still living in exile.
2 CONC &amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;     &lt;p style="line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;In the spring of 1756, two     English sloops, the &lt;em&gt;Mary&lt;/em&gt; and the &lt;em&gt;Vulture&lt;/em&gt;, transported     approximately 170 Acadians from th
2 CONC e Cap-Sable area to New York and     Massachusetts.&amp;nbsp; One of these Cap-Sable deportees was Jacques &lt;strong&gt;Mius     d&amp;#39;Entremont&lt;/strong&gt;,     &lt;em&gt;p&amp;egrave;re&lt;/em&gt;&amp;#39;s son Jacques, &lt;em&gt;fils&lt;
2 CONC /em&gt;, Jacques, &lt;em&gt;fils&lt;/em&gt;&amp;#39;s wife     Marguerite &lt;strong&gt;Amireau&lt;/strong&gt;, and some of their children; they sailed on the &lt;em&gt;Vulture     &lt;/em&gt;to Boston, Massachusetts.&amp;nbsp; Jacques, &lt;em&gt;f
2 CONC ils&lt;/em&gt; died at Walpole,     Massachusetts, in July 1759, age 80, and was buried at nearby Roxbury.&amp;nbsp;     Jacques, &lt;em&gt;fils&lt;/em&gt;&amp;#39;s daughter Anne married Abel, son of fellow Acadian Jean &lt;strong&gt;Duon&lt;/stro
2 CONC ng&gt;,     at Marblehead, Massachusetts, in 1756.&amp;nbsp; Jacques, &lt;em&gt;fils&lt;/em&gt;&amp;#39;s son     Joseph married Agn&amp;egrave;s, daughter of fellow Acadian Charles &lt;strong&gt;Belliveau&lt;/strong&gt;, in     Massachusetts in 17
2 CONC 63.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;     &lt;p style="line-height: 150%" align="center"&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;     &lt;p style="line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;The other &lt;strong&gt;D&amp;#39;Entremont&lt;/strong&gt;s of Pobomcoup,     including childre
2 CONC n of the captured Jacques, &lt;em&gt;fils&lt;/em&gt;, escaped the     1756 deportation.&amp;nbsp; They may have made their way to one of the Maritime     islands north of peninsula Acadia, either to &amp;Icirc;le St.-Jean, today&amp;#39;s Princ
2 CONC e     Edward Island, or to &amp;Icirc;le Royale, today&amp;#39;s Cape Breton Island, which in 1755 were areas still controlled by     France, or, more likely, the family eluded the British forces in the spring     of 1756 and remained at Pobomcoup.
2 CONC &amp;nbsp; No baptismal, marriage, or burial record places any of the     &lt;strong&gt;D&amp;#39;Entremont&lt;/strong&gt;s     of Cap-Sable on any of the Maritime islands.&lt;/p&gt;     &lt;p style="line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;Their respit
2 CONC e from  British     oppression was short-lived.&amp;nbsp; After the fall of the French fortress  at Louisbourg     in July 1758, 400 British soldiers disembarked at Cap-Sable in late     September to     search for Acadians still in the area.&amp;n
2 CONC bsp; Two sailboats manned by  British troops sailed along the shore of the Cap-Sable area &amp;quot;to prevent     the vermin from escaping in canoes,&amp;quot; one British officer commented.&amp;nbsp;  This time     luck ran out for the &lt;strong
2 CONC &gt;D&amp;#39;Entremont&lt;/strong&gt;s of Pobomcoup.&amp;nbsp;     The British burned all of the houses and other buildings in the area to deny     the Acadians shelter and sustenance.&amp;nbsp; In late October, the British     embarked 68 Acadian
2 CONC s they had captured at Cap-Sable, plus their priest, on     the transport &lt;em&gt;Alexander II&lt;/em&gt;.&amp;nbsp; This probably included &lt;strong&gt;D&amp;#39;Entremont&lt;/strong&gt;s.&amp;nbsp;     Several Acadian families escaped the ruth
2 CONC less Rangers sent out to catch them but     surrendered to British authorities the following summer and were held as     prisoners at Georges Island, Halifax.&amp;nbsp; Meanwhile, the &lt;em&gt;Alexander II &lt;/em&gt;sailed     from Cap-Sable to H
2 CONC alifax, which it reached the first week of     November.&amp;nbsp; From Halifax, in December 1758, the British sent the Cap-Sable     Acadians to France with other Acadians from the Maritime islands.&amp;nbsp; The &lt;strong&gt;D&amp;#39;Entremont&
2 CONC lt;/strong&gt;s     went to Cherbourg.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;     &lt;p style="line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;Among the Maritime Acadians sent to France in     late 1758 were Marie-Madeleine, daughter of Joseph &lt;strong&gt;Mius d&amp;#
2 CONC 39;Azy&lt;/strong&gt;     and wife of Jean-Baptiste &lt;strong&gt;Henry&lt;/strong&gt;, her younger sisters Genevi&amp;egrave;ve, wife     of Fran&amp;ccedil;ois &lt;strong&gt;Gu&amp;eacute;rin&lt;/strong&gt;, and Rosalie, wife of &amp;Eacute;loi &
2 CONC lt;strong&gt;Lejeune&lt;/strong&gt;, and     their brother Charles-Benjamin, married to Marie-Jos&amp;egrave;phe &lt;strong&gt;Gu&amp;eacute;dry&lt;/strong&gt;.&amp;nbsp;     They were deported from &amp;Icirc;le St.-Jean aboard the British transpo
2 CONC rt &lt;em&gt;Duke     William&lt;/em&gt;,&lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt; &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;and all of them perished at sea.&amp;nbsp; Their     cousin Marie-Madeleine &lt;strong&gt;D&amp;#39;Entremont&lt;/strong&gt;, widow of Jean &lt;strong&gt;La
2 CONC fitte&lt;/strong&gt; of     &amp;Icirc;le Royale, also was deported to France in 1758.&amp;nbsp; Marie-Madeleine &lt;strong&gt;D&amp;#39;Entremont     &lt;/strong&gt;ended up at Rochefort, near La Rochelle, where she died in August 1760,     age 70
2 CONC .&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;     &lt;p style="line-height: 150%" align="left"&gt;That the Cap-Sable &lt;strong&gt;D&amp;#39;Entremont&lt;/strong&gt;s     were at Cherbourg by     late January 1759 is attested to by the baptism of Abraham, younge
2 CONC r son of     Jacques &lt;strong&gt;Mius d&amp;#39;Entremont&lt;/strong&gt; III and Marguerite &lt;strong&gt;Landry&lt;/strong&gt; of     Pobomcoup, at Tr&amp;eacute;s-St.-Trinit&amp;eacute;, Cherbourg, on 22 January 1759; Abraham&amp;#39;s baptisma
2 CONC l     record states that he was born &amp;quot;&lt;em&gt;aux quatre&lt;/em&gt; Sables&amp;quot; on 8     December 1758, so this gives an idea of when the family was transported from     Acadia to France.&amp;nbsp; The following year, 1760, was espe
2 CONC cially tragic for the     Cap-Sable &lt;strong&gt;D&amp;#39;Entremont&lt;/strong&gt;s at Cherbourg:&amp;nbsp; Marie-Jeanne-Charlotte, age 3 weeks,     daughter of Simon, and Simon, age 5, perhaps another child of Simon, died in February.&amp;nbsp
2 CONC ;     Claire, wife of Charles-Paul &lt;strong&gt;H&amp;eacute;bert&lt;/strong&gt;, age 50, and Joseph, age 72, son of Jacques,     &lt;em&gt;p&amp;egrave;re&lt;/em&gt;, died in March.&amp;nbsp; Charles,     age 33, a bachelor, son of Joseph; Anne
2 CONC , age 30; and Marguerite, age 45, daughter of Charles,     died in May.&amp;nbsp;     Two more bachelor sons of Joseph, Jean, age     27, and Abraham, age 38, died in June.&amp;nbsp; Jacques III also died at Cherbourg     in 1760.&amp;nbsp;     Th
2 CONC e rigors of deportation probably contributed to so many deaths in the     family that year; also, French ports were hotbeds of ship-borne epidemics     such as smallpox and plague.&amp;nbsp; There     were also moments for the family to celebrate
2 CONC , however, such as young Abraham&amp;#39;s baptism in January     1759.&amp;nbsp; But tragedy always lurked in the dark corners of their     lives.&amp;nbsp; C&amp;eacute;cile, daughter of Joseph &lt;strong&gt;D&amp;#39;Entremont&lt;/strong&gt;, di
2 CONC ed at     Cherbourg in c1762, in her late 30s.&amp;nbsp; Simon, son of Joseph &lt;strong&gt;D&amp;#39;Entremont&lt;/strong&gt; and widower of     Marie &lt;strong&gt;Amireau&lt;/strong&gt;, married Anne, daughter of fellow Acadian Gabriel &lt;stron
2 CONC g&gt;Molaison&lt;/strong&gt;     of Pobomcoup and widow of Francois &lt;strong&gt;Viger&lt;/strong&gt;, in September 1763.&amp;nbsp;     Joseph, son of Charles &lt;strong&gt;D&amp;#39;Entremont&lt;/strong&gt;, married Anne, daughter of     fellow A
2 CONC cadian Fran&amp;ccedil;ois &lt;strong&gt;Landry&lt;/strong&gt;, in February 1764, soon after his     parents died at Cherbourg.&amp;nbsp; Joseph&amp;#39;s son     Joseph David was born that December, and Pierre Marin was born posthumously in Augus
2 CONC t     1766; Joseph had died at Cherbourg in March.&amp;nbsp;     Madeleine, daughter of Joseph &lt;strong&gt;D&amp;#39;Entremont&lt;/strong&gt;, married Jean, son of     fellow Acadian Jean &lt;strong&gt;Granger&lt;/strong&gt; of Port-Royal, in Ma
2 CONC y 1764.&amp;nbsp; That     same month, Madeleine, daughter of Charles &lt;strong&gt;D&amp;#39;Entremont&lt;/strong&gt;,     married Basile, son of fellow Acadian Pierre &lt;strong&gt;Boudrot&lt;/strong&gt; of Port-Royal,     but she died in Decembe
2 CONC r 1770, age 40.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;Pierre, son of Charles &lt;strong&gt;D&amp;#39;Entremont&lt;/strong&gt;,     died at Cherbourg in July 1778; he was 47 years old and never     married.&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;     &lt;p style="line-height: 1
2 CONC 50%" align="left"&gt;Not all of the &lt;strong&gt;D&amp;#39;Entremont&lt;/strong&gt;s     remained at Cherbourg.&amp;nbsp; By the early 1780s, Jacques IV, now in his 20s,     had moved down the coast to St.-Malo in northern Brittany, where he marri
