1 _META <metadataxml><content><line>&lt;p&gt;THE THOMAN-BOWMAN FAMILY&lt;br&gt;Prowell&amp;rsquo;s History of York County written in 1907 has this to say about the Thoman family:&amp;nbsp; &amp;ldquo;The Thoman family of York and Adams Counties is one of t
2 CONC he oldest in that section of which a continuous trace has been kept from January 5, 1678, to the present time (1907), covering a period of 228 years.&amp;nbsp; The York County Thomans are the descendants of John Jacob Thoman (Veltin 1517, Bartlin 1
2 CONC 550, Michel 1583, Johannes 1602, Hans Jakob 1631, Heinrich 1685) of Bubendorf, Canton Basel, Switzerland, and are widely disseminated in the United States, some being located in Chicago, prominent among them being Judge Leroy Thoman, one of the fir
2 CONC st Civil Service commissioners of the United States, appointed under President Cleveland&amp;rsquo;s first administration; others located in Philadelphia; and others scattered through Ohio, Indiana, Kansas and other points in the West.&amp;nbsp; Th
2 CONC e greater number, however, are found in Pennsylvania, and doubtless in York and Adams counties, near Gettysburg, Abbottstown, Hanover, and Porter&amp;rsquo;s Sideling.&lt;br&gt;The earliest trace we have of the Thoman name is that of John Jacob Tho
2 CONC man, of Bubendorf, Switzerland.&amp;nbsp; The following is a verbatim translation from a German manuscript, which was preserved for more than two centuries, and which was brought from Switzerland to America by the first emigrants to this country:&a
2 CONC mp;nbsp; &amp;lsquo;Henry Thoman, from here, was honorably born of honorable parents and was baptized in the holy church June 21, 1685, parents were John Jacob Thoman from here and Margaret Bergellman (Geigelmann); sponsors were Bartel Thoman and J
2 CONC acob Stohler.&amp;nbsp; Anna Schozze (Tschopp), Henry&amp;rsquo;s bride, has her baptismal certificate from Holstein.&amp;nbsp; They were legitimately married here August 1, 1719, and had begotten a son, Hans Jacob Thoman, who was baptized Octobe
2 CONC r 21, 1723 (actually born November 16, 1723 and baptized November 21, 1723); sponsors were Joki Stohler, Marti Marti and Anna Bergellman (Geigelmann), all from here.&amp;nbsp; Given with the gracious permission of the venerable Herr Landwacht, an
2 CONC d from the holy baptismal register.&amp;nbsp; Bonifacious Burkhardt, T. Vicarius, Bubendorff, May 15, 1738.&amp;rdquo;&amp;nbsp; &lt;br&gt;The following data were found in Rupp&amp;rsquo;s Collection of 30,000 Foreigners, names of immigrants in Phi
2 CONC ladelphia, Pennsylvania 1880, List of Foreigners Imported, etc., Pennsylvania Archives, 2nd Series, Vol. XVII, page 159, vis.:&amp;nbsp; &amp;ldquo;At Philadelphia, September 11, 1738, 100 Palatines imported from Rotterdam in the ship Robert and Ol
2 CONC iver of Dublin, Walter Goodman, Commander, last from Dover, took the oath of allegiance.&amp;nbsp; Among those who signed their names were Heinrich Thoman and Jacob Thoman.&amp;nbsp; Henry signed his own name, but a clerk signed for Jacob, who wa
2 CONC s then a boy about sixteen years of age.&amp;nbsp; Only male passengers over sixteen years took the oath, so that names of women are not recorded.&amp;nbsp; There were 320 passengers aboard this vessel, so that there were more women than men.&amp;r
2 CONC dquo;&amp;nbsp; &lt;br&gt;The next date we find of the Thoman family after their landing at Philadelphia are from the records kept by Rev. John Walddschmidt, of Cocalico (now Ephrata), Lancaster County, PA and handed down to his descendants.&amp;nb
2 CONC sp; According to his baptismal record book, Rev. Waldschmidt, pastor of the Reformed Church of Cocalico, baptized the following Thoman children at Cocalico:&amp;nbsp; &amp;ldquo;John Jacob Thoman and Susanna (Bauman/Bowman), to them was born a so
2 CONC n the 6th day of March, 1754, and was baptized the 7th of April, 1754.&amp;nbsp; Sponsors were John Jacob Bollinger and his wife Anna Marie.&amp;nbsp; The son was christened John Jacob Thoman (he died in 1775).&amp;nbsp; To the same was born a daug
2 CONC hter June 20, 1755, and was baptized July 3, 1755, christened Anna Christina Thoman.&amp;nbsp; Sponsors were Philip Shaffer and his wife, Anna Christina.&amp;rdquo;&amp;nbsp; Later data record a son born July 25, 1757, named Henry Thoman (married M
2 CONC agdalena Fredrika Hoffman, had eleven children and died August 15, 1835); a daughter, November 20, 1759, named Margaret Thoman (married Oswald Dubs, Jr. (1758-1845), had ten children and died May 24, 1841); a son November 29, 1760, named Rudolph Th
2 CONC oman (married Christine Rudisell).&lt;br&gt;Susanna Bowman was born September 8, 1732 in Lancaster County, PA and married in 1753 Johann Hans Jakob Thoman.&amp;nbsp; Her grandfather, Wendel Bowman, was born in 1681 near Thun, Switzerland and arrive
2 CONC d in America in 1706.&amp;nbsp; He first settled in Germantown in 1707 and later, in 1709, was one of the nine original settlers of Pequea who acquired ten thousand acres of land on the Pequea Creek, four miles south of the city of Lancaster, PA.&a
2 CONC mp;nbsp; &lt;br&gt;Wendel Bowman&amp;rsquo;s son, Heinrich, married Catherine Darr and had twelve children.&amp;nbsp; Five of their children settled in York County near Hanover, PA.&amp;nbsp; They were:&amp;nbsp; Catharina Bowman married George Mye
2 CONC rs; Salome Bowman married Oswald Dubs; Anna Maria Bowman married Jacob Bollinger; Henry Bowman married Elizabeth Hoffman, and Susanna Bowman.&amp;nbsp; Please note that the first wife of Jacob Runkle, the immigrant, is believed to have been the dau
2 CONC ghter of Salome Bowman and Oswald Dubs.&amp;nbsp; This information was received through the internet from the Bailor (Behler) family history.&amp;nbsp; Correct information in parentheses was provided by Charles Chestnutwood of Utah, a Thoman descen
2 CONC dant.&lt;br&gt;About 1761 the Thoman family removed from Cocalico to what is now known as Heidelberg Township, York County (then Manheim Township), between Porter&amp;rsquo;s Sideling and Smith&amp;rsquo;s Station.&amp;nbsp; At this place a large t
2 CONC ract of land was purchased from the heirs of William Penn, consisting of 600 acres, which later was divided into three or more farms, through which, in later years, the Hanover and Hanover Junction Railroad was built (now the Western Maryland Railr
2 CONC oad.)&amp;nbsp; In 1907 the tract was owned by a descendant, Aaron S. Thoman.&lt;br&gt;After locating here the following children were born:&amp;nbsp; Anna Maria Thoman, born January 24, 1762, married Johannes Runkle; Catherine Thoman, born Januar
2 CONC y 6, 1764, married John Jacob Runkle, Jr.; Susannah Thoman, born November 9, 1765, married Conrad Schreiber/Shriver and died October 20, 1835; Abraham Thoman, born December 18, 1767; Magdalena Thoman, born September 11, 1769; Elizabeth Thoman, bor
2 CONC n July 15, 1772; John Thoman, born February 3, 1774.&amp;nbsp; All of these children lived to the age of maturity.&amp;nbsp; Both father and mother of this family died in the year 1804, the former being aged eighty-one years, ten months and thirtee
2 CONC n days; while the latter was seventy-two years, five months and sixteen days old.&amp;rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;</line></content></metadataxml>
